// Generated from the reviewed question bank. Edit the source JSON, not this file.
export default [{"id":"sci-1-001","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks are what kind of animal?","why":"Sharks breathe underwater with gills, like other fish. Whales and dolphins breathe air, so they are mammals.","hint":"Think about how a shark breathes underwater.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["A bird","A mammal","A fish"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-1-002","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the butterfly's life in order, from the very start.","why":"A butterfly changes shape completely. It hatches as a caterpillar and grows before it turns into a butterfly.","hint":"A butterfly begins very small.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Egg","Caterpillar","Chrysalis","Butterfly"]},{"id":"sci-1-003","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What is the Sun?","why":"The Sun is a star, a giant ball of hot glowing gas. Planets and moons only shine by reflecting light.","hint":"It makes its own light.","tags":["space"],"opts":["A star","A moon","A planet"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-1-004","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Dinosaurs like T. rex lived at the same time as people.","why":"Big dinosaurs like T. rex died out about 66 million years ago, long before the first people.","hint":"Think about how old fossils are.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-005","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many hearts does an octopus have?","why":"An octopus has three hearts: two pump blood to the gills and one pumps blood to the body.","hint":"It is more than you have, but fewer than five.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-1-006","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What job do roots do for a plant?","why":"Roots drink water from the soil and hold the plant in place. Flowers make seeds and attract bees.","hint":"Roots grow underground.","tags":[],"opts":["Make the seeds for new plants","Drink water from the soil","Attract bees to the flower"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-007","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a PULL?","why":"A pull moves something toward you. Kicking and hitting push things away.","hint":"A pull brings something closer to you.","tags":["machines","baseball"],"opts":["Opening a drawer","Hitting a baseball","Kicking a ball"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-1-008","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these animals in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Each animal is bigger than the one before. A blue whale is longer than two school buses.","hint":"Think about how much space each one takes up.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["Ant","Cat","Horse","Blue whale"]},{"id":"sci-1-009","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon makes its own light.","why":"The Moon shines because it bounces back light from the Sun. Only stars make their own light.","hint":"What lights up the Moon at night?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-010","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the life of a plant in order, from the start.","why":"A seed sprouts roots and a stem, grows into a small plant, and then makes flowers.","hint":"Everything begins as a seed.","tags":[],"opts":["Seed","Sprout","Small plant","Flower"]},{"id":"sci-2-001","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of a shark's body sweeps side to side to push it forward?","why":"The tail fin swishes back and forth and drives the shark through the water. Gills are for breathing.","hint":"Think about what pushes a swimming fish forward.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Sharp teeth","The gills","Tail fin"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-2-002","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the water cycle in order, starting with the Sun heating the sea.","why":"Heat turns sea water into invisible vapor. High up it cools into cloud drops, and rain returns the water.","hint":"What happens first: warming or cooling?","tags":["weather","ocean"],"opts":["Sun heats the water","Water vapor rises","Vapor cools into clouds","Rain falls"]},{"id":"sci-2-003","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Triceratops was a herbivore. What did it eat?","why":"Herbivores eat only plants. Tyrannosaurus rex was a carnivore and ate meat.","hint":"Herb- sounds like herbs and plants.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Other dinosaurs","Plants","Fish"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-2-004","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sound is made when something vibrates, or wiggles back and forth very fast.","why":"Pluck a rubber band and you can see it wiggle. Those fast wiggles make the sound you hear.","hint":"Touch your throat while you hum.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-005","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many legs does an insect have?","why":"All insects have six legs. Spiders have eight legs, so they are not insects.","hint":"Count the legs on an ant.","tags":["animals"],"a":6},{"id":"sci-2-006","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Why is it dark at night?","why":"Earth spins like a top. When your part turns away from the Sun, it is night.","hint":"Earth keeps turning around.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Our side of Earth turns from the Sun","The Sun gets switched off until morning","The Moon moves in front of the Sun"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-007","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these planets in order, starting closest to the Sun.","why":"A memory trick: My Very Eager Mother. Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars come first.","hint":"Mercury is the closest.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mercury","Venus","Earth","Mars"]},{"id":"sci-2-008","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Friction slows down a baseball that rolls across the grass.","why":"Friction is rubbing between two things. The grass rubs the ball and slows it until it stops.","hint":"Why does a rolling ball stop?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-009","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which body part pumps blood around your body?","why":"The heart is a strong muscle that pumps blood. Lungs bring in air and the stomach breaks down food.","hint":"Put your hand on your chest.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Stomach","Lungs","Heart"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-2-010","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put a frog's life in order, from the start.","why":"Tadpoles breathe with gills, then grow legs and lungs and change into frogs.","hint":"Tadpoles live in water.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Egg","Tadpole","Tadpole with legs","Adult frog"]},{"id":"sci-3-001","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A shark's skin feels like sandpaper. What covers it?","why":"Shark skin is covered with tiny scales shaped like teeth. They help water flow smoothly past the shark.","hint":"Think rough, not soft.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Thick layers of fur","Hard shell plates","Tooth-like scales","Soft, fluffy feathers"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-3-002","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What is a fossil?","why":"Fossils are bones, shells, footprints or other traces of living things preserved in rock for a very long time.","hint":"Fossils show us living things from long ago.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A trace of ancient life in rock","A rock made from cooled lava","A seashell that washed up this year","A plant that grows deep in caves"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-003","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these giant planets in order, starting closest to the Sun.","why":"The four giant planets lie beyond Mars and the asteroid belt. Jupiter is nearest the Sun and Neptune is farthest.","hint":"They all come after Mars.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Jupiter","Saturn","Uranus","Neptune"]},{"id":"sci-3-004","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A seesaw is an example of which simple machine?","why":"A lever is a bar that turns around a point called a fulcrum. A seesaw turns on its middle.","hint":"It tips back and forth on a middle point.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Wedge","Lever","Pulley","Screw"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-3-005","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In a fair test, you change only one thing at a time.","why":"If you change two things, you cannot tell which one caused the result.","hint":"Think about testing two paper airplanes.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-006","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Water boils at about how many degrees Celsius at sea level?","why":"Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius and freezes at 0. That is 212 and 32 degrees Fahrenheit.","hint":"It is a nice round number.","tags":["weather"],"a":100},{"id":"sci-3-007","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Coral reefs are built by tiny animals. What are they called?","why":"Coral polyps build hard skeletons around themselves. Over a very long time, many polyps build a reef.","hint":"They are tiny and stay in one place.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Jellyfish","Sea stars","Sponges","Coral polyps"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-3-008","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have hard bones inside their bodies.","why":"A shark's skeleton is made of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ears and nose, not bone.","hint":"Feel the end of your nose.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-3-009","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Why does the Moon seem to change shape each night?","why":"The Moon is always half lit by the Sun. As it orbits Earth, we see more or less of that lit half.","hint":"The Moon itself never changes shape.","tags":["space"],"opts":["We see changing amounts of its lit half","Earth's shadow covers different parts of it","Clouds in space cover different parts of it","It melts and regrows every single month"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-010","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put this ocean food chain in order, starting with the energy source.","why":"Seaweed uses sunlight to make food, small fish eat the seaweed, and sharks eat the fish.","hint":"Where does all the energy start?","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["Sunlight","Seaweed","Small fish","Shark"]},{"id":"sci-4-001","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A shark loses a tooth while biting. What happens next?","why":"Sharks have rows of spare teeth behind the front ones. They move forward like a conveyor belt.","hint":"Sharks have many rows of teeth.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["A new tooth grows back in one day","A spare tooth moves up to replace it","Nothing happens; sharks keep all teeth","It stops eating until the tooth regrows"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-4-002","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Long lines of fossil dinosaur footprints can show scientists what?","why":"Footprints are trace fossils. Their spacing shows how fast the dinosaur walked, and their size hints at its body.","hint":"Footprints record movement, not color.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["What it ate for its last lunch","What sounds it made when it roared","How it moved and how big it was","What color its skin and eyes were"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-4-003","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put Earth's layers in order, starting at the outside.","why":"We live on the thin crust. Below it are the thick mantle, the liquid outer core and the solid inner core.","hint":"Go from where we live down to the center.","tags":[],"opts":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"]},{"id":"sci-4-004","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A hit baseball flies, slows down, then falls. Which forces act on it in the air?","why":"Gravity pulls the ball down and air pushes against it. The bat's push ends the moment they separate.","hint":"Think about what pushes back as the ball moves.","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["Magnetism and gravity","Only gravity","Gravity and a lasting push from the bat","Gravity and air resistance"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-4-005","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"On the Moon, a hammer and a feather dropped together land at the same time.","why":"The Moon has no air to slow the feather. Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott showed this in 1971.","hint":"What would slow the feather on Earth?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-4-006","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many chambers (rooms) does a human heart have?","why":"Two small chambers on top take in blood. Two strong chambers on the bottom pump it out.","hint":"Two on top, two on the bottom.","tags":["body"],"a":4},{"id":"sci-4-007","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"What mainly causes ocean tides?","why":"The Moon's gravity tugs on the ocean and makes the water bulge. The Sun adds a smaller pull.","hint":"Something far away in space is pulling.","tags":["ocean","space"],"opts":["The pull of the Moon's gravity","Strong winds over the sea","Rivers pushing water out to sea","Whales swimming in groups"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-4-008","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the ocean zones in order, from the surface down.","why":"Light fades with depth. Below about 1,000 meters it is completely dark, and the water is near freezing.","hint":"The sunlit zone is the one near the top.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Sunlit zone","Twilight zone","Midnight zone","Abyss"]},{"id":"sci-4-009","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Sound travels faster through water than through air.","why":"Water's tiny particles are packed closer together, so sound passes along them faster. That helps whales call far away.","hint":"Think about how whales communicate.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-4-010","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"You see wet footprints by a pool and say, \"Someone just swam here.\" What is that?","why":"An observation is what you sense, like wet footprints. An inference is a smart explanation based on it.","hint":"Is it what you saw, or what you figured out?","tags":[],"opts":["A measurement","An observation","An inference","A prediction"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-5-001","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A nurse shark can rest on the sea floor without swimming. How does it still breathe?","why":"Some sharks can use their mouth and throat to pump water over the gills. Others must swim to keep water flowing.","hint":"Gills need a steady flow of water.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["It breathes through its skin","It pumps water over its gills","It uses a blowhole like a whale","It gulps air at the surface"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-002","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which living animals are the closest relatives of Tyrannosaurus rex?","why":"Fossils show many meat-eating dinosaurs had feathers and wishbones. Birds are their living descendants.","hint":"Think feathers and wishbones.","tags":["dinosaurs","animals"],"opts":["Elephants","Sharks","Lizards","Birds"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-5-003","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these planets in order from smallest to largest.","why":"Mercury and Mars are small and rocky. Earth is bigger, and Neptune and Jupiter are giant planets.","hint":"Rocky planets are smaller than giant planets.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mercury","Mars","Earth","Neptune","Jupiter"]},{"id":"sci-5-004","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A bat hits a baseball and the ball speeds up. Where did its extra motion energy come from?","why":"Energy passes from the moving bat to the ball. That is why a faster swing sends the ball farther.","hint":"What was moving before they touched?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["The ball's own weight","The swinging bat","The air around it","The noise of the crowd"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-005","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Earth has seasons because it moves closer to and farther from the Sun.","why":"Seasons come from Earth's tilt. When your part of Earth leans toward the Sun, it gets stronger sunlight and summer.","hint":"Think about Earth's tilt.","tags":["space","weather"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-006","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A pitch travels 18 meters in half a second. How many meters does it travel each second at that speed?","why":"Half a second goes into one second twice, so double the distance: 18 times 2 is 36 meters.","hint":"Double the distance.","tags":["baseball"],"a":36},{"id":"sci-5-007","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why does ocean water taste salty?","why":"Water evaporates from the sea but the salts stay behind, so over millions of years the ocean got salty.","hint":"Salt comes from rocks.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Fish give off salt as they swim","Waves mix in salt from the sea air","Rivers wash rock salts into the sea","The Sun bakes salt into the sea water"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-5-008","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order from smallest to largest.","why":"Cells join to make tissues, tissues make organs, and organs work together in systems of the body.","hint":"Start with the tiniest building block.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Cell","Tissue","Organ","Body system","Whole body"]},{"id":"sci-5-009","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sound can travel through outer space just like it travels through air.","why":"Space is almost empty, so there is nothing to carry the vibrations. In space, no one can hear a rocket roar.","hint":"Sound needs something to wiggle.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-5-010","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"You test whether warm water melts ice faster. Which should stay the SAME in every cup?","why":"The thing you change is the water temperature. The ice size must be the same so the test is fair.","hint":"Keep everything the same except one thing.","tags":[],"opts":["The size of the ice cubes","Which cup you pick","The water temperature","The time it takes to melt"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-6-001","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have jelly-filled pores on their snouts called ampullae of Lorenzini. What do they sense?","why":"Living animals give off a faint electric field. Sharks can feel it, even when the prey is hidden in sand.","hint":"It is a sense humans do not have.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Faint light from deep in the water","How salty the water around it is","Sound waves coming from far away","Faint electric signals from prey"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-6-002","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Similar dinosaur fossils are found on continents now far apart. What helps explain this?","why":"Continents slowly drift on moving plates. Long ago many lands were connected, so animals could walk between them.","hint":"Think about plate tectonics.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Dinosaurs swam across oceans each year","Wind blew bones across the sea","Those lands were once joined together","Fossils form in the same way everywhere"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-6-003","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the Moon's phases in order, starting at new moon.","why":"The lit part grows to a full moon in about two weeks, then shrinks through last quarter back to new.","hint":"The lit part grows first, then shrinks.","tags":["space"],"opts":["New moon","Waxing crescent","First quarter","Full moon","Last quarter"]},{"id":"sci-6-004","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A curveball curves in flight. What makes it curve?","why":"Spin drags air around the ball, so the pressure is unequal on its sides. The higher pressure pushes it sideways.","hint":"Think about the ball's spin.","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["Gravity pulls harder on spinning balls","Spin changes air pressure around it","One side of the ball gets much heavier","Strong wind always pushes it sideways"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-6-005","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Mercury's year (one trip around the Sun) is longer than Earth's year.","why":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it travels a shorter path much faster. Its year is only about 88 days.","hint":"Closer planets orbit faster.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-6-006","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Sound travels about 340 meters each second in air. You hear thunder 3 seconds after you see lightning. About how many meters away was the lightning?","why":"You see lightning almost instantly, so the delay is the sound's trip. 340 times 3 equals 1,020 meters.","hint":"Multiply the speed by the time.","tags":["weather"],"a":1020},{"id":"sci-6-007","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The deeper you dive in the ocean, the more the water presses on you. Why?","why":"The water above you has weight, and the deeper you go, the more of it you carry. Divers feel pressure in their ears.","hint":"Think about a pile of books on you.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Sea creatures push you down","The water gets saltier and sticky","Deep water is always colder","More water is stacked above you"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-6-008","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the life of a star like our Sun in order.","why":"A star forms from a cloud, shines steadily for billions of years, swells into a red giant, then shrinks to a white dwarf.","hint":"Stars are born in clouds.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Cloud of gas and dust","Steady star like our Sun","Red giant","White dwarf"]},{"id":"sci-6-009","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Salt water freezes at a lower temperature than fresh water.","why":"Salt gets in the way of ice crystals forming. The ocean must be colder than 0 degrees Celsius to freeze.","hint":"Why do we put salt on icy roads?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-6-010","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A student grows one bean plant in sun and one in the dark and decides light helps beans grow. What would make the test more trustworthy?","why":"One plant could grow badly by chance. Using many plants in each place makes the pattern easier to trust.","hint":"One try could be luck.","tags":[],"opts":["Test several plants in each place","Water only the sunny plant","Use a bigger pot for the sunny plant","Keep only the best-looking plant"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-1-001","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A whale swims north. Which way is the opposite direction?","why":"North and south are opposites, and so are east and west.","hint":"Picture a compass: what is across from north?","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["West","South","East"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-002","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a NEED, not just a want?","why":"Needs are things people must have to live, like food, water and shelter. Wants are nice extras.","hint":"What do you need to stay alive?","tags":["money"],"opts":["A video game","A toy","Food"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-1-003","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these places in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Maps zoom out like this: a room sits inside a house, a house in a city, and a city in a country.","hint":"Each place fits inside the next one.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Your room","Your house","Your city","Your country"]},{"id":"wor-1-004","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Penguins live at the North Pole.","why":"Penguins live in the Southern Hemisphere, like Antarctica. Polar bears are the ones that live near the North Pole.","hint":"Which pole is in the far south?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-005","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Most maps used in the United States show how many continents?","why":"The seven continents are Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America and South America.","hint":"Count them on a globe.","tags":["maps"],"a":7},{"id":"wor-1-006","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Great Pyramids were built long ago in the desert of which continent?","why":"The Great Pyramids stand in Egypt, in northern Africa. Ancient Egyptians built them as tombs for their kings.","hint":"Egypt is next to the Sahara.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Africa","Antarctica","South America"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-1-007","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Giant tortoises live on the Galápagos Islands. Which ocean are the islands in?","why":"The Galápagos Islands sit in the Pacific Ocean, off the west coast of South America.","hint":"They are west of South America.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Arctic Ocean","Pacific Ocean","Atlantic Ocean"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-008","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the compass directions in order going clockwise, starting at north.","why":"Clockwise is the way clock hands move. A trick: Never Eat Soggy Waffles.","hint":"Think of a clock face.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["North","East","South","West"]},{"id":"wor-1-009","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"In Spanish, the word \"tiburón\" means which animal?","why":"Tiburón is Spanish for shark. Ballena means whale and tortuga means turtle.","hint":"Think of a fin sticking out of the water.","tags":["sharks","animals"],"opts":["Turtle","Whale","Shark"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-1-010","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order for buying a baseball glove.","why":"You need to earn money first, then save it until you have enough, and then spend it.","hint":"You cannot spend money you do not have.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["Earn some money","Save it up","Buy the glove"]},{"id":"wor-2-001","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Emperor penguins raise their chicks on which icy continent?","why":"Antarctica is a cold continent at the bottom of the world, where emperor penguins gather to raise chicks.","hint":"It is at the South Pole.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["North America","Antarctica","Australia"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-2-002","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What does a compass rose on a map show?","why":"The compass rose points out north, south, east and west so you can read the map.","hint":"It looks like a star with letters.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Directions","Mountain heights","Store prices"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-003","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these four oceans in order from largest to smallest.","why":"The Pacific covers about a third of Earth. The Arctic Ocean is the smallest of these four.","hint":"The Pacific is biggest, the Arctic coldest and smallest.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Pacific Ocean","Atlantic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Arctic Ocean"]},{"id":"wor-2-004","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The country Ecuador is named after a line around the middle of Earth. What is it?","why":"Ecuador is the Spanish word for equator. The country sits right on that line.","hint":"Spanish: ecuador. English: ...","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The Date Line","The Equator","The Prime Meridian"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-2-005","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The Hawaiian Islands are in the Pacific Ocean.","why":"Hawaii is a chain of islands in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, far from any continent.","hint":"Think of surfing and volcanoes.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-006","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"number","q":"On a map legend, 1 tree symbol means 10 real trees. A forest shows 3 tree symbols. How many real trees are there?","why":"A map legend explains what symbols mean. Three symbols times 10 trees each is 30 trees.","hint":"Count by tens.","tags":["maps"],"a":30},{"id":"wor-2-007","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which famous tower is in Paris, France?","why":"The Eiffel Tower stands in Paris. The Leaning Tower is in Pisa, Italy, and Big Ben is in London.","hint":"It is made of iron lattice.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Big Ben","The Leaning Tower","The Eiffel Tower"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-2-008","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The Bahamas are famous for being made of many what?","why":"The Bahamas are a chain of hundreds of islands in the Atlantic Ocean, with warm, clear water around them.","hint":"Picture palm trees and beaches with turquoise water.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Islands","Deserts","Volcanoes"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-009","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"All deserts are hot and sandy.","why":"A desert is a place with very little rain. Antarctica is a cold desert covered in ice.","hint":"Deserts are about rain, not heat.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-2-010","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the steps of running a lemonade stand in order.","why":"You pay for supplies first (your cost), then make and sell the product, and finally count what you earned.","hint":"You make it before you sell it.","tags":["money","food"],"opts":["Buy lemons","Make lemonade","Sell cups","Count your money"]},{"id":"wor-3-001","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Galápagos Islands are part of which country?","why":"The Galápagos belong to Ecuador, even though they are about 1,000 kilometers out in the Pacific Ocean.","hint":"They sit in the Pacific, west of South America.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Peru","Chile","Ecuador","Colombia"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-3-002","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What does a map scale help you find out?","why":"A scale like 1 cm = 10 km turns map distances into real distances. A compass rose shows direction.","hint":"It looks like a little ruler.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Which way is north on the map","How tall the mountains are","The names of all the countries","Real distances between places"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-3-003","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these buildings in order from oldest to newest.","why":"The Great Pyramid is about 4,500 years old. The Eiffel Tower was finished in 1889, so it is the newest.","hint":"Ancient builders came before iron towers.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Great Pyramid of Giza","Parthenon in Athens","Colosseum in Rome","Eiffel Tower in Paris"]},{"id":"wor-3-004","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Profit is the money you have left after you...","why":"A lemonade stand earns $12 and spent $5 on supplies, so the profit is $7. Profit is earnings minus costs.","hint":"Earnings minus what you spent.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Spend it all","Pay your costs","Raise your price","Borrow more"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-3-005","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator is a line that runs from the North Pole to the South Pole.","why":"The Equator runs around the middle of Earth, east to west. Lines from pole to pole are called meridians.","hint":"Does it circle the middle or go up and down?","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-3-006","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"number","q":"You buy a used baseball glove for $12 and sell it for $20. How many dollars of profit do you make?","why":"Profit is what you sell for minus what you paid: 20 minus 12 equals 8.","hint":"Subtract the cost from the price.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":8},{"id":"wor-3-007","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Great Barrier Reef, a huge coral reef, is off the coast of which continent?","why":"The Great Barrier Reef lies off the northeast coast of Australia in the Coral Sea.","hint":"It is also called the Land Down Under.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Australia","Africa","South America","Europe"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-3-008","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order from north to south.","why":"This is the Pacific coast of North America. Each city is a little farther south than the one before.","hint":"Canada is the farthest north.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Vancouver, Canada","Seattle","Portland","San Francisco"]},{"id":"wor-3-009","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Hawaiian Islands were built by volcanoes rising from the sea floor.","why":"Lava built up from the ocean floor over millions of years until it poked above the surface as islands.","hint":"Think of Kilauea.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-3-010","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order from west to east.","why":"Honolulu is out in the Pacific Ocean. Heading east you reach the Pacific coast, the Rocky Mountains and the Atlantic coast.","hint":"Start in the Pacific Ocean.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Honolulu","Los Angeles","Denver","New York City"]},{"id":"wor-4-001","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator divides Earth into which two halves?","why":"The Equator splits Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. The Prime Meridian splits east from west.","hint":"The Equator is the halfway line between the poles.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["A land half and a water half","The day side and the night side","Eastern and Western halves","Northern and Southern halves"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-4-002","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Mount Rainier, a snowy volcano near Seattle, belongs to which mountain range?","why":"The Cascades run from Canada through Washington and Oregon. The Rockies are farther east, and the Appalachians are on the other side of the country.","hint":"This range is in the Pacific Northwest.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Appalachian Mountains","Cascade Range","Sierra Nevada","Rocky Mountains"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-4-003","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these climate zones in order, from the Equator toward the North Pole.","why":"It gets cooler as you travel away from the Equator. Tropical is warmest, temperate is in between, and polar is coldest.","hint":"Start where it is warmest.","tags":["maps","weather"],"opts":["Tropical zone","Temperate zone","Polar zone"]},{"id":"wor-4-004","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Only 5 rare baseball cards are for sale, but 50 kids want one. What will probably happen to the price?","why":"When a lot of people want something that is in short supply, sellers can ask for more money.","hint":"Think about how many cards there are compared to kids.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["It goes up","It goes down","It stays the same","The cards become free"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-4-005","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator passes through the Galápagos Islands.","why":"The line crosses Isabela Island, so the Galápagos lie in both the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.","hint":"Think about which country the islands belong to.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-4-006","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Florida is a peninsula. What does that mean?","why":"A peninsula is land that sticks out into the water. An island is surrounded by water on every side.","hint":"Look at Florida on a map.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["It has no coast or beaches","It stands on top of a mountain","Water touches it on three sides","Water surrounds it on every side"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-4-007","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A map scale says 1 cm = 5 km. A tagged shark's path is 4 cm long on the map. How many kilometers did it swim?","why":"Every centimeter on the map stands for 5 real kilometers. Four centimeters is 4 times 5.","hint":"Multiply the map distance by the scale.","tags":["sharks","ocean","maps"],"a":20},{"id":"wor-4-008","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Long ago, sailors steered at night by one star in the northern sky that hardly seems to move. What is it called?","why":"The North Star, Polaris, sits almost straight above the North Pole. Sailors knew it showed them north.","hint":"Its name tells you which way it points.","tags":["space","maps"],"opts":["The Evening Star","The Morning Star","The Dog Star","The North Star"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-4-009","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order for how bread reaches a store.","why":"Goods pass through many workers before you buy them. Each step needs the one before it.","hint":"The wheat has to exist first.","tags":["food","money"],"opts":["Farmers grow wheat","Mill grinds wheat into flour","Bakery bakes the bread","Store sells the bread"]},{"id":"wor-4-010","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A bank pays you a little extra money for keeping your savings there. What is that extra money called?","why":"Interest is a small reward for letting the bank use your savings. The longer you save, the more it can grow.","hint":"It is money that your money earns.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Profit from selling","Interest","A tax","A price tag"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-5-001","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which imaginary lines run east to west and tell you how far north or south a place is?","why":"Latitude lines are like the rungs of a ladder. Longitude lines run from pole to pole.","hint":"Picture the rungs of a ladder.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Lines of longitude","Lines of scale","Lines of latitude","Lines of elevation"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-5-002","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A baseball game in New York starts at 7:00 PM New York time. New York is 3 hours ahead of Los Angeles. What hour is it in Los Angeles then?","why":"Places farther west have earlier clock times, because the Sun reaches the east first. Subtract 3 hours from 7.","hint":"Which way do you go on the clock?","tags":["baseball","maps"],"a":4},{"id":"wor-5-003","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"You have $10 and choose a baseball cap instead of a book. What is the book called in this choice?","why":"Opportunity cost is the next best thing you give up when you choose. It is not the money, but the lost choice.","hint":"It is what you gave up.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["The opportunity cost","The interest you earn","The profit you earn","The budget you set"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-004","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order, from nearest to farthest from the Equator.","why":"Quito is almost on the Equator. Each city after it is farther north.","hint":"One of them sits almost on the Equator.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Quito, Ecuador","Miami","Seattle","Anchorage, Alaska"]},{"id":"wor-5-005","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If a storm destroys many orange crops, orange juice will probably cost more.","why":"Fewer oranges means a smaller supply. When something becomes scarce, sellers can charge a higher price.","hint":"Think about supply and price.","tags":["food","money"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-006","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why are river deltas, where a river reaches the sea, often good for farming?","why":"Rivers carry bits of soil downstream. At the delta the water slows and drops it, making soil full of nutrients.","hint":"Think about what a slow river drops.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The land is high and rocky","Rivers drop rich soil there","It never rains in deltas","Volcanoes heat the ground there"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-5-007","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans all touch a cold ocean that circles Antarctica. What is it called?","why":"The Southern Ocean wraps around Antarctica. The Arctic Ocean is near the North Pole.","hint":"Its name tells you its direction.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Mediterranean Sea","Arctic Ocean","Caribbean Sea","Southern Ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-5-008","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Prime Meridian is the line of 0 degrees longitude.","why":"Longitude is measured east and west from the Prime Meridian. It passes through Greenwich, near London.","hint":"It is the starting line for longitude.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-009","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why are the polar regions cold all year?","why":"Near the poles the Sun's rays hit at a low angle and cover a larger area, so each spot gets less heat.","hint":"Think about a flashlight shining at a slant.","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["They are the closest places to the Sun","Cold winds from space cool them","Sunlight arrives at a low slant","The Sun never shines on them at all"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-5-010","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Ancient Egyptian builders used ramps and levers to help move heavy stones. A ramp is which simple machine?","why":"An inclined plane is a slanted surface. It lets you raise a load with less push over a longer distance.","hint":"It is a slanted surface.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Inclined plane","Pulley","Wheel and axle","Screw"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-001","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Quito, Ecuador, sits almost on the Equator but has cool, spring-like weather. Why?","why":"Air gets colder as elevation rises. Quito is about 2,850 meters up, so the Equator's heat is cooled by the altitude.","hint":"Think about how it feels at the top of a tall mountain.","tags":["maps","weather"],"opts":["Cold ocean water surrounds it","It sits farther from the Sun","It sits high in the Andes","Snow falls on it all year long"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-6-002","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Each time zone is about 15 degrees of longitude wide. Earth is 360 degrees around. About how many time zones fit around the whole Earth?","why":"360 divided by 15 is 24. That matches the 24 hours in a day, because Earth turns once a day.","hint":"Divide 360 by 15.","tags":["maps","space"],"a":24},{"id":"wor-6-003","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Seattle gets lots of rain, but the land just east of the Cascade Mountains is dry. Why?","why":"Moist Pacific air rises up the mountains, cools and drops rain. The air that descends the other side is dry.","hint":"What happens to air as it rises over a mountain?","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["Mountains squeeze rain from the air","The ocean is much colder east of them","Rain only ever falls near the coast","Dry winds blow in from the Atlantic"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-004","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these ways of finding direction at sea in order, from oldest to newest.","why":"Sailors used stars first. Then they added the compass, and the sextant measured star angles. GPS satellites came last.","hint":"Satellites are the newest invention here.","tags":["maps","ocean"],"opts":["Watching the stars","Magnetic compass","Sextant","Satellite GPS"]},{"id":"wor-6-005","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A place at 30 degrees South latitude and 120 degrees West longitude is in both the Southern and Western Hemispheres.","why":"South latitude means south of the Equator. West longitude means west of the Prime Meridian.","hint":"Read the directions in each number.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-006","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why did people invent money instead of only trading goods (barter)?","why":"If you have fish and want wheat, the wheat seller must also want fish. Money works for everyone.","hint":"Think about finding someone who wants your fish.","tags":["money","ocean"],"opts":["Goods were always too heavy to carry","Rulers made direct trading illegal","Only rich people were allowed to trade","Both must want what the other has"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-6-007","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why do islands far from any continent, like the Galápagos, often have animals found nowhere else?","why":"Few animals reach distant islands. Those that do live apart from their relatives and slowly change.","hint":"Think about being cut off from the mainland.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Volcanoes there create new animals","Isolated animals slowly change","Ocean water changes their bodies","Islands have no predators or diseases"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-6-008","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these US time zones in order, from where the Sun rises first to where it rises last.","why":"Earth turns from west to east, so the Sun reaches the east first. Noon comes to Eastern first and Pacific last.","hint":"The Sun rises in the east.","tags":["maps","space"],"opts":["Eastern","Central","Mountain","Pacific"]},{"id":"wor-6-009","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Antarctic scientists study air bubbles trapped in very old ice. What can the bubbles tell them?","why":"Falling snow squeezes air into ice layers. Deeper layers hold older air, like pages in a very old book.","hint":"Older ice holds older air.","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["What the air was like long ago","Which animals once lived on the ice","How deep the ocean is below it","Which ships have sailed nearby"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-010","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A bakery raises the price of its cookies and fewer people buy them. Which idea does this show?","why":"When something costs more, fewer people want to buy it at that price. That is how demand usually works.","hint":"Think about how shoppers react to a higher price.","tags":["money","food"],"opts":["Profit is always the same as price","Supply always exactly matches demand","Demand often falls when prices rise","Bakeries never pay for ingredients"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-1-001","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The blue whale is huge. Which word means almost the same as huge?","why":"Huge means very big. Small is the opposite, and fast is about speed, not size.","hint":"Picture the biggest animal in the sea.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["very big","very small","very fast"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-1-002","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sam put on his coat, hat, and gloves. Then he ran outside to play in the snow. What was the weather like?","why":"Coats, gloves, and snow are clues for cold weather. A hot day would not need them.","hint":"Think about why someone wears gloves.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["hot","cold","warm and dry"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-003","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the story in order.","why":"She must pick up the bat first. Then she swings, the ball flies, and the crowd cheers.","hint":"What must happen before she can swing?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Mia picked up her bat.","She swung at the ball.","The ball flew over the fence.","Everyone cheered."]},{"id":"rea-1-004","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Quiet' and 'loud' mean almost the same thing.","why":"Quiet means making little sound, and loud means making a lot. They are opposites.","hint":"Picture a library and a loud drum.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-005","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"number","q":"An octopus has three hearts. Two hearts help its gills. One heart helps the rest of its body. How many hearts does an octopus have?","why":"The first sentence says it directly. Two hearts work with the gills and one works for the body.","hint":"Reread the first sentence.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-1-006","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Ben dropped his ice cream on the ground. Tears filled his eyes. How did Ben feel?","why":"Tears and a dropped treat are clues. Ben is sad, not proud.","hint":"What do tears usually show?","tags":[],"opts":["proud","sleepy","sad"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-1-007","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is a fact, something that can be proven?","why":"You can check stitches by looking. Best and yummy are feelings, so they are opinions.","hint":"Can you check it by looking?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["A baseball has red stitches.","Baseball is the best game ever.","Hot dogs at games taste yummy."],"a":0},{"id":"rea-1-008","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from quietest to loudest.","why":"A whisper is very soft, talking is in the middle, and shouting is very loud.","hint":"Which one would you use in a library?","tags":[],"opts":["whisper","talk","shout"]},{"id":"rea-1-009","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A shark has rows of teeth. When one falls out, a new one grows in. What is this mostly about?","why":"Every sentence talks about teeth, so teeth is the main idea.","hint":"What do all the sentences talk about?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["baby sharks","shark teeth","shark food"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-010","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'unfair' means 'not fair.'","why":"The prefix un- means not. So unfair means not fair.","hint":"What does un- at the start of a word do?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-001","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The shark glided through the water without a splash. What does glided mean?","why":"The clue is 'without a splash.' Gliding is smooth and easy, not bumpy or noisy.","hint":"Use the clue 'without a splash.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["splashed around loudly","turned and bumped hard","moved smoothly"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-2-002","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the rocket story in order.","why":"A rocket waits ready, counts down, launches, and then travels around Earth.","hint":"What happens right before blast-off?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The rocket sat on the launch pad.","The countdown began.","The rocket blasted off.","It circled high above Earth."]},{"id":"rea-2-003","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Before the big game, Ava's hands shook. She kept checking her glove. How did she feel?","why":"Shaky hands and checking her glove are clues she was worried, not bored or relaxed.","hint":"What do shaky hands often show?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["nervous","bored","relaxed"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-004","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A crab that scurried across the sand moved slowly.","why":"Scurry means to run fast with quick little steps. A slow mover would plod or creep.","hint":"Picture a crab running away from a wave.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-2-005","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many sentences are in this passage? The whale dove deep. It stayed under for a long time. Then it rose to breathe.","why":"Each sentence ends with a period: dove deep, long time, and breathe.","hint":"Count the periods.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-2-006","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Some dinosaurs ate plants. Others ate meat. What is the main idea?","why":"Both sentences tell what dinosaurs ate. Only one answer covers plants and meat.","hint":"Which answer fits both sentences?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["All dinosaurs ate only plants.","Dinosaurs ate different foods.","Meat tasted better than plants."],"a":1},{"id":"rea-2-007","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from slowest to fastest.","why":"Crept is very slow and sprinted is full speed. Walked and jogged fall in between.","hint":"Which one is full speed?","tags":[],"opts":["crept","walked","jogged","sprinted"]},{"id":"rea-2-008","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A shark's skin is covered with tiny scales shaped like teeth. What are the tiny scales shaped like?","why":"They are called denticles, which means 'little teeth.' They make shark skin feel rough.","hint":"Read the first sentence again.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["stars","leaves","teeth"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-2-009","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"'The Moon is the prettiest thing in the sky' is an opinion.","why":"Prettiest is a feeling, and people can disagree. A fact can be checked.","hint":"Can everyone prove it, or can people disagree?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-010","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"At the aquarium, Kai saw hammerhead sharks, nurse sharks, and a whale shark. How many different kinds of shark did he see?","why":"Hammerhead, nurse, and whale shark are three kinds. Count the kinds, not each animal.","hint":"Count the kinds, not the sharks.","tags":["sharks"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-001","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The octopus camouflaged itself against the rocks. Hungry sharks swam right past it. What does camouflaged mean?","why":"Camouflaged means hidden by looking like the background. That is why the sharks swam right past.","hint":"Why did the sharks not notice the octopus?","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["blended in with the rocks","swam off at top speed","squirted ink all over","grew larger to look scary"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-3-002","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The huge dinosaur ____ through the thick mud. Which word shows it moved slowly and heavily?","why":"Trudged means walking with slow, heavy steps. Skipped, pranced, and zipped are light or quick.","hint":"Think about walking in sticky mud.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["skipped","trudged","pranced","zipped"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-3-003","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put the fossil story in order.","why":"Fossils form slowly: burial first, then rock forming over time, and only later can people dig them up.","hint":"Which step needs the most time?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A dinosaur died near a muddy river.","Mud covered its bones.","Over millions of years, the bones turned to rock.","A scientist dug up the fossil."]},{"id":"rea-3-004","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The coach let the pitcher rest because his arm was sore. What was the cause of the rest?","why":"The word because points to the cause. His sore arm made the coach give him a rest.","hint":"Find the word because.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The other team scored a run","The ball got lost in the grass","His arm was sore","The umpire called a time-out"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-3-005","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Read: Sharks breathe underwater with gills. Dolphins come up to breathe air. True or false: Dolphins breathe with gills.","why":"Dolphins are mammals and breathe air at the surface. Sharks are the ones with gills.","hint":"Reread what the text says dolphins do.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-3-006","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Read: (1) The Sun is a star. (2) The Sun is the prettiest star. (3) The Sun gives Earth light and heat. How many sentences are facts?","why":"Sentences 1 and 3 can be checked. 'Prettiest' is an opinion because people can disagree.","hint":"Which sentence can people disagree about?","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-3-007","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The suffix -less means 'without.' What does fearless mean?","why":"-less means without. Do not mix it up with -ful, which means full of.","hint":"Swap -less for the word without.","tags":[],"opts":["full of fear","fear again","afraid of fear","without fear"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-008","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a real English word?","why":"Kerfuffle means a fuss or commotion, and it is in dictionaries. The other three are made up.","hint":"One of these sounds silly but is in dictionaries.","tags":[],"opts":["kerfuffle","blorptic","zimbrel","quantiffle"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-3-009","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"In Triceratops, tri- means 'three' and ceratops means 'horned face.' How many horns did a Triceratops have?","why":"Two long brow horns plus one nose horn make three, matching the tri- in its name.","hint":"Use the meaning of tri-.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-010","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A predator hunts other animals. Prey are the animals that get hunted. True or false: A seal can be prey for a shark.","why":"Some sharks hunt seals, so a seal is prey in that case. Predator and prey are jobs in a food chain.","hint":"Ask yourself who is hunting whom.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-001","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"After her perfect science talk, the teacher said Mia hit it out of the park. What does that mean?","why":"A home run that leaves the park is a huge success, so the idiom means doing something extremely well.","hint":"A home run is the best hit in baseball.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["She threw a ball very far","She did an excellent job","She left school early","She broke a window"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-4-002","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Hundreds of small fish suddenly turned together and darted away. A large, dark shape glided past. What most likely made the fish flee?","why":"A large shape gliding past is the clue. Small fish flee together when a predator comes near.","hint":"What does a large dark shape suggest?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["a sudden thunderstorm rolling in","a sudden drop in water temperature","a bigger animal hunting nearby","a swarm of tiny glowing plankton"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-4-003","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Mara said, 'I'm so hungry I could eat a horse!' How many horses will Mara really eat?","why":"This is hyperbole, a big exaggeration. She just means she is very hungry.","hint":"Is she telling the exact truth?","tags":["food"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-4-004","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In the words 'dehydrate' and 'hydrant,' the root hydr- means water.","why":"Hydr- comes from the Greek word for water. Dehydrate means to lose water, and a hydrant supplies it.","hint":"Think about what a fire hydrant holds.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-005","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"On Earth, Zoe's spacesuit felt heavy. On the Moon, the same suit felt light and she bounced when she walked. What best explains this?","why":"The Moon pulls less strongly than Earth, so things weigh less there. Air and warmth do not change weight.","hint":"What force pulls things toward a planet?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The Moon has much thicker air","Her suit shrank during the trip","The Moon is much warmer than Earth","The Moon's gravity is weaker"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-4-006","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the sea turtle's life story in order.","why":"Eggs come first, then hatching, then the trip to the water, and growing up at sea.","hint":"What must happen before babies can crawl?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["A mother turtle lays eggs on the beach.","The baby turtles hatch.","They crawl to the sea.","They grow up in the ocean."]},{"id":"rea-4-007","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball glove has a deep pocket to catch the ball and long fingers to reach it. A leather web helps scoop it up. What is the main idea?","why":"Every sentence describes a glove part that helps catch. The other choices are never discussed.","hint":"What do all three sentences have in common?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["how gloves catch baseballs","how pitchers throw curveballs","why leather is expensive","when baseball first began"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-008","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The word astronaut comes from Greek words meaning 'star' and 'sailor.' What does astronaut literally mean?","why":"Astro means star and naut means sailor, so an astronaut is a 'star sailor.'","hint":"Combine the two Greek meanings.","tags":["space"],"opts":["moon walker","star sailor","sky builder","rocket driver"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-4-009","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"The prefix bi- means two and tri- means three. How many wheels do a bicycle and a tricycle have in all?","why":"A bicycle has two wheels (bi-) and a tricycle has three (tri-). Two plus three is five.","hint":"Find each prefix, then add.","tags":["machines"],"a":5},{"id":"rea-4-010","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"'It's raining cats and dogs' means that pets are falling from the sky.","why":"It is an idiom. It means rain is falling very hard, not that animals are falling.","hint":"Idioms do not mean exactly what the words say.","tags":["weather","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-5-001","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Tele- means 'far.' Phone means 'sound,' and vis- means 'see.' What does the word television literally mean?","why":"Tele- (far) plus vis- (see) means seeing from far away. Sound from far away would be a telephone.","hint":"Match each root to its meaning.","tags":["space"],"opts":["sound from far away","light from a faraway star","sight from far away","a long, flat screen"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-5-002","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'The Moon was a silver coin in the dark sky' is a simile.","why":"A simile uses like or as. This sentence says the Moon was a coin, with no like or as, so it is a metaphor.","hint":"Look for the words like or as.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-5-003","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from the smallest amount of water to the largest.","why":"A drop is one bead. A trickle is a thin flow, a stream is steady, and a torrent is a rushing flood.","hint":"Which word sounds like a flood?","tags":["ocean","weather"],"opts":["drop","trickle","stream","torrent"]},{"id":"rea-5-004","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists found seashell fossils on a high mountain. Long ago, that rock lay on a sea floor. Slow movements of Earth's crust pushed it upward. What lifted the shells?","why":"The passage names the cause: the crust slowly pushed the rock upward. Nothing in the text mentions waves or people.","hint":"Find the sentence that says what pushed the rock.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Giant waves tossing them up there","Ancient people carried them up","Seabirds dropping them from above","Slow movements of the crust"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-5-005","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is an opinion instead of a fact?","why":"Best is a feeling that people can disagree about. The other three can be checked in a rule book.","hint":"Which one has a judgment word like best?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The seventh-inning stretch is the best part.","A regulation baseball weighs about five ounces.","A pitcher throws from a raised mound.","A regular game has nine innings."],"a":0},{"id":"rea-5-006","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"number","q":"The root bio- means 'life.' How many of these words contain it: biology, bicycle, biome, antibiotic, biography?","why":"Biology, biome, antibiotic, and biography all have bio-. Bicycle only has bi-, which means two.","hint":"Look for the exact letters b-i-o.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"rea-5-007","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The name dinosaur was built from two Greek word parts. What does the name mean?","why":"Deinos means terrible and sauros means lizard. Thunder lizard is what brontosaurus means, a different name.","hint":"Think about what the Greek word parts might describe.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["thunder lizard from the sky","terrible lizard","giant swimming reptile","fierce prehistoric bird"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-5-008","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The shortstop was on thin ice with his coach after missing three practices. What does on thin ice mean?","why":"On thin ice means close to getting in trouble. Missing practice puts him in a risky spot.","hint":"Why might a coach be unhappy?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["playing very well","standing in cold air","in a risky spot","ready to be promoted"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-5-009","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these sentences in the order a hurricane forms.","why":"Heat from the ocean comes first. Rising air starts the spin, the storm strengthens, and only later does it arrive.","hint":"What gives a hurricane its energy at the start?","tags":["weather","ocean"],"opts":["Warm ocean water heats the air above it.","The warm air rises, and winds begin to spin.","The spinning storm grows stronger.","The hurricane reaches land."]},{"id":"rea-5-010","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Jada trained all spring for the big game. When the coach read the lineup, her name was missing. Her shoulders sagged and she stared at the floor. How does Jada feel?","why":"Sagging shoulders and staring down are clues of sadness over a letdown, not joy, relief, or loud anger.","hint":"Which clue shows her body language?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["thrilled and proud","angry enough to yell","relieved and calm","disappointed"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-6-001","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word planet comes from a Greek word meaning what?","why":"Ancient watchers saw planets slowly move against the fixed stars, so the Greeks called them wanderers.","hint":"Think about how planets move across the sky.","tags":["space"],"opts":["wanderer","ball of fire","sky shepherd","night lantern"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-6-002","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A coach praises a pitcher who keeps working hard after losing. Which word gives the most positive feeling? 'He is a ____ player.'","why":"All four words describe someone who will not give up, but only determined sounds like praise. The others sound like complaints.","hint":"Which word would you like to hear about yourself?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["stubborn","determined","pigheaded","bullheaded"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-6-003","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words begin with a prefix meaning 'not': unlucky, impossible, dishonest, review, incorrect?","why":"Un-, im-, dis-, and in- can all mean not. Re- in review means again, so it does not count.","hint":"Check what each beginning part means.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"rea-6-004","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word quarantine comes from an old Italian word for 'forty,' because ships once waited about forty days before landing.","why":"Quaranta giorni means forty days in Italian. Port cities made ships wait before sailors could come ashore.","hint":"Think of an old number word.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-6-005","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Researchers found three fossil bones. The lead scientist said, 'This is only the tip of the iceberg.' What does she mean?","why":"Most of an iceberg is underwater. The idiom means you see only a small part of something much bigger.","hint":"Most of an iceberg is below the water.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["The bones came from frozen ground","The dig is almost finished for the year","There is much more still hidden","The find is not very important"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-6-006","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Read: (1) Saturn has rings. (2) Saturn's rings are gorgeous. (3) Saturn is a gas giant. (4) Everyone should see Saturn. How many sentences are facts?","why":"Sentences 1 and 3 can be checked by scientists. Gorgeous and should are judgments, so those are opinions.","hint":"Ask which sentences could be tested or measured.","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-6-007","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Paleo- means 'ancient' and -ology means 'study of.' A paleontologist is a scientist who studies what?","why":"Paleontologists study fossils of ancient plants and animals. Ancient buildings are studied by archaeologists.","hint":"Build the meaning from the roots.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["old books","ancient buildings","the oldest stars","ancient life"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-6-008","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from least sure to most sure.","why":"Maybe means it might happen. Probably means it likely will, and definitely means there is no doubt.","hint":"Which word leaves no doubt?","tags":[],"opts":["maybe","probably","definitely"]},{"id":"rea-6-009","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists found fossil footprints: many small tracks beside a few large ones, all heading the same way. Which inference is best supported by this clue?","why":"Tracks of different sizes going the same direction suggest a group. The clue does not show hunting or a flood.","hint":"Which answer fits only what the clue shows?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Different sizes may have traveled as a herd","The small dinosaurs were hunting the big ones","A sudden flood forced them all to run","One giant dinosaur made every track"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-6-010","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Bamboozle is a real English word that means to trick or confuse someone.","why":"Bamboozle is in dictionaries. It has been used in English for centuries to mean fool or puzzle someone.","hint":"It sounds silly, but check the meaning.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-1-001","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is right for the fish with a big fin and sharp teeth?","why":"The sound 'ar' in shark is spelled with the letters a and r, like car and park.","hint":"Think of the word park.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["shark","sherk","shurk"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-002","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"One crab is on the rock. Now there are two. Which word tells about two?","why":"Most words add an s to mean more than one. Crab becomes crabs.","hint":"What small letter do we add for more than one?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["crabes","crabs","craby"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-1-003","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables (claps) are in the word 'octopus'?","why":"Clap it out: oc-to-pus. That is three claps, so three syllables.","hint":"Clap once for each beat in the word.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-1-004","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? 'Today my friend sam hit a home run.'","why":"Names of people start with a capital letter. Friend and home are common words.","hint":"Which word is somebody's name?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["friend","home","sam"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-1-005","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these sea animals in ABC order.","why":"ABC order follows the alphabet by first letter: c, e, s, w.","hint":"Say the alphabet and find each first letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["crab","eel","seal","whale"]},{"id":"wds-1-006","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'sun' and 'son' sound the same but have different meanings.","why":"Words that sound alike but mean different things are called homophones.","hint":"Say both words out loud.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-007","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word is a compound word, made from two small words?","why":"Starfish is star plus fish. Elephant and dinosaur are not made from two smaller words.","hint":"Look for two small words inside.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["starfish","elephant","dinosaur"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-008","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many letters are in the word 'baseball'?","why":"Count each letter: b-a-s-e-b-a-l-l. That makes eight.","hint":"Touch each letter as you count.","tags":["baseball"],"a":8},{"id":"wds-1-009","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the whale ____ out of the water. Which spelling is right?","why":"We write the past with -ed, even when it sounds like a t.","hint":"Think about how we spell the past.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["jumpted","jumped","jumpd"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-1-010","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Tooths' is the right way to say more than one tooth.","why":"Tooth is an irregular word. We say teeth, not tooths, when there is more than one.","hint":"Say 'one tooth, two ___.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-2-001","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the right spelling for the smart sea animal that clicks and squeaks?","why":"The sound f in dolphin is spelled ph, like in phone. The ending is -in.","hint":"Think of phone for the f sound.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["dolfin","dolphen","dolphin"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-2-002","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The shark's ____ swished from side to side. Which word fits?","why":"Tail is the part at the end of an animal. A tale is a story.","hint":"Which word is about a body part?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["tail","tale","tayl"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-2-003","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many words are in this sentence? The big shark swam fast.","why":"Count each word: The, big, shark, swam, fast. That makes five.","hint":"Point to each word as you count.","tags":["sharks"],"a":5},{"id":"wds-2-004","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the pitcher ____ the ball hard. Which word is correct?","why":"Throw is an irregular verb. Its past is threw, not throwed.","hint":"Say it out loud: 'Yesterday I ___.'","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["throwed","threw","thrown"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-2-005","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these in ABC order.","why":"All start with m, so check the second letter: a, e, e, o. Then the third: r before t.","hint":"They all start with the same letter.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mars","Mercury","meteor","moon"]},{"id":"wds-2-006","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What is the short way to write 'do not'?","why":"A contraction squeezes two words into one. The apostrophe takes the place of the missing o.","hint":"Where did the letter o go?","tags":[],"opts":["dont","do'nt","don't"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-2-007","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"You can call more than one fish just 'fish.'","why":"Fish is a special plural. We say 'two fish' when we mean several of the same kind.","hint":"Say 'one fish, two ___.'","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-2-008","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the sentences in story order.","why":"An egg is laid first, then it waits, then it cracks, and only then can a baby peep out.","hint":"What must happen before an egg can crack?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A mother dinosaur laid an egg.","The egg sat in a warm nest.","The egg cracked open.","A baby dinosaur peeped out."]},{"id":"wds-2-009","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? 'We sailed near alaska to see whales.'","why":"Names of places start with a capital letter. Sailed and whales are common words.","hint":"Which word names a place?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["alaska","sailed","whales"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-2-010","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'know' and 'no' sound the same but have different meanings.","why":"Know means to understand. No means not any. They are homophones, and know starts with a silent k.","hint":"Say both words out loud.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-3-001","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the right spelling for a giant reptile that lived long ago?","why":"Dinosaur comes from Greek roots: dino plus saur. It ends in -aur, not -ore.","hint":"The ending sounds like 'sore' but is spelled -saur.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["dinasaur","dinosaur","dinosore","dynosaur"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-002","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"We can ____ the stars tonight. Which word fits?","why":"See means to look with your eyes. A sea is a big body of salt water.","hint":"Which word is about using your eyes?","tags":["space"],"opts":["sea","seem","see","seen"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-3-003","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables are in the word 'stegosaurus'?","why":"Clap it out: ste-go-sau-rus. That is four parts.","hint":"Clap once for each beat.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-3-004","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the shark ____ a fish. Which word is correct?","why":"Eat is an irregular verb. Its simple past is ate. Eaten needs a helper like has or had.","hint":"Say 'Yesterday I ___ lunch.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["eated","eaten","eat","ate"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-3-005","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in ABC order.","why":"All start with sh, so look at the third letter: a, e, i, o.","hint":"Check the third letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["shark","shell","ship","shore"]},{"id":"wds-3-006","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase shows that the glove belongs to Mia?","why":"Add an apostrophe and s to a single owner's name to show it owns something.","hint":"Where does the apostrophe go for one owner?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Mia's glove","Mias glove","Mias' glove","Mia glove's"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-3-007","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Add -ing to 'glide.' Which spelling is correct?","why":"When a word ends in silent e, drop the e before adding -ing.","hint":"What happens to the silent e?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["glideing","gliding","glidding","glydeing"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-008","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these word parts in order to spell a shark with a wide, flat head.","why":"The parts ham, mer, and head spell hammerhead, a shark with eyes at the sides of its wide head.","hint":"Say the word slowly, one part at a time.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["ham","mer","head"]},{"id":"wds-3-009","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'jumped' has two syllables.","why":"Jumped has only one syllable. The -ed ending does not add a beat unless the word ends in t or d.","hint":"Clap it: how many beats?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-010","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","why":"A question needs a question mark at the end. A period or comma does not fit a question.","hint":"What does a question end with?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Do sharks ever stop swimming.","Do sharks ever stop swimming,","Do sharks ever stop swimming?","Do sharks ever stop swimming"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-4-001","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The whale lifted ____ tail out of the water. Which word fits?","why":"Its with no apostrophe shows belonging. It's always means it is or it has.","hint":"Try saying 'it is tail.' Does that work?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["it's","its'","it","its"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-002","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many vowel letters (a, e, i, o, u) are in the word 'astronaut'?","why":"The vowels are a, o, a, and u. The other letters are consonants.","hint":"Go through the word one letter at a time.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-4-003","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The shark has ____ across the bay many times. Which word is correct?","why":"After has, have, or had, use swum. Swam is for the simple past, like 'yesterday it swam.'","hint":"Does the sentence have the helper word has?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["swum","swam","swimmed","swimmen"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-4-004","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The baseball team celebrated ____ best season ever. Which word fits?","why":"Their shows that something belongs to them. There means a place, and they're means they are.","hint":"Whose best season was it?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["there","their","they're","thare"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-4-005","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these space words in order from the fewest syllables to the most.","why":"Star has one beat, plan-et two, as-ter-oid three, and con-stel-la-tion four.","hint":"Clap out each word.","tags":["space"],"opts":["star","planet","asteroid","constellation"]},{"id":"wds-4-006","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the correct spelling of the word that means 'to spell wrongly'?","why":"The prefix mis- is added to the whole word spell, so there are two s letters in a row and two l's.","hint":"Add mis- to the word spell without dropping a letter.","tags":[],"opts":["mispell","misspel","misspell","misspelle"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-4-007","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In the contraction 'doesn't,' the apostrophe takes the place of the letter e.","why":"Does not becomes doesn't. The missing letter is the o in not.","hint":"Which word lost a letter, does or not?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-4-008","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words end with a suffix meaning 'without': fearless, careless, helpful, endless, joyful?","why":"Fearless, careless, and endless end in -less, which means without. Helpful and joyful end in -ful, which means full of.","hint":"Look at the last four letters of each.","tags":[],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-009","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Three players each have a glove. Which sentence is correct?","why":"For a plural that already ends in s, add just an apostrophe after the s to show owning.","hint":"There is more than one owner.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The player's gloves are on the bench.","The players gloves are on the bench.","The players's gloves are on the bench.","The players' gloves are on the bench."],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-010","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence has the correct punctuation?","why":"Put a comma after an interjection like wow. End a strong statement with an exclamation mark.","hint":"Where does a short pause belong?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Wow, that comet is bright!","Wow that comet is bright!","Wow, that comet is bright?","Wow. that comet is bright!"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-5-001","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon's pull has a strong ____ on ocean tides. Which word fits?","why":"Effect is usually the noun, meaning a result. Affect is usually the verb meaning to change something.","hint":"Is the missing word a thing or an action?","tags":["space","ocean"],"opts":["affect","effect","affekt","efect"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-002","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables are in the word 'carnivorous'?","why":"Clap it out: car-niv-o-rous. That is four syllables.","hint":"Clap once for each beat.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-5-003","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have many senses, ____ smell. Which spelling is correct?","why":"Especially starts with es, not ex, and ends with -ally, since the base word is especial.","hint":"It begins like the word escape.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["expecially","especialy","especially","especally"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-5-004","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is in the past tense?","why":"Streaked tells about something that already happened. The other sentences are present or future.","hint":"Which sentence tells about something already over?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The comet streaks across the sky.","The comet will streak across the sky.","The comet is streaking across the sky.","The comet streaked across the sky."],"a":3},{"id":"wds-5-005","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words are spelled correctly: friend, freind, believe, beleive, receive?","why":"Friend, believe, and receive are right. The rule: i before e, except after c, as in receive.","hint":"Remember i before e, except after c.","tags":[],"a":3},{"id":"wds-5-006","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'Swimming in the deep sea.' is a complete sentence.","why":"It has no subject telling who is swimming, so it is a fragment. 'Sharks are swimming in the deep sea' is complete.","hint":"A sentence needs someone doing something.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-007","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses they're, their, and there correctly?","why":"They're means they are, and their shows belonging. There names a place.","hint":"Replace each with 'they are' to test.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["They're hoping their team wins.","Their hoping they're team wins.","There hoping their team wins.","They're hoping there team wins."],"a":0},{"id":"wds-5-008","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"order","q":"These four ocean animals all start with st. Put them in ABC order.","why":"All begin with st, so check the third letter: a, i, o, u.","hint":"The first two letters match, so look at the third.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["starfish","stingray","stonefish","sturgeon"]},{"id":"wds-5-009","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Add -ly to 'hungry' to tell how the shark ate. Which spelling is right?","why":"When a word ends in y after a consonant, change the y to i before adding -ly.","hint":"What happens to the y?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["hungryly","hungrily","hungrly","hungryily"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-010","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The plural of 'moose' is 'moose.'","why":"Moose does not change. Deer, sheep, and fish also keep the same form for one or many.","hint":"Think about 'one moose, two ___.'","tags":["animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-001","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the correct spelling of the word that means 'needed'?","why":"Remember: one collar (c) and two sleeves (ss). Necessary has one c and two s's.","hint":"One collar, two sleeves.","tags":[],"opts":["neccessary","necesary","necessary","neccesary"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-6-002","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these word parts in order to spell the scientist who studies stars.","why":"Astronomer comes from Greek words for star and law: as-tron-o-mer.","hint":"Say the word slowly and listen to each part.","tags":["space"],"opts":["as","tron","o","mer"]},{"id":"wds-6-003","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A loud crowd can ____ a pitcher's focus. Which word fits?","why":"Affect is usually the verb, meaning to change. Effect is usually the noun, meaning a result.","hint":"Is the missing word an action?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["effect","affact","effekt","affect"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-6-004","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses who's and whose correctly?","why":"Who's means who is. Whose shows belonging. Test: 'Who is the player?' works, and 'who is bat' does not.","hint":"Try replacing each with 'who is.'","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Who's the player whose bat broke?","Whose the player who's bat broke?","Who's the player who's bat broke?","Whose the player whose bat broke?"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-6-005","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"number","q":"How many letters are in the word 'tyrannosaurus'?","why":"Count carefully: t-y-r-a-n-n-o-s-a-u-r-u-s. It has two n's and thirteen letters in all.","hint":"Write the letters down and count them.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":13,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-006","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'conscience' has the whole word 'science' hiding inside it.","why":"Look at the letters: con-SCIENCE. The word science is spelled right there in the second half.","hint":"Read the last seven letters.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-007","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is correct for the word meaning 'without a doubt'?","why":"Definitely has the word finite hiding inside it: de-FINITE-ly. It is never spelled with 'ately.'","hint":"Look for the word finite inside.","tags":[],"opts":["definately","definitely","definitly","defanitely"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-6-008","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"order","q":"These words share their first letters. Put them in ABC order, checking each letter in turn.","why":"Comet comes first (e before m). Comma is a shorter version of command, so it comes first. Then commit (a before i).","hint":"Compare letter by letter. A shorter word comes first.","tags":["space"],"opts":["comet","comma","command","commit"]},{"id":"wds-6-009","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is right for 'to keep things apart'?","why":"Separate has a rat in the middle: sepARATe. That helps you remember the a.","hint":"Look for a small animal hiding in the middle.","tags":[],"opts":["seperate","separete","separate","seperete"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-6-010","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses commas correctly?","why":"Extra information in the middle of a sentence is set off by a comma before and after it.","hint":"The extra phrase needs a comma on both sides.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["The hammerhead a shark, eats rays.","The hammerhead, a shark eats rays.","The, hammerhead a shark, eats rays.","The hammerhead, a shark, eats rays."],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-1-001","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🦈 🐟 🦈 🐟 🦈 ❓","why":"The pattern is shark, fish, shark, fish. After a shark comes a fish.","hint":"Say the pattern out loud. Which two repeat?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["🦈","🐟","🐙"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-1-002","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Bat, mitt and snorkel: which one is NOT used to play baseball?","why":"A bat hits the ball and a mitt catches it. A snorkel is for swimming.","hint":"Think about what players use on the field.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Bat","Mitt","Snorkel"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-003","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐  How many ⭐ are in the row?","why":"Count only the stars. They sit between the planets: 1, 2, 3, 4.","hint":"Touch each ⭐ and count. Skip the 🪐.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"log-en-1-004","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🔴 🔴 🟦 🔴 🔴 ❓","why":"The pattern is two reds, then one blue. We just had two reds, so blue is next.","hint":"Look for the group that keeps repeating.","tags":[],"opts":["🟦","🔴","🟢"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-1-005","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these sea animals in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"A shrimp fits in your hand. A turtle is bigger, a great white is bigger still, and a blue whale is giant.","hint":"Start with the one you could hold.","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["Shrimp","Sea turtle","Great white shark","Blue whale"],"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-1-006","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Counting by 2s should go 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Here is a row: 2, 4, 6, 7, 10. Which number is a mistake?","why":"After 6 we add 2 and get 8, not 7. The 7 broke the pattern.","hint":"Add 2 each time and see where it goes wrong.","tags":[],"opts":["4","7","10"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-1-007","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: after 🔺 🔵 🔺 🔵 🔺 🔵 the next shape is 🔺.","why":"The pattern is triangle, circle, triangle, circle. The row ended on a circle, so a triangle is next.","hint":"See which shape the row ended on.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-1-008","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A baseball team has 9 players on the field. 3 of them are in the outfield. How many are NOT in the outfield?","why":"9 take away 3 leaves 6 players. They are the pitcher, catcher and infielders.","hint":"Start at 9 and count back 3.","tags":["baseball"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-1-009","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put a baby dinosaur's life in order, first to last.","why":"Dinosaurs started in an egg. They hatched, grew up, and then could lay eggs of their own.","hint":"What comes before hatching?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Egg","Hatchling","Young dinosaur","Grown-up dinosaur"]},{"id":"log-en-1-010","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"🦈 🦈 🦈 🐬 🦈  Which place is the odd one out?","why":"Four animals are sharks and one is a dolphin. The dolphin is in the 4th spot.","hint":"Find the one that is different, then count from the left.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["2nd","3rd","4th"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-011","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A rocket countdown goes 5, 4, 3, ❓, 1. What number is missing?","why":"Count backward by 1 each time. After 3 comes 2, and then 1.","hint":"Count backward one step at a time.","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-012","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A whale is bigger than a crab. A crab is bigger than a shrimp. Which one is the smallest?","why":"The shrimp is smaller than the crab, and the crab is smaller than the whale. So shrimp is smallest.","hint":"Line them up from big to small.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Whale","Crab","Shrimp"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-001","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A rocket counts down by 2s: 30, 28, 26, 24, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is 2 less than the one before. 24 minus 2 is 22.","hint":"Find how much each number drops.","tags":["space"],"a":22},{"id":"log-en-2-002","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A dino dig numbers its bones: 3, 8, 13, 18, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"The numbers go up by 5 each time. 18 plus 5 is 23.","hint":"Subtract neighbors to find the jump.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["21","23","24"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-2-003","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Shark A shows 15 teeth. Shark B shows 51 teeth. Which shark shows MORE teeth?","why":"51 has 5 tens and 15 has only 1 ten. The tens digit decides.","hint":"Compare the tens digits first.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["They are the same","Shark A: 15 teeth","Shark B: 51 teeth"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-004","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Home runs hit by four players: 18, 9, 31, 24. Put them from fewest to most.","why":"Compare the tens first: 9 is the smallest, then 18, then 24, then 31.","hint":"Find the smallest first, then the next.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["9","18","24","31"]},{"id":"log-en-2-005","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The fins box holds a shark, a tuna and an eagle. Which one is in the wrong box?","why":"Sharks and tuna are fish with fins. An eagle has wings, so it belongs in a different box.","hint":"Ask which animal has no fins.","tags":["sharks","animals"],"opts":["Eagle","Shark","Tuna"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-2-006","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A star pattern grows: group 1 has 1 star, group 2 has 3 stars, group 3 has 5 stars. How many stars are in group 5?","why":"Each group adds 2 stars: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. Group 5 has 9 stars.","hint":"Find how many stars each group adds.","tags":["space"],"a":9},{"id":"log-en-2-007","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The even box holds 4, 8 and 12. Which number also belongs in the even box?","why":"Even numbers split into two equal groups. 18 does, but 15 and 21 leave one extra.","hint":"Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8.","tags":[],"opts":["18","15","21"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-2-008","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Sam throws faster than Lee. Lee throws faster than Ty. Put the pitchers from slowest to fastest.","why":"Ty is slower than Lee and Lee is slower than Sam. So the order is Ty, Lee, Sam.","hint":"Find who is slower than everyone.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Ty","Lee","Sam"]},{"id":"log-en-2-009","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Four of these numbers are even and one is not: 12, 14, 16, 19, 20. Which does NOT belong?","why":"12, 14, 16 and 20 are all even. 19 is odd, so it is the one left out.","hint":"Check the last digit of each number.","tags":[],"opts":["12","20","19"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-010","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: if you count by 5s from 5 (5, 10, 15, 20...), you will say the number 18.","why":"Counting by 5s lands on numbers ending in 5 or 0. 18 ends in 8, so you skip it.","hint":"Look at the last digit each time.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-2-011","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"The Rangers scored 6 runs. The Waves scored 4 more runs than the Rangers. How many runs did the Waves score?","why":"4 more than 6 is 10. The Waves scored 10 runs.","hint":"Start at 6 and count up 4.","tags":["baseball"],"a":10},{"id":"log-en-2-012","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which shape goes in the empty spot? Row 1: 🔺 🔷 🔺. Row 2: 🔷 🔺 🔷. Row 3: 🔺 🔷 ❓","why":"Every row and column swaps between triangle and diamond. After 🔺 🔷 comes 🔺.","hint":"Look at what is above the empty spot.","tags":[],"opts":["🔷","🔺","🟢"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-3-001","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A moon rover finds this many rocks each day: 2, 5, 8, 11. If the pattern keeps going, how many on day 5?","why":"The pattern adds 3 each day. 11 plus 3 is 14.","hint":"Check how much it grows from day to day.","tags":["space"],"opts":["13","14","15","16"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-3-002","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Jo is taller than Kai. Lee is shorter than Kai. Mo is taller than Jo. Put the four players from shortest to tallest.","why":"Lee is below Kai, Kai is below Jo, and Jo is below Mo. Chain them together.","hint":"Draw a line and place one player at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Lee","Kai","Jo","Mo"]},{"id":"log-en-3-003","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A fossil hunter's boxes hold 4, 7, 10, 13 bones, and so on. How many bones are in the 7th box?","why":"Each box holds 3 more. After 13 comes 16, 19, then 22 in the 7th box.","hint":"Keep adding the same number, box by box.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":22},{"id":"log-en-3-004","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Each row and column has one 🦈, one 🐙 and one 🐢. Row 1: 🦈 🐙 🐢. Row 2: 🐙 🐢 🦈. Row 3: 🐢 ❓ 🐙. What goes in ❓?","why":"Row 3 already has 🐢 and 🐙, so it needs 🦈. Column 2 agrees.","hint":"Which animal is missing from Row 3?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["🐬","🐙","🐢","🦈"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-3-005","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"The pattern 🪐 ⭐ ⭐ repeats over and over. Among the first 10 items, how many are ⭐?","why":"Items 1 to 10 are 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐. That is three groups of 2 stars.","hint":"Write out all 10 items and count.","tags":["space"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-3-006","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In a dino parade, Stegosaurus walks behind Triceratops. T. rex walks in front of Triceratops. Who walks last?","why":"The line is T. rex, Triceratops, Stegosaurus. The one behind everybody is last.","hint":"Put the three dinos in a line.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["T. rex","Triceratops","Stegosaurus","Can't tell"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-3-007","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: in the pattern 20, 17, 14, 11, the next number is 8.","why":"Each number is 3 less than the one before. 11 minus 3 is 8.","hint":"Find how much each number drops.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-3-008","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Work out each sum, then put them from least to greatest.","why":"The sums are 8, 10, 11 and 13. Order the answers, not the first numbers.","hint":"Add first, then compare the answers.","tags":[],"opts":["4 + 4","3 + 7","2 + 9","5 + 8"]},{"id":"log-en-3-009","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A skip pattern goes 6, 9, 12, 16, 18. Which number is the mistake?","why":"Adding 3 each time gives 6, 9, 12, 15, 18. The 16 should be 15.","hint":"Add the same jump each time to find the break.","tags":[],"opts":["16","9","12","18"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-3-010","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"In each row of a number grid, the three numbers add up to 12. Row 1: 5, 4, 3. Row 2: 6, ❓, 2. What number is ❓?","why":"6 plus 2 is 8. To reach 12 you need 4 more.","hint":"First add the two numbers you can see.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"log-en-3-011","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Three of these are sharks. Which one is NOT a shark?","why":"Hammerheads, great whites and whale sharks are fish. An orca is really a dolphin, a mammal that breathes air.","hint":"Think about which are fish and which breathe air.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Orca","Hammerhead","Great white","Whale shark"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-3-012","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The letters follow a pattern: A B A C A D A ❓. What comes next?","why":"Every other letter is A. The letters in between go B, C, D, so E is next.","hint":"Look at the letters between the A's.","tags":[],"opts":["B","D","A","E"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-001","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Mystery number: it is more than 20, less than 30, a multiple of 7, and odd. What is it?","why":"The multiples of 7 between 20 and 30 are 21 and 28. Only 21 is odd.","hint":"List the multiples of 7 near 20 first.","tags":[],"a":21},{"id":"log-en-4-002","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A pitcher's jersey number has two digits. The digits add up to 9, and the tens digit is double the ones digit. What is the number?","why":"Try 6 and 3. They add to 9, and 6 is double 3. So the number is 63.","hint":"Try pairs of digits that add to 9.","tags":["baseball"],"a":63},{"id":"log-en-4-003","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Every shark in an aquarium is gray. Mia sees a gray fish and says it must be a shark. What is wrong with her thinking?","why":"The clue says sharks are gray, not that every gray fish is a shark.","hint":"Does the clue work backward too?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Other kinds of fish can be gray too","Sharks are never gray","Fish cannot be gray","Nothing is wrong"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-4-004","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Leo has 3 boxes with 4 stickers in each. He says 3 + 4 = 7 stickers. How many stickers does he really have?","why":"Each box has 4, and there are 3 boxes: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12. He added the wrong numbers.","hint":"Add the stickers box by box.","tags":[],"a":12},{"id":"log-en-4-005","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Nia, Omar and Pia each love a different thing: sharks, dinosaurs or space. Nia loves neither sharks nor dinosaurs. Omar does not love sharks. Who loves sharks?","why":"Nia must love space. Omar does not love sharks, so he loves dinosaurs. That leaves Pia with sharks.","hint":"Start with the kid who has the most clues.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs","space"],"opts":["Nia","Omar","Can't tell","Pia"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-006","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four dinosaurs race. Yan finishes right after Zed. Wes finishes before Zed. Xia finishes after Yan. Put them in finishing order, first to last.","why":"Wes comes before Zed, Yan is right after Zed, and Xia is after Yan. That makes Wes, Zed, Yan, Xia.","hint":"Place the one with a 'before' clue first.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Wes","Zed","Yan","Xia"]},{"id":"log-en-4-007","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A crab walks from A to B, moving only right or down. B is 2 steps right and 2 steps down from A. How many different shortest paths can it take?","why":"List them: RRDD, RDRD, RDDR, DRRD, DRDR, DDRR. That is 6 paths.","hint":"List the paths in an organized way.","tags":["ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-4-008","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: All sharks are fish, and a hammerhead is a shark. So a hammerhead must be a fish.","why":"A hammerhead is a shark, and every shark is a fish. So the conclusion must be true.","hint":"Follow the chain one link at a time.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-4-009","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which number fits ALL the clues? It is between 40 and 60. It is a multiple of 5. The tens digit is 5. The ones digit is odd.","why":"Multiples of 5 with a tens digit of 5 are 50 and 55. Only 55 has an odd ones digit.","hint":"Cross out numbers one clue at a time.","tags":[],"opts":["50","45","60","55"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-010","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four fans leave a baseball game after 1/8, 1/4, 1/2 and 3/4 of it is done. Put them from the first to leave to the last.","why":"Eighths are the smallest pieces, then fourths, then halves. 3/4 is the biggest, so that fan leaves last.","hint":"Picture a pizza cut into pieces.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["1/8","1/4","1/2","3/4"]},{"id":"log-en-4-011","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Today is Wednesday. A rocket launches in 10 days. What day of the week is the launch?","why":"7 days later is Wednesday again, and 3 more days is Saturday.","hint":"Count a full week first, then the extra days.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Friday","Saturday","Sunday","Monday"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-4-012","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A coach says: 'We lost our last 3 games, so we will lose the next one.' What is wrong with that thinking?","why":"The next game is a new game. Earlier losses cannot force a result.","hint":"Does the past control what happens next?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Teams cannot lose 3 in a row","He should say they will win","Nothing is wrong","Past games do not decide the next one"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-5-001","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If it rains, the baseball game is canceled. The game was NOT canceled. What must be true?","why":"If rain always cancels the game, then no cancellation means there was no rain.","hint":"Test it: what would rain have done to the game?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["It rained","We cannot tell","Rain started late","It did not rain"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-5-002","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A toy shark balances 3 toy fish on a scale. One toy fish balances 2 toy crabs. How many toy crabs balance one toy shark?","why":"One shark equals 3 fish. Each fish equals 2 crabs. So 3 groups of 2 make 6 crabs.","hint":"Turn the shark into fish first.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-5-003","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Moon rocks on a balance scale: A is heavier than B. C is lighter than B. D is heavier than A. Order them from lightest to heaviest.","why":"C is below B, B is below A, and A is below D. Chain the clues together.","hint":"Build a line one clue at a time.","tags":["space"],"opts":["C","B","A","D"]},{"id":"log-en-5-004","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"What number comes next in this pattern? 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 15, ❓","why":"The steps take turns: add 1, then double. 15 doubled is 30.","hint":"Two different steps take turns.","tags":[],"opts":["30","16","28","31"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-5-005","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A fossil hunter doubled her bones, then found 5 more. Now she has 21 bones. How many did she start with?","why":"Work backward: 21 minus 5 is 16. Half of 16 is 8.","hint":"Undo the steps in reverse order.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":8},{"id":"log-en-5-006","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If it rains, the game is canceled. The game was canceled. Did it rain?","why":"Rain causes cancellation, but other things could cancel a game too, such as lightning or a broken field.","hint":"Could anything else cancel a game?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Yes, it must have rained","No, it did not rain","Yes, but only a little","We cannot tell for sure"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-5-007","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"In a class of 20 kids, 12 like sharks and 10 like dinosaurs. Every kid likes at least one. How many like both?","why":"12 + 10 = 22, which is 2 more than 20 kids. Those 2 were counted twice.","hint":"Add the groups, then compare with the class size.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-5-008","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A toy starfish and 2 marbles balance 5 marbles on a scale. How many marbles balance one toy starfish?","why":"Take 2 marbles from each side. The starfish balances the 3 marbles left.","hint":"Remove the same thing from both sides.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["2","3","4","5"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-5-009","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: If a shape is a square, it has 4 sides. This shape has 4 sides, so it must be a square.","why":"Rectangles and diamonds have 4 sides too. Square leads to 4 sides, but 4 sides does not lead to square.","hint":"Can you draw a 4-sided shape that is not a square?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-5-010","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A pattern grows: ⚾ 🧢 ⚾⚾ 🧢 ⚾⚾⚾ 🧢 and so on. Counting from 1, in which spot is the 5th 🧢?","why":"Caps sit after groups of 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 balls. That is 15 balls and 5 caps, so spot 20.","hint":"Keep writing the pattern out and count each spot.","tags":["baseball"],"a":20},{"id":"log-en-5-011","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sam thinks of a number, adds 6, then halves the result. He gets 7. What was his number?","why":"Undo halving: 7 doubled is 14. Undo adding 6: 14 minus 6 is 8.","hint":"Undo the last step first.","tags":[],"opts":["14","20","8","10"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-5-012","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A space show ended at 4:10 PM after running 2 hours and 25 minutes. When did it start?","why":"Back up 2 hours to 2:10. Then back up 25 minutes to 1:45 PM.","hint":"Go back one hour piece, then the minutes.","tags":["space"],"opts":["1:35 PM","1:45 PM","2:15 PM","1:55 PM"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-001","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A toy bat and a toy ball cost $1.10 together. The bat costs $1 more than the ball. How many cents does the ball cost?","why":"Try 5 cents: the bat is $1.05 and the total is $1.10. A 10-cent ball makes the total $1.20.","hint":"Test your first guess by adding both prices.","tags":["baseball"],"a":5,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-6-002","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Two coins add up to 30 cents. One of them is not a nickel. Which two coins are they?","why":"The quarter is the one that is not a nickel. The other coin IS a nickel. 25 + 5 = 30.","hint":"Read 'one of them' very carefully.","tags":["money"],"opts":["A dime and a nickel","A quarter and a nickel","Two dimes","A quarter and a dime"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-6-003","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Finn and Gill are sharks in a riddle. Each always tells the truth or always lies. Finn says: 'At least one of us is a liar.' Who is who?","why":"If Finn were a liar, his sentence would be true. That is impossible. So Finn is honest, and Gill is the liar.","hint":"Test what happens if Finn is a liar.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Both always tell the truth","Both always lie","Finn lies and Gill tells the truth","Finn tells the truth and Gill lies"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-6-004","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"6 baseball players shake hands after a game. Every player shakes hands with every other player exactly once. How many handshakes happen?","why":"Each of 6 players shakes 5 hands, which counts every handshake twice: 30 divided by 2 is 15.","hint":"Count one player's handshakes, then fix the double counting.","tags":["baseball"],"a":15},{"id":"log-en-6-005","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A cube net has four squares in a row: A, B, C, D. Square E is above B and square F is below B. Folded into a cube, which square is opposite B?","why":"A, B, C, D wrap around the cube like a belt. Squares two steps apart are opposite, so D faces B.","hint":"Picture the row of four wrapping around a box.","tags":[],"opts":["A","C","F","D"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-6-006","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Five players bat in order. Rae bats last. Sol bats right before Rae. Pat bats before Quinn. Tia bats before Pat. Put them in batting order, first to last.","why":"Rae is 5th and Sol is 4th. Tia, Pat, Quinn fill spots 1 to 3, with Tia before Pat before Quinn.","hint":"Place the sure spots first, then fill the gaps.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Tia","Pat","Quinn","Sol","Rae"]},{"id":"log-en-6-007","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A 3 by 3 by 3 cube is painted red on the outside, then cut into 27 small cubes. How many small cubes have exactly 2 red faces?","why":"Those are the edge cubes, one in the middle of each of the 12 edges. Corners show 3 red faces.","hint":"Where do two painted faces meet?","tags":[],"a":12},{"id":"log-en-6-008","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A museum has 17 dinosaur skeletons. All but 9 of them are moved to a new hall. How many skeletons did NOT move?","why":"'All but 9' means 9 stayed behind. The other 8 moved. The 17 is a distraction.","hint":"Which ones did NOT move?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["9","8","17","26"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-6-009","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Algae in a tide pool doubles every day. On day 12 it covers the whole pool. On which day was the pool half covered?","why":"One doubling later it fills the pool. So half-covered was one day earlier, on day 11.","hint":"Doubling goes forward. What does half do backward?","tags":["ocean"],"a":11},{"id":"log-en-6-010","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A dark drawer holds 10 red baseball socks and 10 blue ones, all mixed up. What is the fewest socks you must grab to be SURE of a matching pair?","why":"Two socks might be one of each color. The third sock must match one of the first two.","hint":"What is the worst luck you could have?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["2","3","4","11"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-011","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: A fair coin lands heads 5 times in a row. On the next flip, tails is more likely.","why":"Each flip is separate. The coin does not remember, so heads and tails are still equally likely.","hint":"Does a coin remember its past flips?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-012","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"8 space rocks look identical, but one is heavier. What is the fewest weighings on a balance scale that is guaranteed to find the heavy one?","why":"Weigh 3 against 3. Balanced means it's in the other 2; otherwise it's in the heavier 3. One more weighing finishes.","hint":"One weighing has 3 outcomes: left, right, balanced.","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-1-001","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🦕 🦖 🥚 🦕 🦖 🥚 🦕 ❓","why":"The pattern repeats in groups of three: 🦕 🦖 🥚. After 🦕 always comes 🦖.","hint":"Find the small group that repeats.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["🦕","🥚","🦖"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-1-002","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Each crab has 2 claws. How many claws do 3 crabs have?","why":"Add the claws crab by crab: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.","hint":"Add the claws one crab at a time.","tags":["ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"logB-1-003","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Earth, Mars and the Moon: which one is NOT a planet?","why":"The Moon circles Earth. Planets circle the Sun, and Earth and Mars both do.","hint":"Which one circles another world?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mars","The Moon","Earth"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-1-004","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the rocket numbers in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Count up: 1 comes before 3, then 6, then 8.","hint":"Find the smallest number first.","tags":["space"],"opts":["1","3","6","8"]},{"id":"logB-1-005","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: counting down 10, 9, 8, 7, the next number is 6.","why":"Counting down means one less each time, so after 7 comes 6.","hint":"Take away one each time.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-1-006","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"In line for the submarine, Kit is first, Lu is second, Dev is third and Ana is fourth. Who is right behind Lu?","why":"Right behind means the very next place. After second place comes third place, and that is Dev.","hint":"Find Lu, then look one place later.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Dev","Kit","Ana"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-1-007","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"I live in the sea. I have gills, fins and sharp teeth. What am I?","why":"Whales breathe air and seagulls fly. Gills and fins are clues for a fish like a shark.","hint":"Which animal breathes with gills?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["A whale","A shark","A seagull"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-1-008","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Count by 10s: 10, 20, 30, ❓, 50. What number is missing?","why":"Each number is 10 more than the one before. 30 + 10 = 40.","hint":"Add 10 each time.","tags":[],"a":40},{"id":"logB-1-009","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the biggest number: 27, 72 or 17?","why":"Look at the tens first. 7 tens is more than 2 tens or 1 ten.","hint":"Compare the tens digit first.","tags":[],"opts":["17","72","27"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-1-010","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: 5 + 2 gives the same answer as 2 + 5.","why":"You can add numbers in either order. Both ways make 7.","hint":"Try adding it both ways.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-1-011","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🧤 ⚾ 🧤 ⚾ 🧤 ❓","why":"The pattern goes glove, ball, glove, ball. After a glove comes a ball.","hint":"Say the pattern out loud.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["🧢","🧤","⚾"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-1-012","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these in the order a runner touches them, first to last.","why":"A runner goes around the diamond: first, second, third, then home.","hint":"Think about where a runner starts and finishes.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["First base","Second base","Third base","Home plate"]},{"id":"logB-1-013","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A shark sees 9 fish. 4 swim away. How many fish are left?","why":"Take away 4 from 9. 9 − 4 = 5.","hint":"Count back 4 from 9.","tags":["sharks"],"a":5},{"id":"logB-1-014","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Stegosaurus, T. rex and whale: which one is NOT a dinosaur?","why":"A whale is a huge sea animal, but it is a mammal, not a dinosaur.","hint":"Which one lives in the ocean today?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Whale","Stegosaurus","T. rex"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-1-015","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"If it is raining, you take an umbrella. It is raining. What do you take?","why":"The rule says rain means umbrella, and it is raining, so you take an umbrella.","hint":"Use the rule on the rainy day.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Sandals","An umbrella","Sunglasses"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-1-016","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: after Tuesday comes Thursday.","why":"Wednesday comes in between: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday.","hint":"Say the days of the week in order.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-1-017","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which row follows the SAME pattern as 🔴 🔵 🔴 🔵?","why":"The pattern is A B A B. Only the green, yellow, green, yellow row matches it.","hint":"Match each picture to its spot.","tags":[],"opts":["🟢 🟡 🟢 🟡","🟢 🟡 🟡 🟢","🟢 🟢 🟡 🟡"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-1-018","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A rocket has 10 seats. 6 astronauts sit down. How many seats are empty?","why":"10 − 6 = 4 empty seats.","hint":"Count up from 6 to 10.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"logB-1-019","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Strike, glove and tent: which one does NOT go with baseball?","why":"Strikes and gloves are part of baseball. A tent is for camping.","hint":"Think about what you see at a ball game.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Glove","Strike","Tent"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-1-020","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"I have a face and two hands, but I cannot wave. What am I?","why":"A clock has a face and two hands that point to the time. They never wave.","hint":"Think about how you tell time.","tags":[],"opts":["A pillow","A pencil","A clock"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-2-001","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A rocket drops a part every 3 minutes: 3, 6, 9, 12, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is 3 more than the one before. 12 + 3 = 15.","hint":"Find how much the numbers grow each step.","tags":["space"],"opts":["15","14","16"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-2-002","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A dinosaur nest holds 4 eggs. How many eggs are in 3 nests?","why":"Add the nests: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 eggs.","hint":"Add 4 three times.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":12},{"id":"logB-2-003","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Think of the outline of a baseball. Which shape has NO corners?","why":"A circle is perfectly round, so it has no corners. Squares and triangles do.","hint":"Run your finger around each shape.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Square","Circle","Triangle"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-2-004","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order during one day, starting in the morning.","why":"The Sun comes up, is high at noon, goes down at sunset, and the day ends at midnight.","hint":"Follow the Sun across the sky.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Sunrise","Noon","Sunset","Midnight"]},{"id":"logB-2-005","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: 10 + 6 is bigger than 9 + 9.","why":"10 + 6 = 16, but 9 + 9 = 18, so 18 is bigger.","hint":"Work out both sums first.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-2-006","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sam, Pia and Tia each have a different pet: a fish, a dog or a bird. Sam's pet has fins. Pia's pet has feathers. What pet does Tia have?","why":"Fins mean Sam has the fish. Feathers mean Pia has the bird. The dog is left for Tia.","hint":"Give each pet to its owner, one at a time.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A fish","A dog","A bird"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-2-007","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The more of me you take, the more you leave behind. What am I?","why":"Every step you take on a sandy beach leaves one more footprint behind you.","hint":"Picture walking along a beach.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Shadows","Cookies","Footsteps"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-2-008","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"I am 10 more than 36. What number am I?","why":"Adding 10 changes only the tens: 36 becomes 46.","hint":"Which digit changes when you add 10?","tags":[],"a":46},{"id":"logB-2-009","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Pitcher, catcher and goalie: which job is NOT on a baseball team?","why":"Pitchers and catchers work together in baseball. Goalies play in sports like soccer and hockey.","hint":"Which job guards a net?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Goalie","Catcher","Pitcher"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-2-010","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: Every dino in this park eats plants. Zed is a dino in this park. So Zed eats plants.","why":"If every dino in the park eats plants, then Zed must too.","hint":"If it is true for every dino, it is true for Zed.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-2-011","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball game starts at 1:00 and lasts 3 hours. When does it end?","why":"Count 3 hours on from 1:00: 2:00, 3:00, 4:00.","hint":"Count the hours one at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["3:00","4:00","5:00"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-2-012","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A diver sees 6 sharks, 3 rays and 4 turtles. How many animals does she see in all?","why":"6 + 3 = 9, and 9 + 4 = 13.","hint":"Add two numbers, then add the third.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"a":13},{"id":"logB-2-013","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"If a pitcher throws 3 strikes, the batter is out. Cal struck out. What did the pitcher throw?","why":"Three strikes make an out. Three balls would let the batter walk instead.","hint":"Use the rule backward.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Three balls","Three home runs","Three strikes"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-2-014","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Four kids sit in a row: Ana, Ben, Cy and Dee. Ana sits on the far left. Ben sits next to Ana. Cy sits next to Ben. Who sits on the far right?","why":"The order from left is Ana, Ben, Cy, then Dee. Dee is the only one left for the end.","hint":"Place the kids from the left, one at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Dee","Cy","Ben"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-2-015","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: the pattern 1, 2, 4, 8 adds the same number each time.","why":"It adds 1, then 2, then 4. The numbers double instead of growing by the same amount.","hint":"Check how much each step grows.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-2-016","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The pattern 🦖 🌋 🌋 repeats forever. What is the 7th picture?","why":"The 🦖 lands in spots 1, 4, 7, and so on. Spot 7 is a 🦖.","hint":"Write the pattern out until you reach 7.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["🌋","🥚","🦖"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-2-017","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A snack costs 3 dollars. Pia buys 2 snacks and pays with a 10-dollar bill. How many dollars of change does she get?","why":"2 snacks cost 6 dollars. 10 − 6 = 4 dollars back.","hint":"Find the total cost first.","tags":["money","food"],"a":4},{"id":"logB-2-018","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which weighs more: a pound of rocks or a pound of feathers?","why":"A pound is a pound! The feathers just take up much more space.","hint":"Read how much of each there is.","tags":[],"opts":["The rocks","They weigh the same","The feathers"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-2-019","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Tom, Uma and Val wear different baseball caps: red, blue or green. Tom wears red. Uma does not wear blue. What color does Val wear?","why":"Tom has red. Uma cannot have blue, so she has green. That leaves blue for Val.","hint":"Start with the color Uma cannot wear.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Green","Red","Blue"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-2-020","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"Today is Monday. A moon rover lands on Friday. How many days from today is that?","why":"Count on: Tuesday 1, Wednesday 2, Thursday 3, Friday 4.","hint":"Count forward day by day.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"logB-3-001","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A countdown goes down by 4 each time: 40, 36, 32, 28, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each step takes away 4. 28 − 4 = 24.","hint":"Subtract the same amount every step.","tags":["space"],"opts":["25","24","22","26"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-3-002","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A shark tank has 4 rows with 5 fish in each row. How many fish are in the tank?","why":"Four rows of 5 is 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20.","hint":"Add the rows, or count by 5s four times.","tags":["sharks"],"a":20},{"id":"logB-3-003","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: when you add any two odd numbers, the answer is always even.","why":"Try 3 + 5 = 8 and 7 + 9 = 16. Two odd numbers always pair up with nothing left over.","hint":"Test a few examples.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-3-004","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Ava, Ben and Cal finish first, second and third in a dino quiz. Ava did better than Ben. Cal did better than Ava. Who finished second?","why":"Cal beat Ava, and Ava beat Ben. So the order is Cal, Ava, Ben.","hint":"Put the clues in a line from best to worst.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Ava","We can't tell","Ben","Cal"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-3-005","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Four baby sharks leave a cave. Fin leaves after Gus. Gus leaves after Hal. Ivy leaves before Hal. Put them in order, first to last.","why":"Ivy is before Hal, Hal is before Gus, and Gus is before Fin.","hint":"Find who is before everyone else.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Ivy","Hal","Gus","Fin"]},{"id":"logB-3-006","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Counting by 6s goes 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36. Which of these numbers is NOT said when counting by 6s?","why":"40 falls between 36 and 42, so it is skipped when counting by 6s.","hint":"Count by 6s until you pass each number.","tags":[],"opts":["12","36","30","40"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-3-007","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A pitcher throws 15 pitches in the first inning and 12 in the second. He may throw 40 in total. How many more pitches can he throw?","why":"15 + 12 = 27 pitches so far. 40 − 27 = 13.","hint":"Add what he threw, then subtract from 40.","tags":["baseball"],"a":13},{"id":"logB-3-008","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A square paper is folded in half, then folded in half again. When you open it, how many equal parts do you see?","why":"The first fold makes 2 parts. The second fold cuts each part in two, making 4.","hint":"Picture each fold cutting the parts in two.","tags":[],"opts":["3","8","4","2"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-3-009","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"If a shark is hungry, it hunts. If it hunts, it swims fast. A shark is hungry. What does it do?","why":"Hungry leads to hunting, and hunting leads to swimming fast. Follow the chain one link at a time.","hint":"Follow the 'if' chain step by step.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Hides in a cave","Stops moving","Rests on the sand","Swims fast"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-3-010","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: every square is also a rectangle.","why":"A rectangle has 4 square corners, and every square has them too. A square is a special rectangle.","hint":"What makes a shape a rectangle?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-3-011","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Think of a number. Double it, then add 1, and you get 11. What was the number?","why":"Work backward: 11 − 1 = 10, and half of 10 is 5.","hint":"Undo the steps in backward order.","tags":[],"a":5},{"id":"logB-3-012","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A bag holds 3 red, 2 blue and 5 green star stickers. You pick one without looking. Which color are you MOST likely to pick?","why":"Green has the most stickers, 5 out of 10, so it is the most likely pick.","hint":"Which color has the biggest group?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Green","Red","Blue","They are all equally likely"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-3-013","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Today is Friday. A comet will be easy to see in 9 days. What day of the week will that be?","why":"7 days after Friday is Friday again. Two more days make Sunday.","hint":"Take away a full week first.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Tuesday","Monday","Saturday","Sunday"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-3-014","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A baseball team has 3 caps and 2 jerseys. A player wears 1 cap and 1 jersey. How many different outfits can he make?","why":"Each of the 3 caps can go with 2 jerseys. 3 × 2 = 6.","hint":"Match one cap with each jersey.","tags":["baseball"],"a":6},{"id":"logB-3-015","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: All T. rex had sharp teeth. This animal has sharp teeth. So it must be a T. rex.","why":"Many animals have sharp teeth, like sharks and lions. Sharp teeth alone do not make a T. rex.","hint":"Could another animal have sharp teeth too?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-3-016","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A secret code says A = 1, B = 2, C = 3 and D = 4. Add up the letters in the word BAD. What do you get?","why":"B is 2, A is 1 and D is 4. 2 + 1 + 4 = 7.","hint":"Swap each letter for its number.","tags":[],"opts":["8","7","6","9"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-3-017","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Zoe has 2 quarters and 3 dimes. How many cents does she have in all?","why":"2 quarters make 50 cents and 3 dimes make 30 cents. 50 + 30 = 80.","hint":"Add the quarters, then the dimes.","tags":["money"],"a":80},{"id":"logB-3-018","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Skip a letter each time: A, C, E, G, ❓. Which letter comes next?","why":"The pattern jumps over one letter each time, so after G (skipping H) comes I.","hint":"Say the whole alphabet and skip one.","tags":[],"opts":["H","J","I","K"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-3-019","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Four rocket launches are set for 7:15, 6:40, 7:05 and 6:55. Put the times in order from earliest to latest.","why":"6:40 is before 6:55, and 7:05 comes before 7:15.","hint":"Compare the hours first, then the minutes.","tags":["space"],"opts":["6:40","6:55","7:05","7:15"]},{"id":"logB-3-020","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A tank has 12 fish. Half of them are red. Half of the red fish have stripes. How many red fish have stripes?","why":"Half of 12 is 6 red fish. Half of 6 is 3 striped red fish.","hint":"Cut the group in half two times.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-4-001","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A robot submarine dives 3 meters deeper every minute. How many meters deep is it after 9 minutes?","why":"3 meters a minute for 9 minutes is 3 × 9 = 27 meters.","hint":"Count by 3s nine times.","tags":["ocean"],"a":27},{"id":"logB-4-002","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A star-spotter counts twice as many stars each night: 3, 6, 12, 24, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is double the one before. Double 24 is 48. Adding 12 again would break the pattern.","hint":"Compare each number with the one before it.","tags":["space"],"opts":["27","48","30","36"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-4-003","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Kai, Liv and Mo play shortstop, pitcher and catcher. Kai is not the pitcher. Liv is not the pitcher and not the catcher. Who is the catcher?","why":"Liv must be the shortstop. Kai is not the pitcher, so Kai is the catcher and Mo pitches.","hint":"Place Liv first, then the others.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["We can't tell","Liv","Mo","Kai"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-4-004","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: every multiple of 10 is also a multiple of 5.","why":"10 is 5 + 5, so any number made of 10s is made of 5s too, like 30 = six 5s.","hint":"Try 20, 30 and 40.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-4-005","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four baby dinosaurs are measured. Put their lengths from shortest to longest.","why":"1 m is 100 cm. So 80 cm is shortest, then 100 cm, then 120 cm, then 200 cm.","hint":"Change every length to centimeters.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["80 cm","1 m","1 m 20 cm","2 m"]},{"id":"logB-4-006","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Three of these numbers are a number times itself (like 4 × 4 = 16). Which one is NOT?","why":"9 = 3 × 3, 25 = 5 × 5 and 36 = 6 × 6. No whole number times itself makes 30.","hint":"Test 5 × 5 and 6 × 6.","tags":[],"opts":["30","36","9","25"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-4-007","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"At a game, a hot dog costs 3 dollars and a drink costs 2 dollars. A family buys 4 hot dogs and 4 drinks. How many dollars is that?","why":"4 hot dogs cost 12 dollars and 4 drinks cost 8 dollars. 12 + 8 = 20.","hint":"Find the cost of each kind, then add.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":20},{"id":"logB-4-008","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"All sharks have gills. Some sea animals are sharks. Which sentence MUST be true?","why":"The sea animals that are sharks have gills. We do not know about the rest, so only 'some' is safe.","hint":"Follow the sharks through both sentences.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["All animals with gills are sharks","No sea animals have gills","Some sea animals have gills","All sea animals have gills"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-4-009","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"On day 1 a dig team has a pile of 4 fossils. Each day the pile grows by 3. How many fossils are in the pile on day 6?","why":"Day 1 is 4. Five more days add 5 × 3 = 15. 4 + 15 = 19.","hint":"Count how many jumps of 3 there are.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":19},{"id":"logB-4-010","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: if today is Monday, then 14 days from now is also a Monday.","why":"14 days is exactly 2 weeks, and the days of the week repeat every 7 days.","hint":"How many weeks are in 14 days?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-4-011","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A ship points north. It makes a quarter turn to the right, then another quarter turn to the right. Which way does it point now?","why":"A right turn from north points east. Another right turn from east points south.","hint":"Turn right twice, one step at a time.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["West","North","East","South"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-4-012","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which pair of numbers has a sum of 20 and a difference of 4?","why":"12 + 8 = 20 and 12 − 8 = 4. The other pairs add to 20 but their differences are 0, 6 and 2.","hint":"Check the sum AND the difference.","tags":[],"opts":["12 and 8","13 and 7","11 and 9","10 and 10"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-4-013","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A 12-foot rope is cut into 3-foot pieces. How many cuts does it take?","why":"12 ÷ 3 = 4 pieces. The last piece needs no cut, so 3 cuts make 4 pieces.","hint":"Draw the rope and mark the cuts.","tags":[],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-4-014","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: 'Some sharks are big' means that EVERY shark is big.","why":"'Some' only means a few. Many sharks, like little cat sharks, are quite small.","hint":"Does 'some' mean the same as 'every'?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-4-015","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A space machine changes 2 into 5, 4 into 9 and 6 into 13. What does it change 10 into?","why":"The rule is double the number and add 1. Double 10 is 20, and adding 1 makes 21.","hint":"Find what happens to 2 to get 5.","tags":["space"],"opts":["19","25","21","15"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-4-016","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Mia has twice as many shells as Jon. Together they have 18 shells. How many shells does Mia have?","why":"Jon has 6 and Mia has 12. That is 6 + 12 = 18, and 12 is double 6.","hint":"Try Jon having 6 shells.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["9","6","12","14"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-4-017","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"In baseball, a team's turn at bat ends after 3 outs. A team bats once in each of 9 innings. How many outs is that in all?","why":"3 outs in each of 9 innings makes 3 × 9 = 27 outs.","hint":"Multiply the outs per inning by the innings.","tags":["baseball"],"a":27,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-4-018","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Cal says, 'Two days ago it was Friday.' What day is it today?","why":"Friday plus one day is Saturday, and plus two days is Sunday.","hint":"Count two days forward from Friday.","tags":[],"opts":["Monday","Thursday","Saturday","Sunday"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-4-019","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four rocket engines burn for these times. Put them from shortest to longest.","why":"1 minute is 60 seconds and 2 minutes is 120 seconds, so the order is 60, 90, 100, 120.","hint":"Change every time to seconds.","tags":["space"],"opts":["1 minute","90 seconds","100 seconds","2 minutes"]},{"id":"logB-4-020","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Monday, Wednesday, Friday, ❓. Which day comes next in this pattern?","why":"The pattern skips one day each time. After Friday, skip Saturday and land on Sunday.","hint":"See how many days it jumps each time.","tags":[],"opts":["Saturday","Sunday","Tuesday","Thursday"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-5-001","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Dina spends half of her money on a toy shark. Then she spends one third of what is left on a snack. She has 6 dollars left. How many dollars did she start with?","why":"Work backward: 6 is two thirds of what remained, so 9 remained. That was half her money, so she started with 18.","hint":"Undo the snack, then undo the toy.","tags":["sharks","money"],"a":18},{"id":"logB-5-002","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"In a toy pool, all the crabs are red, and no red toy can float. Which sentence MUST be true?","why":"Crabs are red, and red toys cannot float. So no crab floats. Other toys might sink too.","hint":"Connect crabs to red, and red to floating.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["No crab in the pool floats","All the crabs float","Every toy that sinks is a crab","All red toys are crabs"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-5-003","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: All multiples of 4 are even, so all even numbers are multiples of 4.","why":"The first part is true. But 6 is even and is not a multiple of 4, so the second part fails.","hint":"Try the even number 6.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-5-004","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Rocket A launches every 4 days. Rocket B launches every 6 days. Both launch today. In how many days will they next launch on the same day?","why":"A launches on days 4, 8, 12 and B launches on days 6, 12. The first day in both lists is 12.","hint":"List each rocket's launch days.","tags":["space"],"a":12},{"id":"logB-5-005","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Five sharks line up by length. Tip is longer than Dot. Ray is longer than Tip. Sam is longer than Ray but shorter than Eve. Put them from shortest to longest.","why":"Dot is shorter than Tip, Tip than Ray, Ray than Sam, and Sam than Eve.","hint":"Chain the clues one after another.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Dot","Tip","Ray","Sam","Eve"]},{"id":"logB-5-006","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Look at this pattern: 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is double the last one plus 1. Double 31 is 62, and 62 + 1 is 63. Just doubling would give 62.","hint":"Look at how each number turns into the next.","tags":[],"opts":["62","47","64","63"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-5-007","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Three numbers in a row (like 7, 8, 9) add up to 33. What is the biggest of the three?","why":"The middle number is 33 ÷ 3 = 11. So the numbers are 10, 11 and 12, and the biggest is 12.","hint":"The middle number is the average.","tags":[],"a":12},{"id":"logB-5-008","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A cube is painted blue on its top and bottom and red on its 4 sides. Along how many of its 12 edges does a blue face touch a red face?","why":"The 4 top edges and 4 bottom edges each join blue to red. The 4 upright edges join red to red.","hint":"Count the top edges, then the bottom edges.","tags":[],"opts":["6","4","8","12"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-5-009","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: if the sum of two whole numbers is odd, one of them must be odd and the other even.","why":"Two odd numbers add to an even number, and so do two even numbers. Only an odd and an even make an odd.","hint":"Test a few pairs of numbers.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-5-010","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The 1st day of a month is a Tuesday. What day of the week is the 30th?","why":"The 29th is exactly 4 weeks after the 1st, so it is a Tuesday. The 30th is the next day, Wednesday.","hint":"Weeks repeat every 7 days. Jump to the 29th.","tags":[],"opts":["Monday","Tuesday","Thursday","Wednesday"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-5-011","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A pitcher throws 40 pitches, and 3/4 of them are strikes. How many are NOT strikes?","why":"One fourth are not strikes, and 40 ÷ 4 = 10.","hint":"What fraction are not strikes?","tags":["baseball"],"a":10},{"id":"logB-5-012","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Three sea turtles named A, B and C line up to cross the sand. How many different orders can they line up in?","why":"Any of 3 can go first, then either of 2 is next, then 1 is left: 3 × 2 × 1 = 6.","hint":"List them all: ABC, ACB, and so on.","tags":["ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"logB-5-013","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Pia, Quin and Rae sit in three seats in a row. Pia is NOT sitting next to Quin. Who sits in the middle?","why":"Whoever sits in the middle is next to both others. Pia and Quin cannot be next to each other, so Rae is in the middle.","hint":"The middle seat touches both other seats.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["We can't tell","Rae","Quin","Pia"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-5-014","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: If it is a baseball, it is round. This object is NOT round. So it is not a baseball.","why":"If every baseball is round, a thing that is not round cannot be a baseball.","hint":"Could a non-round object be a baseball?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-5-015","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Look at this pattern: 1, 10, 2, 20, 3, 30, ❓. What comes next?","why":"Two patterns are mixed: 1, 2, 3 on the odd spots and 10, 20, 30 on the even spots. Next is 4.","hint":"Look at every other number.","tags":[],"opts":["4","31","5","40"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-5-016","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A rocket launched at 11:50 PM and landed at 2:20 AM the next morning. How many minutes was the flight?","why":"11:50 to midnight is 10 minutes, and midnight to 2:20 is 140 minutes. 10 + 140 = 150.","hint":"Count to midnight first.","tags":["space"],"a":150},{"id":"logB-5-017","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"In a 3 by 3 magic square, every row adds up to 15. The top row is 8, ❓, 6. What number goes in the ❓ spot?","why":"8 + 6 = 14, and 15 − 14 = 1.","hint":"Add the two known numbers, then use 15.","tags":[],"opts":["3","4","2","1"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-5-018","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Exactly one of these three signs is FALSE. A: 'The fossil is in box 1.' B: 'The fossil is NOT in box 1.' C: 'The fossil is in box 3.' Where is it?","why":"A and B disagree, so one of them is already the false sign. That makes C true, so the fossil is in box 3.","hint":"Do any two signs disagree?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Box 1","We can't tell","Box 3","Box 2"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-5-019","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these baseball numbers in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"As decimals they are 0.3, 0.45, 0.5 and 0.6.","hint":"Change every number to a decimal.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["3/10","0.45","1/2","0.6"]},{"id":"logB-5-020","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A fish tank has 3 more guppies than tetras. There are 11 fish in all. How many guppies are there?","why":"If there were 3 fewer guppies, both kinds would match: 8 fish split in two is 4 each. So 4 tetras and 7 guppies.","hint":"Take away the extra 3 first.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["4","7","6","8"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-6-001","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball team has 13 players. Which sentence MUST be true?","why":"There are only 12 months, so 13 players cannot all have different birth months. A shared birthday is not guaranteed.","hint":"There are only 12 months to give out.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Two players share a birthday","Two players were born in the same month","Some month has nobody born in it","No two players share a birth month"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-6-002","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A fish weighs 10 pounds plus half of its own weight. How many pounds does it weigh?","why":"Half of its weight must be 10 pounds, so the whole fish is 20 pounds. Check: 10 + 10 = 20.","hint":"10 pounds is how much of the fish?","tags":["ocean"],"a":20},{"id":"logB-6-003","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"How many two-digit numbers have digits that add up to 5?","why":"They are 14, 23, 32, 41 and 50. Do not forget 50, but 05 is not a two-digit number.","hint":"List them in order, starting with 14.","tags":[],"opts":["3","6","4","5"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-6-004","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: With two fair dice, a total of 7 is more likely than a total of 12.","why":"A total of 12 needs 6 and 6 only. A total of 7 has six ways, like 1 and 6 or 2 and 5.","hint":"Count the ways to make each total.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-6-005","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Five dinosaurs line up. Tri is first. Bra is not last. Ank is not next to Bra. Ste is right in front of Rex. Put them in order, front to back.","why":"If Bra is second, Ank cannot be third or fourth. Ank goes last, and Ste then Rex fill the middle.","hint":"Test where Bra can stand.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Tri","Bra","Ste","Rex","Ank"]},{"id":"logB-6-006","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday was two days after Monday. What day is tomorrow?","why":"Two days after Monday is Wednesday, so yesterday was Wednesday. Today is Thursday, and tomorrow is Friday.","hint":"Find yesterday first, then step forward twice.","tags":[],"opts":["Thursday","Saturday","Friday","Wednesday"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-6-007","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"You have lots of dimes and nickels. In how many different ways can you make exactly 25 cents? Using just one kind of coin is allowed.","why":"Use 0 dimes and 5 nickels, or 1 dime and 3 nickels, or 2 dimes and 1 nickel. That is 3 ways.","hint":"Start with no dimes, then add one at a time.","tags":["money"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-6-008","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Two players take turns taking 1 or 2 stones from a pile of 10. Whoever takes the last stone wins. How many should the first player take to be sure of winning?","why":"Taking 1 leaves 9. Then whatever the other player takes, you take enough to leave 6, then 3, then 0.","hint":"Try to leave a multiple of 3.","tags":[],"opts":["1","It makes no difference","2","Neither number wins"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-6-009","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A pile of crates is stacked in layers. The bottom layer is 3 by 3, the middle layer is 2 by 2 and the top is 1 crate. How many crates are in the pile?","why":"The layers hold 9, 4 and 1 crates. 9 + 4 + 1 = 14.","hint":"Find each layer's number, then add.","tags":[],"opts":["15","14","13","12"],"a":1},{"id":"logB-6-010","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: Every shark is a fish, and no fish is a mammal. So no shark is a mammal.","why":"Sharks belong inside the fish group, and the fish group has no mammals. So sharks are not mammals.","hint":"Think of circles inside circles.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-6-011","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"How many months of the year have at least 28 days?","why":"February has 28 days and every other month has 30 or 31, so all 12 months have at least 28.","hint":"The words are 'at least'.","tags":[],"opts":["2","11","1","12"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"logB-6-012","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A bucket holds more than 1 baseball. Counting the balls by 2s, 3s, 4s, 5s or 6s always leaves exactly 1 ball over. What is the smallest possible number?","why":"The smallest number that 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 all divide into is 60. One more is 61.","hint":"What numbers do 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 all divide?","tags":["baseball"],"a":61},{"id":"logB-6-013","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Eight astronauts sit evenly around a round table. They are numbered 1 to 8 in order. Which number sits directly across from number 3?","why":"Across the table is halfway around, which is 4 seats. 3 + 4 = 7.","hint":"Halfway around is half of 8 seats.","tags":["space"],"opts":["7","6","8","5"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-6-014","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Look at this pattern: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is the sum of the two before it. 8 + 13 = 21.","hint":"Add the last two numbers.","tags":[],"opts":["24","26","21","18"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-6-015","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"At a ballpark, some people and some dogs have 10 heads and 28 legs in all. Dogs have 4 legs and people have 2. How many dogs are there?","why":"If all 10 had 2 legs, that would be 20 legs. The 8 extra legs come from dogs with 2 extra each, so 4 dogs.","hint":"Pretend everyone has 2 legs first.","tags":["baseball","animals"],"a":4},{"id":"logB-6-016","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 is bigger than 3 × 3 × 3.","why":"2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 is 32 and 3 × 3 × 3 is 27, so 32 is the bigger one.","hint":"Work out both products.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"logB-6-017","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A fish tank is half full. After 6 buckets of water are poured in, it is three quarters full. How many buckets fill an empty tank?","why":"Six buckets fill one quarter of the tank. A whole tank is 4 quarters, and 4 × 6 = 24 buckets.","hint":"What fraction did 6 buckets add?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["30","12","18","24"],"a":3},{"id":"logB-6-018","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"🦈 + 🦈 + 🐙 = 17 and 🦈 + 🐙 + 🐙 = 13. What is 🦈 + 🐙?","why":"Add both lines: 3 sharks and 3 octopuses make 30. One of each is a third of that, 10.","hint":"Add the two lines together.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"a":10},{"id":"logB-6-019","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"What is the most pieces you can cut a round pizza into with 3 straight cuts?","why":"Three cuts that all cross each other inside the pizza make 7 pieces. Cuts through one center point give only 6.","hint":"Make the cuts cross in different places.","tags":["food"],"opts":["6","8","7","9"],"a":2},{"id":"logB-6-020","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these fractions in order from least to greatest.","why":"As decimals they are about 0.58, 0.6, 0.625 and 0.67.","hint":"Change each fraction to a decimal.","tags":[],"opts":["7/12","3/5","5/8","2/3"]},{"id":"reaB-1-001","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Leo wears his red cap to every baseball game. What does Leo wear to baseball games?","why":"The story says Leo wears a red cap. The other things are not in the story.","hint":"Find the clothing word in the first sentence.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["A red cap","A blue glove","A green coat"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-1-002","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A hammerhead shark has a wide, flat head. True or false: its head is shaped like a round ball.","why":"The text says wide and flat, which is not round like a ball.","hint":"Check the words wide and flat.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-1-003","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Ana's puppy ran to the door and wagged its tail when she got home. How did the puppy feel?","why":"A wagging tail and running to the door show joy. Sleepy dogs stay still.","hint":"What do wagging tails usually show?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Angry","Happy","Sleepy"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-1-004","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the beach story in order.","why":"The bucket has to be filled and flipped before there is a tower to wash away.","hint":"What must happen before the tower can stand?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Dan filled a bucket with wet sand.","He flipped the bucket over.","A little tower stood on the beach.","A wave washed it away."]},{"id":"reaB-1-005","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The shark swam fast to catch a fish. Why did the shark swim fast?","why":"The story gives the reason right in the first sentence: to catch a fish.","hint":"Look for the word 'to' in the sentence.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["To get warm","To hide in the sand","To catch a fish"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-1-006","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sea otters hold hands while they sleep, so they do not drift apart. What is this mostly about?","why":"Every part of the sentence is about otters holding hands as they sleep. Food and nests are not mentioned.","hint":"Ask what most of the sentence tells about.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["How otters find food","Otters holding hands","Where otters build nests"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-1-007","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Read: Three whales swam by. Then two more whales joined them. How many whales are there now?","why":"Three whales plus two more whales makes five whales.","hint":"Add the whales together.","tags":["ocean"],"a":5},{"id":"reaB-1-008","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A book has a title on its cover. What does the title tell you?","why":"The title is the name of the book. It does not tell you the ending or the length.","hint":"Think about what you call a book.","tags":[],"opts":["The name of the book","Where the book ends","How long the book is"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-1-009","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sam grabbed his glove and ran to the field. His team was waiting. What will Sam most likely do next?","why":"A glove, a field and a waiting team all point to a baseball game.","hint":"Use the clues about the glove and the field.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Bake a cake","Go to sleep","Play baseball"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-1-010","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon does not make its own light. It shines because sunlight bounces off it. True or false: moonlight is really sunlight bouncing off the Moon.","why":"The Moon only reflects sunlight, a bit like a mirror does.","hint":"Reread the second sentence.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-1-011","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Waves rolled onto the sand and gulls called overhead. Dan dug with a shovel. Where is Dan?","why":"Waves, sand and gulls are all beach clues.","hint":"Which clues name sand and waves?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["In a snowy town","At the beach","In a quiet forest"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-1-012","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these ocean animals in ABC order, first letter first.","why":"The first letters c, f, s and w come in that order in the alphabet.","hint":"Say the alphabet and find each first letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["crab","fish","shark","whale"]},{"id":"reaB-1-013","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Mo found his lost baseball under the bed. He jumped up and shouted, 'Yes!' How did Mo feel?","why":"Jumping up and shouting 'Yes!' shows excitement, not fear or sadness.","hint":"How do people act when they find something good?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Sad","Scared","Excited"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-1-014","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A cat has fur. A fish has scales. What does a fish have that a cat does not?","why":"The text says fish have scales and cats have fur. A cat does not have scales.","hint":"Compare the two sentences.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Scales","Fur","Four legs"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-1-015","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Read: Kai had 4 baseball cards. Dee gave him 3 more. How many cards does Kai have now?","why":"Kai had 4 cards and got 3 more, so 4 plus 3 equals 7.","hint":"Add the cards Dee gave.","tags":["baseball"],"a":7},{"id":"reaB-1-016","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A book shows how to make a paper boat, step by step. Why did the author write it?","why":"Step-by-step directions are written to teach you how to do something.","hint":"What do step-by-step directions help you do?","tags":[],"opts":["To tell a funny story about pirates","To make readers feel scared","To show how to make a boat"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-1-017","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A T. rex had tiny arms but strong legs and a huge head. Which part of a T. rex was tiny?","why":"The text says tiny arms. The legs were strong and the head was huge.","hint":"Find the word tiny.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Its legs","Its arms","Its head"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-1-018","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A glossary is a list of words and what they mean. True or false: a glossary can help you learn a new word.","why":"A glossary lists words with their meanings, so it helps with new words.","hint":"Think about what the list tells you.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-1-019","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A sea star has no brain and no blood. It moves sea water through its body instead. Which of these does a sea star NOT have?","why":"The text says no brain and no blood. Sea stars do have a mouth and arms.","hint":"Find the word no in the first sentence.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["A brain","A mouth","Arms"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-1-020","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Ruby looked through her telescope at the night sky. She saw bright dots far away. What was Ruby looking at?","why":"A telescope at night shows faraway bright dots, which are stars.","hint":"Where was she looking, and how far away were the dots?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Fireflies nearby","Fish in a pond","Stars"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-2-001","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The tiny hermit crab was timid. When a big wave came, it hid inside its shell. What does timid mean?","why":"The crab hid when the wave came, which shows it is shy and easily scared.","hint":"What does hiding tell you about the crab?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Hungry","Shy","Silly"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-002","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Jake's team was behind by one run. He took a deep breath, gripped the bat and watched the pitcher closely. What is Jake trying to do?","why":"Gripping the bat and watching the pitcher means he is getting ready to hit.","hint":"Think about why a batter watches the pitcher.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Hit the ball","Leave the game","Fix the base"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-2-003","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have skeletons made of cartilage, not bone. Cartilage is the soft, bendy stuff in your ears. True or false: a shark's skeleton is made of cartilage.","why":"Sharks have no bones. Their cartilage skeleton bends, like the tip of your ear.","hint":"Reread the first sentence.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-2-004","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the butterfly's life story in order.","why":"The egg comes first, and the butterfly comes out only after the chrysalis stage.","hint":"What is the very first thing in a butterfly's life?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A tiny egg sits on a leaf.","A caterpillar hatches and eats leaves.","The caterpillar makes a chrysalis.","A butterfly comes out."]},{"id":"reaB-2-005","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Seahorse dads carry the eggs in a pouch on their bellies. The babies pop out when they are ready. What is the main idea?","why":"Both sentences are about the dad carrying eggs. Land and seaweed are never mentioned.","hint":"What do both sentences talk about?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["Seahorses lay eggs on dry land","Baby seahorses eat lots of seaweed","Seahorse dads carry eggs"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-2-006","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Tim's team won the championship. He hugged his teammates and could not stop smiling. How did Tim feel?","why":"Hugging and smiling after a win show Tim felt thrilled, which means very happy.","hint":"Look at what Tim does with his face.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Worried","Thrilled","Annoyed"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-007","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"Read: A baseball game has 9 innings. The team has played 4 innings. How many innings are left?","why":"9 innings minus 4 played innings leaves 5 innings.","hint":"Subtract the played innings from the total.","tags":["baseball"],"a":5},{"id":"reaB-2-008","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Because gravity pulls everything toward Earth, a baseball hit high into the air comes back down. What brings the baseball down?","why":"Gravity pulls objects toward Earth. Wind can move a ball sideways but does not pull it down.","hint":"What force does the first part of the sentence name?","tags":["baseball","space"],"opts":["Gravity","Magnets","The wind"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-2-009","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of a book lists the chapters and their page numbers?","why":"The table of contents shows chapters and pages. A glossary explains words instead.","hint":"It is found near the front of the book.","tags":[],"opts":["The glossary at the back","The cover and spine","The table of contents"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-2-010","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The plant on the windowsill drooped because nobody watered it. True or false: the plant drooped because it got too much water.","why":"The text says nobody watered it, so it drooped from too little water.","hint":"Find the word because and read what follows.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-011","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The astronaut floated past the window, and her pencil drifted in the air beside her. Where is she most likely?","why":"Objects float inside a spacecraft in orbit instead of falling as they would on Earth.","hint":"On Earth, a pencil would fall.","tags":["space"],"opts":["At a ballpark","On a spaceship","In a swimming pool"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-012","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The dinosaur's footprint was enormous. A whole family could stand inside it. What does enormous mean?","why":"If a whole family fits in the footprint, it must be very large.","hint":"How big must a footprint be for a family to fit?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Very old","Very flat","Very large"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-2-013","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the baseball play in order.","why":"The pitch starts the play, and the crowd claps only after the catch.","hint":"What has to happen before anyone can catch the ball?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The pitcher threw the ball.","The batter hit a line drive.","The shortstop caught it.","The crowd clapped for the out."]},{"id":"reaB-2-014","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A writer tells a funny story about a dog who wears sunglasses. Why did the writer most likely write it?","why":"Funny stories are written to entertain. Directions and facts are not the goal here.","hint":"Is the story meant to teach or to be fun?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["To make readers smile","To give directions to readers","To list facts about dogs"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-2-015","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Whale sharks are huge, but they eat tiny plankton. They swim with their mouths open and filter food from the water. What do whale sharks eat?","why":"Size can fool you. The text says this huge shark eats tiny plankton.","hint":"Find the food named in the first sentence.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Large seals and fish","Tiny plankton","Big squid and octopus"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-016","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Jellyfish have no bones, no brain and no heart. True or false: a jellyfish has a heart.","why":"Jellyfish are made mostly of water and have no heart, brain or bones.","hint":"Look for the word no in the passage.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-2-017","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Orbit means to travel around something. The Moon orbits Earth. What does the Moon do?","why":"Orbit means travel around, so the Moon travels around Earth.","hint":"Use the meaning the passage gives for orbit.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Sits still beside Earth","Flies away from Earth","Travels around Earth"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-2-018","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"Read: A shark lost 2 teeth while eating lunch and 4 more while eating dinner. New teeth grew in each time. How many teeth did it lose in all?","why":"The shark lost 2 and then 4 more, so 2 plus 4 equals 6. Regrowing does not change the count lost.","hint":"Add only the teeth that fell out.","tags":["sharks"],"a":6},{"id":"reaB-2-019","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A dictionary page has the guide words dog and duck. Which word could be found on that page?","why":"Door comes after dog and before duck in ABC order, so it fits between the guide words.","hint":"Compare the first letters, then the second letters.","tags":[],"opts":["Cat","Door","Fish"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-2-020","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A frog has smooth, wet skin. A toad has dry, bumpy skin. How is a toad's skin different from a frog's?","why":"The toad's skin is dry and bumpy, while the frog's is smooth and wet.","hint":"Compare the two skin descriptions.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["It is dry and bumpy","It is smooth and wet","It is fuzzy and warm"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-001","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The explorer was parched after hours in the hot desert. She gulped down a whole bottle of water. What does parched mean?","why":"Gulping a whole bottle of water is a clue: parched means very thirsty.","hint":"Why would someone gulp water?","tags":[],"opts":["Very sleepy","Very cold","Very thirsty","Very lost"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-3-002","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The coach looked at the dark clouds, then at the scoreboard. 'Let's finish quickly before the rain,' she said. What is she worried about?","why":"The dark clouds and the word rain show she fears the weather will stop the game.","hint":"Connect the clouds to what the coach said.","tags":["baseball","weather"],"opts":["Rain stopping the game","Losing a bat before the game","A school bus running late","A broken strap on a glove"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-003","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A caption is the short line of words under a photograph or drawing. True or false: a caption explains what the picture shows.","why":"Captions give details about a picture, like who or what is in it.","hint":"Think about the words that sit next to a photo.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-004","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Octopuses squirt dark ink when frightened. The ink makes a cloud in the water, and the octopus swims away. Why does an octopus squirt ink?","why":"The ink cloud hides the octopus so it can get away from a hungry predator.","hint":"What happens after the cloud forms?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["To paint the rocks","To signal its friends","To catch its lunch","To escape danger"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-3-005","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A comet is a ball of ice and dust. Near the Sun it heats up and grows a glowing tail. Which sentence tells the main idea?","why":"Both sentences tell how a comet grows a tail near the Sun. Comets hold ice and dust, not just rock.","hint":"Which choice covers both sentences?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Comets are made of nothing but rock","Comets grow tails near the Sun","Comets stay far away from the Sun","Comets circle the Moon"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-3-006","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Read: Rex has 5 books about sharks and 4 books about dinosaurs. He gives 3 books away. How many books does Rex have left?","why":"5 plus 4 makes 9 books. After giving 3 away, 9 minus 3 leaves 6.","hint":"Add first, then subtract.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs"],"a":6},{"id":"reaB-3-007","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Every day after practice, Lena stays late to pick up balls, stack the bats and sweep the dugout, even when nobody asks. Which word best describes Lena?","why":"Doing extra jobs without being asked shows she is helpful, not lazy.","hint":"What do her actions show about her?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Lazy","Bossy","Helpful","Forgetful"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-3-008","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"I felt my heart pound as I walked to the mound to pitch. Who is telling this story?","why":"The word I shows the storyteller is the person in the story, the pitcher.","hint":"Which word shows who is speaking?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The pitcher, using I","The cheering crowd","The umpire","A reporter watching from far away"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-009","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put the water cycle in order.","why":"Water must warm and rise before it can cool into clouds and fall.","hint":"Rain cannot fall until clouds have formed.","tags":["ocean","weather"],"opts":["The Sun warms water in the ocean.","Water vapor rises into the sky.","The vapor cools and forms clouds.","Rain falls back to the ground."]},{"id":"reaB-3-010","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Maya tiptoed into the kitchen at midnight and opened the fridge as quietly as she could. Why was she tiptoeing?","why":"Midnight and 'as quietly as she could' show she did not want to wake anyone.","hint":"Why do people move quietly at midnight?","tags":[],"opts":["She was late for school","She wanted to stay quiet","She had lost her shoes","She was practicing ballet"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-3-011","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks and rays both have skeletons made of cartilage. A shark's body is shaped like a torpedo, but a ray's body is flat. What do sharks and rays share?","why":"The passage says both have cartilage skeletons. Only rays have flat bodies.","hint":"Find the thing the first sentence says they share.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Flat bodies","Thick bones","Lungs for breathing air","Cartilage skeletons"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-3-012","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Ice cubes melt faster in sunshine because the Sun gives off heat. True or false: ice cubes melt faster in a cool, shady spot than in the sunshine.","why":"The Sun's heat melts ice faster, so a shady spot slows the melting.","hint":"Which place has more heat from the Sun?","tags":["weather"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-3-013","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence would an author write to persuade you?","why":"Should tells you what to do. The other sentences only give facts.","hint":"Which sentence tries to change your mind?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["You should visit an aquarium.","An aquarium has many large tanks.","Sea water is very salty.","Many fish live in the tanks."],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-014","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The bats flew out of the cave at dusk, just as the sky was turning dark. What does dusk mean?","why":"The sky was turning dark, so dusk is the time when day ends and evening begins.","hint":"When does the sky start to turn dark?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Early morning","Noon","Early evening","Midnight"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-3-015","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Read: The team scored 2 runs in the first inning, none in the second and 3 runs in the third. How many runs did it score in the first three innings?","why":"2 plus 0 plus 3 equals 5 runs.","hint":"Remember that none means zero.","tags":["baseball"],"a":5},{"id":"reaB-3-016","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The wind bent the palm trees almost flat, and waves crashed over the pier. What kind of weather was it?","why":"Bending trees and crashing waves are signs of strong wind, like a storm.","hint":"What would make trees bend and waves crash?","tags":["weather","ocean"],"opts":["A calm morning","A fierce storm","A foggy night","A light drizzle"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-3-017","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Unlike toothy sharks, baleen whales have no teeth. They filter tiny food from the water with bristly plates in their mouths. What do baleen whales use instead of teeth?","why":"The passage says bristly plates in their mouths strain food from the water.","hint":"Find the word instead in the question.","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["Hard beaks","Long tusks","Strong claws","Mouth plates"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-3-018","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put Ravi's pitching routine in order.","why":"Signal words like first, next, then and finally show the order.","hint":"Look for the signal words.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["First, Ravi stretched his arms.","Next, he threw warm-up pitches.","Then he faced the first batter.","Finally, he struck the batter out."]},{"id":"reaB-3-019","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"At first, Omar was afraid to put his face in the water. After weeks of practice, he swam across the pool and grinned. How did Omar change?","why":"He went from afraid at first to grinning after he succeeded.","hint":"Compare how he acts at the start and the end.","tags":[],"opts":["From fearful to proud","From sleepy to angry","From bored to scared","From happy to sad"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-3-020","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Penguins huddle close together in cold winds. They take turns standing on the outside. That way no penguin stays in the coldest spot for long. What is the main idea?","why":"All the sentences describe penguins taking turns in the cold. Nothing mentions homes or caves.","hint":"What do all three sentences describe?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Penguins build homes of ice","Penguins swim in big groups","Penguins take turns","Penguins hide in deep caves"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-4-001","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence contains a simile?","why":"A simile compares two things using like or as. 'Like lightning' compares the fastball to lightning.","hint":"Look for the word like or as.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["His fastball moved like lightning.","The pitcher threw the ball very hard.","The crowd roared loudly.","The ball sailed over the fence."],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-4-002","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The ocean waves whispered secrets to the sleepy shore. What is the author doing?","why":"Waves cannot whisper or tell secrets. Giving human actions to nature is personification.","hint":"Can waves really whisper?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Using the word like or as","Giving waves human actions","Telling a plain fact about water","Giving an order to the reader"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-4-003","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"When the ocean gets too warm, coral pushes out the tiny algae living inside it, and the coral turns white. True or false: the algae are what turn the coral white.","why":"Losing the algae turns the coral white. The algae give coral its color.","hint":"Read the order of events carefully.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-4-004","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The new student told a joke to break the ice with her classmates. What does break the ice mean?","why":"Breaking the ice means making an awkward start feel friendly and comfortable.","hint":"Think about why someone tells a joke to new classmates.","tags":[],"opts":["Crack frozen water","End an argument","Help people relax","Win a contest"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-4-005","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Mia practiced her curveball every day. She missed the strike zone for weeks but kept trying. By spring, she struck out three batters. What lesson does the story teach?","why":"Her struggles turned into success because she kept practicing. That is the lesson of the story.","hint":"Ask what changed from the start to the end.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Curveballs are very easy","Spring is the best season","Teams should never lose","Practice leads to progress"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-4-006","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Read: A pitcher threw 15 pitches in the first inning, 12 in the second and 18 in the third. How many pitches did he throw in all?","why":"15 plus 12 makes 27, and 27 plus 18 makes 45.","hint":"Add the three numbers one at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"a":45},{"id":"reaB-4-007","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Lila read about space for hours. Every night she watched the stars and drew maps of the planets. Which sentence is best supported by these clues?","why":"Reading, stargazing and drawing planets all show strong interest in space. Nothing hints at fear.","hint":"Which choice matches ALL of her actions?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Lila loves space.","Lila is afraid of the dark.","Lila wants to be a baker.","Lila dislikes science."],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-4-008","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the bat's hunting steps in order.","why":"Echoes come after the squeak, and the bat can only swoop once it hears the echo.","hint":"What does the bat need before it can find the moth?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["The bat makes a high squeak.","The sound hits a moth and bounces back.","The bat hears the echo.","The bat swoops to catch the moth."]},{"id":"reaB-4-009","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Tiny plant-like plankton need sunlight to grow, so they float near the ocean's surface. What would happen to them in the dark deep sea?","why":"Without sunlight they cannot make food, so they could not grow well. That is why they stay near the top.","hint":"What do they need to grow?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["They would grow faster","They could not grow well","They would grow bigger","They would glow brighter"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-4-010","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In the sentence 'Many sharks hunt fish; however, whale sharks eat plankton,' what does the word however signal?","why":"However shows a difference: many sharks hunt fish, but whale sharks eat plankton.","hint":"Does the second part match or differ from the first?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["A cause","A time order","A contrast","A list"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-4-011","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon has no wind or rain to wipe away footprints, so astronaut boot prints can last a very long time. True or false: footprints on the Moon disappear quickly because of strong winds.","why":"The Moon has no wind, so footprints stay. That is why they can last a very long time.","hint":"Check what the text says about wind.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-4-012","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The reef was teeming with fish. They darted through every crack and crowded around every coral. What does teeming mean?","why":"Fish in every crack and crowding around coral shows the reef was packed with life.","hint":"What do darting and crowding tell you?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Empty and quiet","Covered in ice","Hidden from view","Packed with life"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-4-013","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In a science book, a word printed in bold type usually means what?","why":"Authors use bold type to point out important words, which are often explained nearby.","hint":"Why would an author make a word stand out?","tags":[],"opts":["It is a key word to learn","It is a spelling error","It is the author's name","It is the end of a chapter"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-4-014","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Read: A shark swam 4 miles before lunch. After lunch it swam twice as far. How many miles did it swim all day?","why":"Twice as far as 4 miles is 8 miles. Add the 4 miles from the morning to get 12.","hint":"Find the afternoon distance first.","tags":["sharks"],"a":12},{"id":"reaB-4-015","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"After striking out, Kai slammed down his glove and stomped to the dugout. A minute later he picked it up and cheered for the next batter. What does this show about Kai?","why":"Kai got upset but calmed down fast and cheered for a teammate. That shows he can recover.","hint":"Compare Kai's actions before and after the minute passed.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["He wants to quit baseball","He bounces back quickly","He never cared about winning","He blames the umpire"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-4-016","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Mars is dry, dusty and cold. Venus is wrapped in thick clouds and is extremely hot. How are the two planets different?","why":"Mars is described as cold and Venus as extremely hot. The other choices do not match the text.","hint":"Match each planet to its temperature word.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mars is hot and Venus is cold","Both are covered with oceans","Mars is cold and Venus is hot","Both are very hot"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-4-017","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the steps of how a pearl forms in order.","why":"The grain comes first, and the layers must build up before the pearl is finished.","hint":"What starts the whole process?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A tiny grain slips inside an oyster.","The oyster coats the grain with smooth layers.","Layer after layer builds up over time.","A shiny pearl is ready to be found."]},{"id":"reaB-4-018","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Pip called the game a thrilling comeback. Cam, who plays for the other team, called it a heartbreaking loss. Why do they feel so differently?","why":"A comeback for one team is a loss for the other, so their points of view differ.","hint":"Which team does each person root for?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["One of them missed the game","One misunderstood the score","They like different sports","They were on opposite teams"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-4-019","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Herbivores eat plants and carnivores eat meat. The prefix omni- means 'all.' What do omnivores eat?","why":"Omni- means all, so omnivores eat all kinds of food, both plants and meat.","hint":"What does 'all' tell you about their food?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Plants and meat","Only seeds and nuts","Only fish and krill","Only bugs and worms"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-4-020","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A summary tells only the most important ideas of a text in a few sentences. True or false: a good summary includes every tiny detail.","why":"A summary keeps the big ideas and leaves out small details.","hint":"Think about how short a summary should be.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-5-001","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The library was a quiet garden where stories grew. What does this metaphor suggest about the library?","why":"A garden is peaceful and things grow there, so the library is calm and ideas grow. It is not a real garden.","hint":"What do gardens and libraries have in common?","tags":[],"opts":["It grows real vegetables","It is calm and inspiring","It is loud and busy","It is outside"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-5-002","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Ruben said his sister was too small to play. When the ball rolled under a fence, only she fit through to get it. Ruben grinned and handed her a glove. What is the theme?","why":"The small sister helped in a way nobody else could. The theme is about value in everyone.","hint":"Ask what Ruben learned by the end.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Fences keep games safe","Big kids always win","Everyone can help","Games should be short"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-5-003","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Read: A museum shows 3 dinosaur skeletons in each of its 4 rooms. It also shows 5 skeletons in the lobby. How many skeletons are on display?","why":"3 skeletons in each of 4 rooms is 12. Add the 5 in the lobby to get 17.","hint":"Multiply first, then add the lobby.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":17},{"id":"reaB-5-004","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A scientist left a cup of sea water in the sun for three days. The cup was almost empty, with white crystals on the bottom. What happened?","why":"Water evaporates into the air, but salt does not. It is left behind as crystals.","hint":"Which part of sea water can float away as vapor?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["It evaporated and left salt behind","It froze solid and cracked the cup","It turned into salt crystals","It soaked through the table slowly"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-5-005","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Plastic bags were tangling sea turtles' flippers. A beach town asked shoppers to use cloth bags instead, and soon fewer turtles were hurt. Which text structure does this passage use?","why":"It names a problem (tangled turtles) and a solution (cloth bags).","hint":"Find the trouble, then find the fix.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Compare and contrast","Sequence of steps","Description of a place","Problem and solution"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-5-006","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Octopuses can squeeze through tiny gaps. Their bodies have no bones, so only the hard beak limits how small a gap they can fit through. True or false: the second sentence explains the first.","why":"The second sentence gives the reason octopuses fit through tiny gaps: no bones.","hint":"Ask whether the second sentence tells why.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-5-007","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Writer A says the new ballpark has narrow seats and long lines. Writer B says the seats are close to the action and the snacks arrive fast. What is the best conclusion?","why":"A lists problems and B lists good things, so they have different opinions.","hint":"Is each writer positive or negative?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["They disagree about it","Both writers dislike it","Both writers love it","Only one saw the game"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-5-008","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A paragraph explains how bats use sound to find food. Which sentence does NOT belong?","why":"Where bats sleep is a different topic than how they find food with sound.","hint":"Which sentence is off topic?","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Bats send out high squeaks.","Echoes tell bats where insects are.","Some bats sleep in caves.","Bats listen for the returning sound."],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-5-009","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these feelings in order from mildest to most intense.","why":"Each word is stronger than the one before it, ending with the most intense, furious.","hint":"Which word fits a tiny problem, and which a huge one?","tags":[],"opts":["bothered","annoyed","angry","furious"]},{"id":"reaB-5-010","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Dani told her team she was not nervous. But her foot tapped, she checked her watch every minute and she kept retying her cleats. What can the reader infer?","why":"Her actions say the opposite of her words, so she is likely hiding nervousness.","hint":"Compare what she says with what she does.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["She is bored and sleepy","She is hiding her nerves","She is angry at her cleats","She is early for practice"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-5-011","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Warm seas let plankton bloom. The plankton drew small fish, and the small fish drew seabirds. What directly drew the seabirds?","why":"The passage says the small fish drew the seabirds. Warm water and plankton were earlier links.","hint":"Find the link right before the seabirds.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Warm sea water","Blooming plankton","Sunny weather","Small fish"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-5-012","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Baby sea turtles crawl toward the brightest horizon, which is normally the moonlit sea. Bright lights on land can confuse them. True or false: bright lights near a beach always help hatchlings reach the sea.","why":"Lights on land can pull hatchlings the wrong way, away from the sea.","hint":"Read the second sentence closely.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-5-013","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The paleontologist was meticulous. She brushed each tiny grain of dust off the bone, one grain at a time. What does meticulous mean?","why":"Brushing off one grain at a time shows she is extremely careful.","hint":"What does going one grain at a time show?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Quick and a bit rough","Forgetful","Extremely careful","Easily bored"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-5-014","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Hummingbirds beat their wings so fast that they hover. They sip nectar with long tongues. They burn so much energy that they must eat often. Which is the best summary?","why":"A good summary covers the key ideas: hovering, sipping nectar and eating often.","hint":"Pick the choice that uses ideas from all three sentences.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Hummingbirds hover and eat often","Hummingbirds are the tiniest birds","Flowers grow from nectar","Wings are made of petals"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-5-015","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Read: A poem has 3 stanzas. Each stanza has 4 lines, and every line has 6 words. How many words are in the poem?","why":"3 stanzas of 4 lines is 12 lines. 12 lines of 6 words each is 72 words.","hint":"First find the total number of lines.","tags":[],"a":72},{"id":"reaB-5-016","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The coach called the runner's plan 'bold.' The umpire called the same plan 'reckless.' How do the two words differ?","why":"Bold praises daring, while reckless warns that it is careless. The feelings are different.","hint":"Think of whether each word sounds like praise or worry.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["They mean exactly the same thing","Bold means very slow","Reckless means very safe","Bold admires; reckless warns"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-5-017","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"At noon the boat sat on dry sand. Six hours later it floated at the dock. True or false: the tide probably came in.","why":"A rising tide fills the shore with water, lifting boats that were sitting on dry sand.","hint":"What would lift a boat off dry sand?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-5-018","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Text A: The Sun gives Earth light and heat. Text B: Without the Sun's heat, Earth's oceans would freeze. What idea do both texts share?","why":"Both texts show things on Earth need the Sun's light and heat.","hint":"Look for what both texts say about the Sun.","tags":["space","ocean"],"opts":["The Sun is very small","Earth needs the Sun","Oceans give off light","Stars are cold"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-5-019","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"An anemone's stinging tentacles keep a clownfish safe. The clownfish cleans the anemone and chases away fish that nibble it. How would you describe this partnership?","why":"Each helps the other: the anemone protects, and the clownfish cleans and guards.","hint":"Ask what each one gets.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Only the anemone benefits","Only the clownfish benefits","Both partners benefit","Neither partner benefits"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-5-020","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The old pitcher's fastball, once feared by every hitter, now barely reached the plate. True or false: the pitcher has probably lost speed over the years.","why":"Once feared and now barely reaching the plate suggest he used to throw faster.","hint":"Compare then and now.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-6-001","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which situation is ironic?","why":"Irony is the opposite of what you expect. A swim coach is expected to know how to swim.","hint":"Which choice is the opposite of what you would expect?","tags":[],"opts":["A swim coach who loves pizza","A swim coach who wears a watch","A swim coach who cannot swim","A swim coach who is very tall"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-6-002","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Claim: sharks help keep the ocean healthy. Which fact best supports this claim?","why":"Evidence must connect to the claim. Only the first fact explains how sharks help the ocean's health.","hint":"Which fact is about HEALTH, not looks or speed?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["They eat weak fish, so others thrive","Their teeth grow back in many rows","Some sharks are gray and some are blue","Many sharks can swim at high speed"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-6-003","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Read: A research boat sailed 3 days to a reef, stayed twice as many days as it sailed, then sailed home in 3 days. How many days did the whole trip take?","why":"Twice 3 days is 6 days at the reef. The trip was 3 plus 6 plus 3, which is 12 days.","hint":"Find the days at the reef first.","tags":["ocean"],"a":12},{"id":"reaB-6-004","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Had the tide not turned, the stranded whale calf would have stayed trapped on the sand. What happened?","why":"Had the tide not turned means it did turn. So the calf did not stay trapped.","hint":"Say the sentence again as what really happened.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["The tide never came back in","It was never stranded at all","It stayed trapped on the sand","It was freed by the tide"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-6-005","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The forecast was dire, yet the captain stayed sanguine and smiled at her crew. What does sanguine most likely mean?","why":"The word yet signals a contrast with dire, so she stayed hopeful and smiling.","hint":"What does the word yet tell you?","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Furious","Hopeful","Confused","Exhausted"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-6-006","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists say sharks were swimming in the oceans over 400 million years ago, before the first forests grew on land. True or false: sharks were around before the first forests.","why":"The passage says sharks swam before forests grew. Sharks are very ancient animals.","hint":"Compare the two time clues in the sentence.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-6-007","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"When the runner wanted to risk stealing second, the coach said, 'A bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.' What advice is she giving?","why":"This saying means a sure thing is worth more than a bigger chance you might lose.","hint":"What is the coach telling the runner not to risk?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Always steal every base","Run faster next time","Do not risk a sure thing","Wait for a better pitch"],"a":2},{"id":"reaB-6-008","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Report A says the home team 'squeaked past' its rival. Report B says it 'crushed' its rival. Which report suggests a close game?","why":"Squeaked past means barely won. Crushed means won by a lot.","hint":"Which word means a tiny margin?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Report A","Report B","Both reports","Neither report"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-6-009","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these sentences in order to make a clear paragraph.","why":"A topic sentence opens, examples follow, and a sentence beginning with So wraps it up.","hint":"Which sentence introduces the topic and which one concludes?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["Sea otters are clever tool users.","For example, they crack clams open with rocks.","Some even keep a favorite rock in a skin pocket.","So tool use is not only for people."],"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-6-010","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Most of an octopus's nerve cells are in its arms, not its head. Which inference is best supported?","why":"With most nerve cells in the arms, each arm can handle some tasks itself. But the head still has a brain.","hint":"Where would you expect a lot of nerve cells to help?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["Its head controls everything","It has eight equal brains","Its arms feel nothing","Its arms work partly alone"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-6-011","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The graceful shark glided past the reef, where more than a hundred kinds of fish live. Which word shows the author's opinion?","why":"Graceful is a judgment someone could disagree with. The other words can be checked.","hint":"Which word could two people disagree about?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["glided","graceful","reef","hundred"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-6-012","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Nina ate four fish tacos and loved each one. She said, 'All fish tacos are delicious.' True or false: four tacos prove her statement about all fish tacos.","why":"A small sample cannot prove something about all fish tacos. Her statement is an overgeneralization.","hint":"Does four tacos cover every taco in the world?","tags":["food"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-6-013","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The Sharks won 7 of their 10 games. The Comets won 6 of their 8 games. Which team won a greater share of its games?","why":"7 of 10 is 70 percent, but 6 of 8 is 75 percent. More wins does not mean a bigger share.","hint":"Compare the shares, not the number of wins.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The Comets","The Sharks","They are tied","You cannot tell"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-6-014","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Light from the Sun takes about eight minutes to reach Earth. So when you see the Sun, you see it as it was about how long ago?","why":"Light takes time to travel, so the sunlight reaching you left the Sun about eight minutes earlier.","hint":"Light needs time to cross space.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Right now","Eight seconds ago","Eight minutes ago","Eight hours ago"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"reaB-6-015","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Read: Each row at the ballpark holds exactly 12 fans. A school group of 50 fans arrives. What is the fewest number of rows that can seat all 50?","why":"Four rows seat only 48 fans. The last 2 fans need a fifth row.","hint":"Divide, then decide what to do with the leftover.","tags":["baseball"],"a":5},{"id":"reaB-6-016","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"An article opens: 'What if you could hear a shark's heartbeat?' Why would an author begin with a question?","why":"An opening question makes readers curious so they keep reading.","hint":"Think about how the first line affects the reader.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["To end the article quickly","To spark the reader's curiosity","To list every shark fact","To give step-by-step directions"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-6-017","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Two books are called 'Parts of a Shark' and 'Why Every Beach Should Protect Sharks.' True or false: the title 'Parts of a Shark' suggests the author wants to persuade readers.","why":"Parts of a Shark sounds like information. The word Should in the other title signals persuasion.","hint":"Which title tells readers what they should do?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"reaB-6-018","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The umpire saw the runner leave the base early but turned a blind eye. What did the umpire do?","why":"Turning a blind eye is an idiom meaning to ignore something on purpose.","hint":"Is the umpire's eyesight the point here?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Reported it right away","Became unable to see","Looked at it closely","Ignored it on purpose"],"a":3},{"id":"reaB-6-019","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Most sharks live in warm water, but a few species thrive in cold seas. True or false: at least one kind of shark lives in cold water.","why":"A few species thrive in cold seas, so at least one kind lives in cold water.","hint":"What does a few species tell you?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"reaB-6-020","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The Greek root chrono means 'time,' and -logy means 'study.' A chronology is a list of events arranged by what?","why":"Chrono means time, so a chronology lists events in the order they happened.","hint":"Use the meaning of the root chrono.","tags":[],"opts":["Time order","Size order","ABC order","Distance"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-001","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What do we call a baby shark?","why":"Baby sharks are called pups, just like young seals and dogs. Many pups swim off on their own right after birth.","hint":"Think of a word we also use for a young dog.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["A cub","A pup","A chick"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-1-002","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A whale is a kind of fish.","why":"Whales are mammals. They breathe air through lungs, feed milk to their calves, and must come up to the surface to breathe.","hint":"Do whales breathe air or water?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-1-003","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which dinosaur had three horns on its face?","why":"Triceratops means 'three-horned face.' Stegosaurus had plates on its back, and Brachiosaurus had a very long neck.","hint":"The first part of the name is a number word.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Stegosaurus","Brachiosaurus","Triceratops"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-1-004","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many legs does a spider have?","why":"Spiders have 8 legs. Insects have only 6, so a spider is not an insect.","hint":"Count the pairs: there are four pairs.","tags":["animals"],"a":8},{"id":"sciB-1-005","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which body part fills with air when you breathe in?","why":"Lungs take in air and give your body oxygen. Your stomach holds food, and your heart pumps blood.","hint":"You breathe with them inside your chest.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Your lungs","Your stomach","Your heart"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-006","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which planet is the biggest one in our solar system?","why":"Jupiter is so big that more than a thousand Earths could fit inside it. Mars is smaller than Earth.","hint":"It is a giant gas planet.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Earth","Mars","Jupiter"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-1-007","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the day in order, as the Sun moves across the sky.","why":"The Sun comes up in the morning, is highest at noon, and goes down in the evening. Then night comes.","hint":"Start when the Sun first peeks over the ground.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Sunrise","Noon","Sunset","Night"]},{"id":"sciB-1-008","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Fish use gills to take in oxygen from the water.","why":"Water moves over a fish's gills, which pull out oxygen. Sharks and most sea fish breathe this way.","hint":"Fish cannot breathe air like we do.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-009","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which tool tells you how hot or cold the air is?","why":"A thermometer measures temperature. A ruler measures length, and a clock tells time.","hint":"Weather reporters read this to tell the temperature.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["A thermometer","A ruler","A clock"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-010","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a gas?","why":"A gas spreads out to fill whatever holds it. Milk is a liquid and ice is a solid.","hint":"A gas has no shape of its own.","tags":[],"opts":["A tall glass of milk","Air inside a balloon","A big frozen ice cube"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-1-011","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the life of a sea turtle in order, from the start.","why":"Mother turtles lay eggs on sand. The babies hatch and race to the water, then grow up in the sea.","hint":"Turtles begin life on land.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Egg on the beach","Baby crawls to the sea","Young turtle swims","Adult turtle"]},{"id":"sciB-1-012","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"You toss a baseball straight up. What pulls it back down?","why":"Gravity pulls everything toward the ground. Light and sound cannot pull on a ball.","hint":"It is the same force that keeps you on the ground.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Gravity","Light","Sound"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-013","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A butterfly can taste with its feet.","why":"Butterflies have taste sensors on their feet. When one lands on a leaf, it can tell if the plant is good for its eggs.","hint":"Think about where a butterfly lands.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-1-014","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these does a plant use to make its food?","why":"Plants use sunlight, water and air to make their own food. That is why most plants grow toward light.","hint":"Plants lean toward a sunny window.","tags":[],"opts":["Moonlight","Sunlight","Wind"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-1-015","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which sea animal has a hard shell and walks sideways?","why":"Crabs have hard shells and bent legs, so they scuttle sideways. Jellyfish have soft bodies, and dolphins swim.","hint":"It lives near rocky shores and has claws.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A jellyfish","A dolphin","A crab"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-1-016","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A shadow forms when something blocks what?","why":"A shadow is a dark spot where light cannot reach because an object is in the way.","hint":"Shadows appear on sunny days.","tags":[],"opts":["Sound","Wind","Light"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-1-017","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What do we call the hot melted rock that flows out of a volcano?","why":"Lava is melted rock from deep inside Earth. When it cools, it turns into hard rock again.","hint":"It glows red and orange.","tags":[],"opts":["Lava","Steam","Snow"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-1-018","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which animal is a mammal that can truly fly?","why":"Bats are the only mammals that really fly. Owls are birds and dragonflies are insects.","hint":"It has fur and hunts at night.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["An owl","A bat","A dragonfly"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-1-019","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What covers the outside of your whole body to protect it?","why":"Skin is your body's biggest organ. It keeps germs out and water in. Bones and muscles are inside.","hint":"You can see it and touch it.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Bones","Skin","Muscles"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-1-020","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which planet is famous for its bright, wide rings?","why":"Saturn's rings are made of ice and rock bits. Mars and Venus have no rings at all.","hint":"It is a giant planet far from the Sun.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mars","Venus","Saturn"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-001","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A shark can feel tiny ripples in the water made by a fish swimming nearby.","why":"A row of sensors along a shark's sides, called the lateral line, feels ripples and tells it where prey is.","hint":"Sharks hunt even in murky water.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-2-002","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which kind of teeth would tell a scientist that a dinosaur ate meat?","why":"Sharp, curved teeth cut through meat. Flat teeth grind leaves and other plants.","hint":"Think about what teeth need to do to cut food.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Wide, flat teeth for grinding","Sharp, pointed teeth","Small teeth like rounded pebbles"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-2-003","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Some dinosaurs sat on their nests of eggs, the way birds do.","why":"Fossils show small dinosaurs curled over their eggs to keep them warm. Birds are the living relatives of dinosaurs.","hint":"Think about what birds do with eggs.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-2-004","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Long ago, people connected stars into star pictures, like a hunter or a bear. What are these called?","why":"A constellation is a pattern of stars that people see as a picture. Comets are icy space travelers.","hint":"A word that starts with 'con' and ends in 'tion'.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Shooting stars","Planet orbits","Constellations"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-005","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which tool shows which way the wind is blowing?","why":"A weather vane swings to point where the wind comes from. A rain gauge measures rain.","hint":"It often sits on top of a barn roof.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["A weather vane","A rain gauge","A scale"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-2-006","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which one shows a solid turning into a liquid?","why":"Heat makes ice cream melt from solid to liquid. Boiling makes a gas, and frost is water turning into ice.","hint":"Look for the one that starts out solid.","tags":["food"],"opts":["Water boiling in a pot","Frost forming on a window","Ice cream melting in the sun"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-007","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A ramp makes it easier to move a heavy box up. Which simple machine is a ramp?","why":"A slanted surface is called an inclined plane. A longer ramp is a gentler climb that needs less push.","hint":"A ramp is a slanted slope.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["An inclined plane","A pulley and rope","A lever with a pivot"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-2-008","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Why does a catcher's mitt have thick padding?","why":"Padding cushions the push of a fast pitch so it stings less. It does not change the ball's weight.","hint":"Think about how a fast ball feels in your hand.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["It makes the ball a lot lighter","It softens the hit of a fast ball","It makes the ball bounce back faster"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-2-009","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Your elbow and your knee are places where two bones meet and let you bend. What are they called?","why":"Joints connect bones and let them move. Ribs are bones in your chest, and nerves carry messages.","hint":"Without these, your legs would not bend.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Joints","Ribs","Nerves"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-2-010","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of a plant carries water up from the roots?","why":"The stem works like a straw, moving water and minerals up to the leaves.","hint":"Think of a straw standing in a cup.","tags":[],"opts":["The flower","The stem","The seed"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-2-011","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"An omnivore eats both plants and animals. Which one is an omnivore?","why":"Bears eat berries, roots, fish and more. Cows eat only plants, and lions eat only meat.","hint":"Look for the animal with the most varied menu.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A cow","A lion","A bear"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-012","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"How does a dolphin breathe air?","why":"Dolphins are mammals with lungs. They pop up to the surface and breathe through a blowhole.","hint":"Dolphins are mammals, not fish.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Through gills along its sides","Through its mouth while underwater","Through a blowhole on its head"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-013","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which animal has fingerprints that look a lot like human fingerprints?","why":"Koalas have tiny ridged fingerprints on their paws that are nearly like ours, even under a microscope.","hint":"Think of an animal that grips branches with its paws.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A koala","A kangaroo","A penguin"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-2-014","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sunlight passes through raindrops and makes a rainbow. What does that show?","why":"Raindrops split white sunlight into its separate colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue and violet.","hint":"The colors come from the light itself.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Raindrops are tiny colored balls","Sunlight has many colors mixed in","Clouds save up colors for rainy days"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-2-015","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A big drum and a small drum are hit. Which one makes the lower sound?","why":"Bigger things wobble more slowly, and slow wobbles make low sounds. Small drums sound higher.","hint":"Think of a deep thump versus a light tap.","tags":[],"opts":["The small drum","The big drum","Both sound the same"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-2-016","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"The Moon is smaller than Earth. Jupiter is much bigger than Earth, but the Sun is far bigger than Jupiter.","hint":"Start with the one that circles Earth.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Moon","Earth","Jupiter","Sun"]},{"id":"sciB-2-017","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"number","q":"Earth goes around the Sun one time in about how many days?","why":"One trip around the Sun takes about 365 days. That trip is what we call a year.","hint":"It is the number of days in a year.","tags":["space"],"a":365},{"id":"sciB-2-018","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Warm air rises above cooler air, which helps hot air balloons float.","why":"Warm air is lighter than the cool air around it, so it floats upward and lifts the balloon.","hint":"Think about what a burner does to the air.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-2-019","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A model bridge falls down during a test. What should an engineer do next?","why":"Engineers learn from failed tests. They fix the weak spot and try again until the design works.","hint":"Failures give clues for a better design.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Build the real one the exact same way","Decide that bridges can never work","Improve the weak part and test again"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-2-020","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which sea animal can make a pearl inside its shell?","why":"An oyster coats a tiny grain with smooth layers, and over time it grows into a pearl.","hint":"It has two shell halves that open and close.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["An oyster","A crab","A sea star"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-001","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A shark can smell a drop of blood in a huge amount of water. What does it smell with?","why":"Water flows into a shark's nostrils, and a large part of its brain handles smell. Gills are for breathing.","hint":"Smelling takes place near the front of the head.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Tiny holes in its fins","Its gill slits","Nostrils on its snout","The rows of its teeth"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-3-002","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Pteranodon soared over the dinosaurs' world. What kind of animal was it?","why":"Pterosaurs like Pteranodon were flying reptiles. They lived alongside dinosaurs but were not dinosaurs.","hint":"It had wings made of skin, not feathers.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A flying reptile","A feathered dinosaur","A giant early bat","A very early bird"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-003","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Rockets burn fuel to fly. Why must a rocket carry its own oxygen into space?","why":"Fire needs oxygen, and space has almost none. So rockets bring oxygen along with their fuel.","hint":"Think about what a fire needs.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Oxygen from Earth is too heavy to burn","The fuel would freeze without it","The engine would grow too hot","There is almost no air in space"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-3-004","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"number","q":"An octopus has 8 arms. How many arms do 5 octopuses have in all?","why":"Five octopuses with 8 arms each is 5 x 8 = 40 arms.","hint":"Count in groups of eight.","tags":["ocean"],"a":40},{"id":"sciB-3-005","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A lizard warms up its body by resting in the sun.","why":"Lizards cannot make much body heat, so they bask in sunshine to warm up, then hide or cool off when too hot.","hint":"Think about where you see lizards in the morning.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-006","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What causes the loud boom of thunder?","why":"Lightning heats the air so fast that it bursts outward, making a boom. Clouds do not crash together.","hint":"Thunder always comes after something you see.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Clouds bumping hard into each other","Air heated suddenly by lightning","Rain hitting the ground hard","Wind rushing through mountains"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-3-007","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A magnet will NOT stick to which of these?","why":"Magnets attract iron and steel. Aluminum, wood and plastic are not magnetic.","hint":"Which one is not made of iron or steel?","tags":[],"opts":["An iron nail","A steel paper clip","A cast-iron pan","An aluminum can"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-3-008","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"An axe blade splits a log by pushing the wood apart. Which simple machine is the blade?","why":"A wedge is thin on one edge and wide on the other, so it turns a push downward into a push outward.","hint":"Think of the shape of a doorstop.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["A pulley","A wedge","A lever","A wheel and axle"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-3-009","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A hard swing sends a baseball much farther than a gentle tap. Why?","why":"A harder hit gives the ball more speed and energy, so it flies farther. The ball's weight stays the same.","hint":"Think about how big the push is.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["A bigger push gives it more speed","The ball weighs less after hard hits","Gentle taps make gravity stronger","A fast swing pushes air away first"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-010","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of your skeleton works like a cage to protect your heart and lungs?","why":"Your ribs curve around your chest like a cage. The skull protects your brain, and the spine protects nerves.","hint":"You can feel them on your chest.","tags":["body"],"opts":["The spine","The skull","The ribs","The hip bones"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-3-011","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Fast swimmers like sharks have smooth, pointed bodies. How does that shape help?","why":"A smooth, tapered shape has less drag, so swimmers use less energy to move fast. Weight and floating are different.","hint":"Think of how a pointed boat cuts through waves.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["It helps the animal float up","Water slides past it easily","It keeps ocean water out","It makes the animal weigh less"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-3-012","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put the steps of how a fossil forms in order.","why":"Burying an animal quickly protects its bones. Over millions of years minerals harden them into stone fossils.","hint":"The fossil has to exist before anyone can find it.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["An animal dies and is buried","Sand and mud pile on top","Minerals slowly turn bone to rock","People dig up the fossil"]},{"id":"sciB-3-013","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The whale shark is a fish, not a whale.","why":"Whale sharks are sharks, so they are fish with gills. They get the name from their huge size.","hint":"Does it have gills or lungs?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-014","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Leaves are green because they hold a green material that captures sunlight. What is it called?","why":"Chlorophyll soaks up sunlight so a leaf can make food. In fall it breaks down, and other colors show.","hint":"Its name starts with 'chloro,' which means green.","tags":[],"opts":["Pollen","Nectar","Sap","Chlorophyll"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-3-015","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Parrotfish scrape algae off coral with hard, beak-like teeth. What does their waste become?","why":"Parrotfish grind up bits of coral, and their waste becomes sand. One fish can make hundreds of pounds a year.","hint":"Think of what a beach is made of.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Tiny pearls","Salt crystals","Fine white sand","New coral reefs"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-3-016","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A window is made of clear glass, and light passes straight through it. What is such a material called?","why":"Transparent materials let light through so you can see clearly. Opaque materials block light completely.","hint":"The word starts with 'trans,' meaning across.","tags":[],"opts":["Transparent","Opaque","Reflective","Magnetic"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-017","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"You shout in a canyon and hear your voice again a moment later. What is this?","why":"An echo is sound that bounces off a hard surface and comes back to you.","hint":"The sound has to come back from somewhere.","tags":[],"opts":["Sound turning into a flash of light","The wind repeating your own words","Sound bouncing off a hard surface","Your ears making a ringing noise"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-3-018","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A first test version of a new invention is called a what?","why":"Engineers build a prototype to test their idea, then improve it before making the final version.","hint":"It is the first model made for testing.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["A prototype","A hypothesis","An ecosystem","An eclipse"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-3-019","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Jellyfish have no brain, no heart and no bones.","why":"A jellyfish is mostly water. A net of nerves senses the world, and the body moves water without a heart.","hint":"Jellyfish are very simple animals.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-3-020","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"During a solar eclipse, the Sun seems to vanish for a few minutes. What happens?","why":"The Moon passes in front of the Sun and blocks its light. Earth's shadow causes a lunar eclipse instead.","hint":"Think about what could block the Sun's light for a few minutes.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Earth's shadow falls across the Sun","The Sun stops shining for a few minutes","A giant space cloud drifts past the Sun","The Moon moves between the Sun and Earth"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-4-001","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A hammerhead shark sweeps its wide head back and forth over the sand. What is it most likely doing?","why":"A hammerhead's wide head holds sensors spread far apart. Sweeping it over the sand helps it find stingrays buried below.","hint":"Think about why wide-set sensors might help.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Cleaning its gills","Digging a nest for eggs","Hunting for hidden prey","Looking for a place to sleep"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-4-002","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Fossil dinosaur poop is called a coprolite. What can scientists learn from it?","why":"Coprolites can hold bits of bone, seeds, or plant fibers. Those clues show what a dinosaur had eaten.","hint":"Think about what comes out after a meal.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["What the dinosaur ate","How loudly it roared","What color its skin was","How fast it could run"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-4-003","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"About how many Earths could fit inside the Sun?","why":"The Sun is enormous. Roughly 1.3 million Earths would fit inside it, even though it looks small from far away.","hint":"The Sun makes Earth look like a tiny marble.","tags":["space"],"opts":["About a hundred","About a thousand","A few hundred thousand","More than a million"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-4-004","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Suppose a coral reef grows 2 centimeters each year. How many centimeters does it grow in 15 years?","why":"Growth adds up each year: 2 cm x 15 years = 30 cm. Real reefs grow slowly, so tall reefs are very old.","hint":"Multiply the yearly growth by the years.","tags":["ocean"],"a":30},{"id":"sciB-4-005","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which kind of rock forms when melted rock cools and hardens?","why":"Igneous rock starts as lava or magma and hardens as it cools. Granite and basalt are examples.","hint":"The name comes from a word for fire.","tags":[],"opts":["Sedimentary rock","Igneous rock","Metamorphic rock","Fossil rock"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-4-006","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is about climate rather than weather?","why":"Weather is what the sky does on a given day. Climate is the usual pattern over many years.","hint":"Climate describes what is true year after year.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["A heavy rain fell on town this morning","A strong wind blew all afternoon long","Snow fell on the hills late last night","This desert has hot, dry summers every year"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-4-007","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"You stir sugar into warm water and it seems to vanish. Where did the sugar go?","why":"Sugar dissolves. Its tiny pieces mix evenly in the water. You can still taste it, so it is not gone.","hint":"Taste the water and you can tell.","tags":["food"],"opts":["It turned into more plain water","It mixed in as tiny, unseen bits","It was destroyed and is gone for good","It sank and hid at the bottom"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-4-008","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A rope runs over a wheel at the top of a flagpole. What does this pulley help you do?","why":"A fixed pulley changes the direction of your pull. It does not lower the weight or the effort needed.","hint":"Think about which way your hands move.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Pull down to raise the flag","Make the flag weigh less","Lift the flag with half the effort","Make the rope longer"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-4-009","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball rolls off a table. While it falls, it goes faster and faster. Why?","why":"Gravity pulls the whole time the ball falls, so its speed keeps growing. The ball's weight stays the same.","hint":"One force acts the entire fall.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The air pushes it hard from above","The ball gets heavier as it falls","Gravity keeps pulling it downward","Spinning adds speed each second"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-4-010","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In bright light, the dark center of your eye gets smaller. What is that center called?","why":"The pupil is the opening that lets light into the eye. The iris is the colored ring that makes it larger or smaller.","hint":"It is a hole, not a ring.","tags":["body"],"opts":["The iris","The pupil","The lens","The eyelid"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-4-011","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Seaweed grows near the ocean's surface but not on the deep sea floor. Why?","why":"Seaweed needs sunlight to make food, and sunlight fades with depth. Below a few hundred meters, it is dark.","hint":"What do plants and seaweed need to make food?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Deep water is far too salty","Waves are too strong down there","Hungry fish eat all the seaweed there","Sunlight cannot reach deep water"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-4-012","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order of when they first appeared on Earth, earliest first.","why":"Trilobites appeared over 500 million years ago, sharks about 450 million, dinosaurs about 230 million, and modern humans only about 300,000.","hint":"Dinosaurs came long after the first sharks.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs"],"opts":["Trilobites","Sharks","Dinosaurs","Humans"]},{"id":"sciB-4-013","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Sea otters sometimes hold paws while they sleep so they do not drift apart.","why":"Sleeping otters float on the water and hold paws or wrap in kelp to stay together. Some otters do this in groups called rafts.","hint":"Think about what floating animals worry about.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-4-014","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Earth's crust is broken into huge pieces that move very slowly. What are they called?","why":"Tectonic plates drift a few centimeters a year. Where they bump or slide, earthquakes and volcanoes can happen.","hint":"These pieces fit together like a giant puzzle.","tags":[],"opts":["Lava tubes","Deep ocean currents","Tectonic plates","Giant magma chambers"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-4-015","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A straw in a glass of water looks bent at the water's surface. Why?","why":"Light changes direction when it moves between water and air, called refraction. The straw is still straight.","hint":"Take the straw out and look again.","tags":[],"opts":["Light bends between water and air","The straw really bends in water","Water heats the straw unevenly","The glass squeezes the straw sideways"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-4-016","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"You hit a drum harder. Why does it sound louder?","why":"A harder hit makes bigger vibrations, and bigger vibrations make louder sound. Speed of sound stays the same.","hint":"Think about how far the drum skin moves.","tags":[],"opts":["The sound travels much faster","The drum becomes much heavier","The skin wiggles much farther","The air around it gets thicker"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-4-017","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"An engineer wants a stronger bridge frame. Which shape adds the most strength?","why":"Triangles do not bend out of shape easily, so bridges and towers use them everywhere.","hint":"Look at the metal frames of big bridges.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Triangles","Circles","Wavy lines","Loose loops"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-4-018","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Fungi and tiny bacteria break down fallen leaves on a forest floor. What does this do for the forest?","why":"Decomposers turn dead leaves into nutrients that plants take up through their roots. Nothing is wasted in a healthy forest.","hint":"Think about what happens to the leftovers of leaves.","tags":[],"opts":["Gives trees new leaves right away","Makes the soil harder and drier","Keeps animals from finding food","Returns nutrients to the soil"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-4-019","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A flounder lies flat on the sandy sea floor and matches its colors. What does this help it do?","why":"Camouflage hides the flounder from predators and from the small animals it hunts.","hint":"Think about why a color match matters.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Stay warm in the cold deep water","Blend in so it is hard to see","Swim much faster than any predator","Breathe more easily on the sand"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-4-020","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Some dinosaurs were as small as a chicken.","why":"Dinosaurs came in all sizes. Little ones like Compsognathus were about as big as a chicken.","hint":"Not every dinosaur was giant.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-5-001","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Most fish float with a gas-filled swim bladder. Sharks have none. What helps a shark stay up?","why":"Shark liver oil is lighter than seawater, so it gives some lift. Fins and a steady swim help too.","hint":"Sharks have no air inside; think of oil floating on water.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Hollow bones full of air","Gulping air at the surface","A big liver full of light oil","Warm blood that lifts the body"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-5-002","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"How do scientists work out how old a dinosaur fossil is?","why":"Rock layers hold natural clocks: some minerals change at a steady pace. That tells the age of the fossils nearby.","hint":"The rock tells time better than the bone.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["By dating the rock layers around it","By weighing the whole fossil today","By measuring how big it once grew","By counting the cracks in its bones"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-5-003","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"From Earth we always see the same side of the Moon. Why?","why":"The Moon does spin. It turns exactly once while it orbits Earth once, so one face always points toward us.","hint":"The Moon spins at a very particular rate.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Its far side is always in Earth's shadow","It never spins on its axis","Clouds hide the far side from us","It spins once for each trip around Earth"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-5-004","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Suppose only 10% of the energy in each step of a food chain passes to the next step. Plankton hold 10,000 units. How many units reach a shark that eats fish that eat the plankton?","why":"Plankton hold 10,000, fish get 10% = 1,000, and the shark gets 10% of that = 100. Energy shrinks at each step.","hint":"Take 10% twice.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"a":100},{"id":"sciB-5-005","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Rising air cools. How does that help a cloud form?","why":"Cool air holds less water vapor, so extra vapor condenses into droplets around bits of dust. Billions of drops make a cloud.","hint":"Think about a cold glass on a warm day.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Dust in the air turns into water","Water vapor turns into tiny droplets","Oxygen freezes into tiny ice crystals","Wind squeezes the rain out of the air"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-5-006","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which one is a chemical change, making a new substance?","why":"Rust is a new substance, formed when iron meets oxygen and water. Melting, dissolving and tearing keep the same material.","hint":"A chemical change cannot be easily undone.","tags":[],"opts":["Ice melting in a glass","Sugar dissolving in tea","Paper being torn into bits","A nail rusting in the rain"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-5-007","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"In a wheelbarrow, where is the pivot point (fulcrum) of the lever?","why":"The wheelbarrow turns around its wheel. The load sits between the wheel and your hands, which lift at the handles.","hint":"The thing that stays in place when lifting is the pivot.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["At the handles","At the wheel","In the middle of the load","At the front of the tray"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-5-008","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A bat hits a ball with a strong push. What does the ball do to the bat?","why":"Forces come in pairs of equal size, pointing opposite ways. That is why a hard hit can sting a batter's hands.","hint":"Think about what a batter's hands feel.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Pushes back with the same size force","Pushes back with a much smaller force","Pushes back with a much bigger force","Does not push back at all"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-5-009","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of your blood helps fight germs?","why":"White blood cells find and attack germs. Red cells carry oxygen, and platelets plug cuts.","hint":"The color tells you the cell type.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Red blood cells","Platelets","White blood cells","Plasma"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-5-010","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which living things make most of the oxygen that comes from the ocean?","why":"Phytoplankton use sunlight to make food and release oxygen. They make a huge share of the oxygen we breathe.","hint":"The answer is small and drifts in sunlight.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Whales, dolphins and other mammals","Tiny drifting algae (phytoplankton)","Giant kelp forests and nothing else","Coral reefs and sea sponges"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-5-011","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these layers of Earth's atmosphere in order, starting at the ground.","why":"Weather happens in the troposphere. Higher up come the stratosphere, mesosphere, and thermosphere, where air is very thin.","hint":"The name of the lowest layer starts with 'tropo.'","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"]},{"id":"sciB-5-012","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A bolt of lightning is hotter than the surface of the Sun.","why":"Lightning heats air to about 30,000 degrees Celsius for a split second. The Sun's surface is only about 5,500.","hint":"Lightning is brief but extremely hot.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-5-013","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists find seashell fossils near the top of a tall mountain. What does this suggest?","why":"Earth's plates push rock upward over millions of years. Mountains can lift old sea floor high into the sky.","hint":"Think about where shellfish live and how land can move.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Shellfish once climbed the mountain","Birds carried the shells up there","Heavy rain washed shells up the slope","Old sea floor was lifted up over time"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-5-014","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A red shirt looks red in sunlight. What is happening to the light?","why":"A thing's color is the light it bounces back to your eyes. A red shirt bounces red and soaks up other colors.","hint":"The color you see is the light that bounces off.","tags":[],"opts":["It makes red light inside itself","It absorbs red light and reflects the rest","It reflects red light and absorbs the rest","It blocks all the light"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-5-015","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Put your ear on a railroad track and you may hear a far-off train sooner than through the air. Why?","why":"Particles in solids are packed close together, so sound passes along faster than in air.","hint":"Think about which material is more tightly packed.","tags":[],"opts":["Sound moves faster in metal than air","Metal always makes sound louder than air","Trains make their sounds only in the metal","The air blocks all the sound of trains"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-5-016","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A team makes a bike frame stronger with a new material, but the bike costs much more. What is this called?","why":"Engineers weigh good and bad results of each design choice. Getting more of one thing often costs something else.","hint":"You gain something and give up something else.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["A prototype","A hypothesis","A trade-off","A variable"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-5-017","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A desert fox has very large ears. How do they help it survive?","why":"Blood flows through the thin, big ears and loses heat to the air, which helps keep the fox cool.","hint":"Think about what a desert does to bodies.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["They let extra body heat escape","They store water for dry days","They hear water underground","They scare away bigger animals"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-5-018","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Leaves have tiny openings, mostly on the underside. What passes through them?","why":"These openings, called stomata, let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapor out.","hint":"They work like tiny doors for gases.","tags":[],"opts":["Sunlight, soil and rainwater","Sap and tiny root hairs","Seeds, pollen and fruit","Air gases and water vapor"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-5-019","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Astronauts float inside the space station even though gravity still pulls on them. Why?","why":"Gravity up there is still about 90% as strong. The station moves sideways so fast that it keeps falling around Earth.","hint":"Gravity has not switched off.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Gravity does not reach that far up","Both are falling around Earth together","Engines inside cancel out the gravity","The station sits above all of Earth's air"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-5-020","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A day on Venus is longer than a year on Venus.","why":"Venus spins very slowly: one turn takes about 243 Earth days, but one trip around the Sun takes only about 225.","hint":"Venus is a very slow spinner.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-6-001","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have skeletons of cartilage, not bone. Which fish are their closest relatives?","why":"Sharks, rays and skates all have cartilage skeletons and share a common ancestor. Tuna and eels have bony skeletons.","hint":"Look for another flat or flexible-skeleton group.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Eels and morays","Tuna and mackerel","Rays and skates","Seahorses and pipefish"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-6-002","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Archaeopteryx had feathers and wings, but also teeth and a long bony tail. Why is this fossil so important?","why":"Archaeopteryx mixes bird traits with dinosaur traits. That supports the idea that birds descended from dinosaurs.","hint":"A mix of two animal groups.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["It has features of dinosaurs and birds","It proves that all dinosaurs could fly","It shows that birds lived before dinosaurs","It proves dinosaurs lived mostly in water"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-6-003","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A star is 10 light-years away. What do you see when you look at it tonight?","why":"A light-year is the distance light travels in one year. Light from that star left 10 years ago, so you see its past.","hint":"Light takes time to travel across space.","tags":["space"],"opts":["The star as it is right now","The star 10 years from now","The star as it was 10 days ago","The star as it was 10 years ago"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-6-004","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Earth makes one full turn, 360 degrees, in 24 hours. How many degrees does it turn in 1 hour?","why":"360 divided by 24 equals 15. This is why time zones are about 15 degrees wide.","hint":"Divide the full turn by the number of hours.","tags":["space"],"a":15},{"id":"sciB-6-005","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A hurricane weakens fast when it moves over land. Why?","why":"Hurricanes get energy from warm ocean water. Over land, that supply is cut off, and rubbing against land slows the winds.","hint":"What is the storm's energy source?","tags":["weather"],"opts":["Land has far too much cool wind","It loses the warm ocean that feeds it","Mountains tear it into small pieces","Land makes the air press down harder"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-6-006","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A solid steel nail sinks, yet a huge steel ship floats. Why?","why":"A ship's hollow shape makes its overall density less than water. It pushes aside water equal to its weight, so it floats.","hint":"Think about the whole ship's shape, not just its metal.","tags":["ocean","machines"],"opts":["Ship steel is a lighter kind of steel","The engine pushes it up from below","Large objects feel less of gravity's pull","Its hollow shape pushes aside lots of water"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-6-007","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A gear with 10 teeth drives a gear with 40 teeth. How many turns of the small gear make the big gear turn exactly once?","why":"Each turn of the small gear moves 10 teeth, so it takes 40 divided by 10 = 4 turns to move all 40 teeth.","hint":"Count how many teeth need to pass by.","tags":["machines"],"a":4},{"id":"sciB-6-008","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A pitched ball's motion energy depends on its speed squared. If a pitch is twice as fast, its motion energy becomes how many times bigger?","why":"Doubling the speed multiplies the speed squared by 2 x 2 = 4. That is why fast pitches are so much harder to catch.","hint":"Square the number 2.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Two times","Four times","Three times","Eight times"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-6-009","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why can two eyes judge distance better than one eye?","why":"The brain compares two slightly different views to work out how far away things are. Close one eye and it is harder.","hint":"Your eyes are a few centimeters apart.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Each eye sees from a different spot","Two eyes take in twice as much light","One eye is always closed inside the brain","Each eye sees a different color of light"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-6-010","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A deep-sea anglerfish dangles a glowing lure. How is the light made?","why":"This is bioluminescence. Chemicals react and give off light without heat. It can lure small prey in the dark.","hint":"Light can come from chemistry, not only from heat or sunlight.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["By soaking up sunlight all day","By electric sparks like a tiny battery","By chemical reactions in living cells","By rubbing its scales together"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"sciB-6-011","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the path of a sound through your ear in order.","why":"Sound shakes the eardrum, tiny bones pass the shaking inward, and nerves turn it into signals your brain understands.","hint":"The eardrum is the first thing to wiggle.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Sound waves enter the ear canal","The eardrum vibrates","Tiny bones pass on the shaking","Nerves signal the brain"]},{"id":"sciB-6-012","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon in the same room because the metal really is colder.","why":"Both are the same temperature as the room. Metal pulls heat out of your hand faster, so it feels colder.","hint":"Both spoons have sat in the same room.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-6-013","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists study air bubbles trapped in very old polar ice. What can the bubbles show them?","why":"Snow packs down into ice and traps bubbles of the air from that time. Deeper ice holds older air.","hint":"The bubbles were sealed in when the snow fell.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["How deep the oceans were","What Earth's air was like long ago","Which animals lived in the ice","How many storms struck last year"],"a":1},{"id":"sciB-6-014","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"number","q":"You stand 2 meters in front of a flat mirror. How many meters away from you does your reflection seem to be?","why":"Your reflection seems to be as far behind the mirror as you are in front: 2 m + 2 m = 4 m from you.","hint":"The reflection is behind the mirror.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"sciB-6-015","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"An ambulance siren sounds higher as it comes toward you and lower as it drives away. Why?","why":"Moving toward you squeezes the waves closer together, giving a higher pitch. Moving away spreads them out.","hint":"The truck moves while the sound is made.","tags":[],"opts":["The siren changes its own tune on purpose","Sound travels faster as the truck leaves","Air gets thicker behind the moving truck","Sound waves bunch up, then stretch out"],"a":3},{"id":"sciB-6-016","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A tall, narrow block tower and a short, wide one are pushed. Why is the wide, low tower harder to tip?","why":"A low center of gravity above a wide base keeps the tower balanced, so a tip needs a larger push.","hint":"Think about where the weight is and what it stands on.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["It has far fewer blocks in the tower","Its blocks are all heavier on one side","Its weight sits lower over a wider base","Wind cannot touch a wide, low tower"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-6-017","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A harmless hoverfly has yellow and black stripes like a stinging wasp. What is this trick called?","why":"In mimicry, one animal looks like another that predators avoid. Camouflage blends into the background instead.","hint":"It copies another animal's look.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Mimicry","Hibernation","Migration","Camouflage"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-6-018","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A tiny seed grows into a tree that weighs tons. Where do the carbon atoms in its wood come from?","why":"Leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air and build it into sugars and wood. Sunlight gives energy, not atoms.","hint":"Trees build wood from a gas.","tags":[],"opts":["Minerals in the soil","Water from the roots","Carbon dioxide in the air","Sunlight shining on the leaves"],"a":2},{"id":"sciB-6-019","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why is a black hole black?","why":"A black hole packs huge mass into a tiny space. Gravity near it is so strong that even light cannot get out.","hint":"Think about what escapes from strong gravity.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Even light cannot escape its gravity","It is wrapped in thick black dust clouds","It is too cold to give off light","It is an empty hole with no mass"],"a":0},{"id":"sciB-6-020","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks lived on Earth before the first trees did.","why":"Sharks swam in oceans more than 400 million years ago. The first trees appeared about 385 million years ago.","hint":"Sharks have been around for a very long time.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-1-001","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A cheetah is fast. A snail is the opposite of fast. Which word fits the snail?","why":"Opposites mean the reverse. Quick means almost the same as fast, so it cannot be the opposite.","hint":"Opposite means the reverse.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["quick","slow","loud"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-1-002","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word rhymes with 'whale'?","why":"Whale and tail end with the same sound. Wheel starts like whale but ends differently.","hint":"Listen to the ending sounds.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["wall","wheel","tail"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-1-003","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Put 'moon' and 'light' together. What new word do you make?","why":"Two small words can join into one compound word. Keep them in order: moon, then light.","hint":"Keep the two words in the same order.","tags":["space"],"opts":["moonlight","lightmoon","moonlite"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-004","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'rocket' has two syllables (claps).","why":"Rock-et has two claps. Each syllable has one vowel sound you can hear.","hint":"Clap the word out loud.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-005","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The dinosaur was huge. Which word means almost the same as huge?","why":"Huge and giant both mean very big. Tiny means the opposite.","hint":"Pick the word that also means very big.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["tiny","quiet","giant"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-1-006","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"In 'The shark swims fast,' which word is the action word?","why":"Swims tells what the shark does, so it is the verb. Shark is the who, and fast tells how.","hint":"Which word shows something happening?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["swims","shark","fast"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-007","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these baseball words in ABC order.","why":"Look at the first letter of each word: b, g, p, u. That is the order of the alphabet.","hint":"Check the first letter of each word.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["ball","glove","pitcher","umpire"]},{"id":"wdsB-1-008","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word begins with the same sound as 'shark'?","why":"The letters sh make one sound. Ship starts that way. Sun starts with s alone, and chip starts with ch.","hint":"Say 'shark' slowly and listen to the start.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["sun","chip","ship"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-1-009","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"One beach, two ____. Which plural is spelled right?","why":"Words that end in ch, sh, s, x, or z add -es to mean more than one.","hint":"Say it out loud with two beaches.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["beaches","beachs","beachies"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-010","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"number","q":"'Octo' means eight. An octopus has that many arms. How many arms is that?","why":"Octo means eight, and an octopus does have eight arms. An octagon has eight sides too.","hint":"The answer is hiding in the question.","tags":["ocean"],"a":8,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-1-011","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Last night we ____ at the stars. Which word fits?","why":"Last night already happened, so the verb needs -ed. Looked tells about the past.","hint":"Last night is over. Use the past form.","tags":["space"],"opts":["looked","look","looking"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-012","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence asks a question?","why":"A question asks something and ends with a question mark. The other two tell or shout.","hint":"Look for the question mark.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Crabs hide under rocks.","Where do crabs hide?","Look at that crab!"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-1-013","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Swimmed' is the right word for what a shark did yesterday.","why":"Swim changes to swam in the past. Some verbs do not follow the -ed rule.","hint":"Say it in a sentence: Yesterday the shark ...","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-1-014","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A diver swims down in deep water. Which word is the opposite of deep?","why":"Deep water goes far down. Shallow water is not deep, so you can stand in it.","hint":"Think of water you can stand in.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["wet","shallow","wide"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-1-015","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Shark, whale, and ____ all live in the sea. Which word fits?","why":"A dolphin is a sea animal like the others. A robin is a bird, and a camel lives in the desert.","hint":"Which animal lives in the ocean?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["robin","camel","dolphin"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-1-016","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the words in order to make a sentence.","why":"A sentence starts with a capital letter, tells who or what, then what it does.","hint":"Start with the word that has a capital letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["The","big","whale","sings."]},{"id":"wdsB-1-017","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Shark starts with S. Which letter comes right before S in the alphabet?","why":"The alphabet goes Q, R, S, T. R comes right before S, and T comes right after.","hint":"Sing the alphabet song up to S.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["R","T","P"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-1-018","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word is spelled the same forward and backward?","why":"Wow reads the same both ways. Wolf backward spells flow. Words like wow are called palindromes.","hint":"Read each word from its last letter.","tags":[],"opts":["wolf","wow","owl"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-1-019","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A shark isn't a mammal. Which two words make 'isn't'?","why":"The apostrophe takes the place of the o in not. Is not squeezes into isn't.","hint":"Which letter does the apostrophe replace?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["is now","is not","in not"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-1-020","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Mars spins.' is a complete sentence.","why":"A complete sentence tells who or what, and what it does. Mars is the who, and spins is the action.","hint":"Does it tell who and what they do?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-001","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which word sounds just like 'whale' and means a long, sad cry?","why":"Wail and whale are homophones: they sound alike but have different spellings and meanings.","hint":"Find the word that sounds the same as whale.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["wail","well","wheel"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-2-002","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"One wolf, two ____. Which plural is spelled right?","why":"Many words that end in f change to -ves in the plural. Leaf becomes leaves, and wolf becomes wolves.","hint":"Say 'two wolves' out loud.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["wolves","wolfs","wolfes"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-003","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sam's bat is long. Leo's bat is longer. Pat's bat is the ____ of all.","why":"Use -er to compare two things. Use -est to compare three or more things.","hint":"Count how many bats are being compared.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["more long","longest","longer"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-2-004","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'huge' and 'tiny' are synonyms.","why":"Synonyms mean the same thing. Huge and tiny mean opposite things, so they are antonyms.","hint":"Do the two words mean the same or opposite?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-2-005","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The coach said the call was unfair. What does 'unfair' mean?","why":"The prefix un- means not. Unfair means not fair.","hint":"Think about what the prefix un- does.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["not fair","very fair","fair again"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-006","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A diver who has no fear is called ____.","why":"The suffix -less means without. Fearful means full of fear, which is the opposite.","hint":"Which word ending means without?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["fearful","fearly","fearless"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-2-007","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the word parts in order to spell a baseball position.","why":"Out + field + er spells outfielder, a player who catches balls far from home plate.","hint":"Think of the grassy part of the field.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["out","field","er"]},{"id":"wdsB-2-008","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which word is NOT a compound word?","why":"Sunshine is sun + shine. Seahorse is sea + horse. Seal is one word, not two small words joined.","hint":"Try splitting each word into two real words.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["sunshine","seahorse","seal"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-2-009","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Shark skin feels rough when you rub it one way. Which word is the opposite of rough?","why":"Smooth means even, with no bumps. It is the opposite of rough. Sharp and rocky are different ideas.","hint":"Think of something with no bumps.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["smooth","sharp","rocky"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-010","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The comet zoomed through the sky. Which word means almost the same as zoomed?","why":"Zoomed means moved very fast. Raced means almost the same. Floated is slow, and stopped is not moving.","hint":"Picture how fast a comet moves.","tags":["space"],"opts":["floated","stopped","raced"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-2-011","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables (claps) are in the word 'triceratops'?","why":"Tri-cer-a-tops has four claps. Clap once for each beat as you say it slowly.","hint":"Say it slowly and clap each beat.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":4},{"id":"wdsB-2-012","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A bat flew out of the cave at dusk. In this sentence, what does 'bat' mean?","why":"Bat can mean the animal or the stick in baseball. The cave and flew are clues. A bat is a mammal, not a bird.","hint":"Use the clues cave and flew.","tags":["animals","baseball"],"opts":["a wooden stick for hitting","a flying animal","a kind of bird"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-2-013","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Please ____ the score on the board. Which word fits?","why":"Write means to put words or numbers on a surface. Right means correct or the opposite of left.","hint":"Which word means putting marks on a board?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["right","write","rite"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-2-014","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'ocean' is a noun.","why":"A noun names a person, place, or thing. Ocean names a place.","hint":"Nouns name people, places, or things.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-015","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"____ octopus has eight arms. Which word fits?","why":"Use an before a word that starts with a vowel sound. Octopus begins with the short o sound.","hint":"Does octopus start with a vowel sound?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A","On","An"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-2-016","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The past tense of 'grow' is 'growed'.","why":"Grow changes to grew in the past. Irregular verbs do not follow the -ed rule.","hint":"Say: Yesterday the baby shark ... bigger.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-2-017","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"In 'The slippery eel hid,' which word is the adjective?","why":"An adjective describes a noun. Slippery tells what the eel is like. Hid is a verb.","hint":"Which word tells what the eel is like?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["slippery","eel","hid"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-018","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The crab scurried across the sand. What does 'scurried' mean?","why":"Scurried means ran fast with short, quick steps. Crabs often scurry sideways.","hint":"Picture a crab hurrying across a beach.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["rested quietly in the warm sun","ran quickly with small steps","dug a very deep hole in sand"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-2-019","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"In the baseball word 'knuckleball', the letter k is silent.","why":"The k is silent in knuckleball, just as in knee and knot. You say 'nuckleball'.","hint":"Say the word aloud and listen for a k sound.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-2-020","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Add -ing to 'swim'. Which spelling is right?","why":"In short words with one vowel and one ending consonant, double the consonant before -ing.","hint":"Listen to the short i sound in swim.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["swiming","swimming","swimeing"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-3-001","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The coach and the umpire could not agree. Which word means 'to not agree'?","why":"The prefix dis- means not or the opposite of. Disagree means to not agree.","hint":"Which prefix means not?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["disagree","reagree","preagree","agreeless"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-3-002","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A shark's bite is full of power. Which word means 'full of power'?","why":"The suffix -ful means full of. Powerless means without power, which is the opposite.","hint":"Which word ending means full of?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["powerless","powerly","powerment","powerful"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-003","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The baseball team was unbeatable. What is the root word in 'unbeatable'?","why":"Take off the prefix un- and the ending -able, and beat is left. The word table is just hiding at the end.","hint":"Take off the beginning and the ending.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["table","beat","able","unbeat"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-3-004","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'pair' and 'pear' are homophones.","why":"Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. A pair is two, and a pear is a fruit.","hint":"Say both words aloud. Do they sound alike?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-3-005","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The shark's teeth were enormous. Which word means almost the same as enormous?","why":"Enormous means very, very big, and so does gigantic. Ordinary means normal, and sharp is a different idea.","hint":"Choose a word that means very big.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["gigantic","narrow","ordinary","sharp"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-3-006","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Shark skin is as ____ as sandpaper. Which word fits?","why":"Coarse means rough. Course is a path or a class. They sound the same but mean different things.","hint":"Which word means rough?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["course","corse","coarse","coarce"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-3-007","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The batter swung at the first pitch. Which meaning of 'pitch' fits?","why":"Pitch has many meanings. Here a batter is swinging at a ball, so it means the throw.","hint":"Use the clue: the batter swung.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["a thick, sticky black tar","how high or low a sound is","to set up a tent","a ball thrown by the pitcher"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-008","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words start with the prefix un-: unlock, uncle, undo, under, unfair?","why":"Unlock, undo, and unfair add un- to a real word. Uncle and under do not, since cle and der are not words.","hint":"Remove un-. Is a real word left?","tags":[],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-009","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In 'The shark swam silently into the cave,' which word is the adverb?","why":"An adverb tells how, when, or where. Silently tells how the shark swam.","hint":"Which word tells how it swam?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["silently","shark","into","cave"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-3-010","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The outfielder ____ the fly ball with one hand. Which word fits?","why":"Catch changes to caught in the past. Catched is not a word.","hint":"The play already happened.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["catched","caught","catch","catching"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-3-011","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from coldest to hottest.","why":"Words can show small or big amounts. Freezing is the coldest, and scorching is the hottest.","hint":"Imagine a thermometer rising.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["freezing","cool","warm","scorching"]},{"id":"wdsB-3-012","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The shark circled its prey, a seal swimming near the rocks. What does 'prey' mean?","why":"Prey is an animal that other animals hunt and eat. The hunter is called the predator.","hint":"What is the shark about to do?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["a safe place to hide","a type of ocean rock","an animal that is hunted","a baby shark"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-3-013","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In which word do the letters ph make the f sound?","why":"In dolphin, ph sounds like f. In uphill, the p and h are separate sounds in different word parts.","hint":"Say each word and listen for an f sound.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["uphill","sharp","chop","dolphin"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-014","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The prefix re- means 'not,' so 'redo' means 'not do.'","why":"Re- means again, so redo means do again. The prefix un- is one that means not.","hint":"What do you do when you redo a puzzle?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-3-015","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The two sharks ____ near the reef every day. Which verb fits?","why":"Two sharks is plural, so the verb has no -s. Swims goes with one shark.","hint":"Is it one shark or more than one?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["swims","swim","swimmed","swimming"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-3-016","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'aqua' means water. Which word has to do with water?","why":"An aquarium holds water and fish. Quarter, equal, and squad only have the letters qua inside them.","hint":"Which word names a place with water inside?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["quarter","equal","squad","aquarium"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-017","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Dinosaur fossils are ancient. Which word is the opposite of ancient?","why":"Ancient means very, very old, so the opposite is modern or new. Old is a synonym.","hint":"An opposite means the reverse.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["old","huge","buried","modern"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-3-018","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'diver' means 'one who dives,' because -er can mean 'one who.'","why":"Adding -er to a verb can name the person who does it: dive and diver, teach and teacher.","hint":"Think of words like baker and teacher.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-3-019","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'astronaut' comes from old words for 'star' and 'sailor.' So an astronaut is a sailor of the ____ .","why":"Astro means star and naut means sailor. Astronaut means star sailor.","hint":"Think about where astronauts travel.","tags":["space"],"opts":["stars","sea","sand","storm"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-3-020","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses 'ray' to mean a flat fish that glides through the sea?","why":"A ray can be a fish or a beam of light. Only one sentence is about the animal.","hint":"Which sentence is about an animal?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A ray of sunshine lit the water.","The coach felt a ray of hope.","A ray glided over the sandy reef.","A ray from the lamp lit the page."],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-4-001","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Fin is to shark as wing is to ____ .","why":"A fin is a part of a shark, and a wing is a part of a bird. A feather is a part of the wing.","hint":"Fin is a part of a shark. A wing is part of what?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["bird","feather","sky","nest"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-4-002","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A triceratops had horns on its face. Triangle, tricycle, and triceratops all start with tri-. What does tri- mean?","why":"Tri- means three. A triangle has three sides, a tricycle has three wheels, and a triceratops had three horns.","hint":"Count the sides of a triangle.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["two","three","four","many"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-4-003","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"After the class show, the teacher said, 'That was a home run!' What did she mean?","why":"A home run is the best hit in baseball, so people use it to say something went very well.","hint":"Is she talking about a real baseball game?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["It was about baseball","It was far too long","It was a big surprise","It went extremely well"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-4-004","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The prefix sub- means 'under,' so a submarine travels under the sea.","why":"Sub- means under or below, and marine means of the sea. A submarine travels under the sea.","hint":"Marine has to do with the sea.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-4-005","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'scope' means to look. Which tool lets you look at stars that are very far away?","why":"Tele means far, as in telephone, so a telescope looks at far things. Micro means small.","hint":"Tele means far, like in telephone.","tags":["space"],"opts":["microscope","periscope","kaleidoscope","telescope"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-4-006","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The shark ____ through the water without a sound. Which word best shows smooth, quiet movement?","why":"Glided means moved smoothly and easily. Crashed, stomped, and bounced sound noisy or bumpy.","hint":"Pick the word that sounds smooth and quiet.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["crashed","stomped","glided","bounced"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-4-007","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The pitcher was tired, ____ he kept throwing hard. Which word fits best?","why":"But shows a contrast: he was tired, yet kept going. So and because show cause and do not fit.","hint":"Does the second part go against the first?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["so","because","but","or"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-4-008","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many adjectives are in this sentence? Huge, gray whales swam slowly.","why":"Huge and gray describe the whales, so they are adjectives. Slowly tells how they swam, so it is an adverb.","hint":"Adjectives describe nouns.","tags":["ocean"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-4-009","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'dinosaur' comes from the Greek words 'deinos' and 'sauros.' Which meaning matches?","why":"Dino means terrible and saur means lizard. Dinosaurs were reptiles, though not true lizards.","hint":"Think of words like stegosaurus and lizard.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["ancient bird","giant bone","scaly dragon","terrible lizard"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-4-010","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The octopus was camouflaged. It matched the rocks so well that the diver swam right past. What does 'camouflaged' mean?","why":"Camouflaged means hidden by looking like the surroundings. The clue is that it matched the rocks.","hint":"Use the clue: matched the rocks.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["afraid of passing divers","hidden by blending in","covered with wet sand","swimming very fast away"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-4-011","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball glove that can be washed is washable. What does the suffix -able mean?","why":"Washable means able to be washed. The suffix -less means without, and -ful means full of.","hint":"Think about what washable says about a glove.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["can be done","having none of","being full of","more than usual"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-4-012","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these windy words in order from the calmest to the strongest.","why":"A calm day has no wind. A breeze is light, a gale is strong, and a hurricane is the most powerful.","hint":"Imagine wind getting stronger and stronger.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["calm","breeze","gale","hurricane"]},{"id":"wdsB-4-013","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The swimmer was pushed along by a strong current. Which meaning of 'current' fits?","why":"Here, current means water moving in one direction. It can also mean happening now, or electricity flowing.","hint":"Think about what pushes a swimmer.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["happening right now","moving water in the sea","a flow of electricity","a small red berry"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-4-014","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Tall is to short as wide is to ____ .","why":"Tall and short are opposites, and so are wide and narrow.","hint":"Find the opposite of wide.","tags":[],"opts":["long","flat","narrow","high"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-4-015","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which group of words is a complete sentence?","why":"A sentence needs a subject and a verb, and it must make sense alone. The other groups leave something out.","hint":"Which one tells who did what?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The tall pitcher.","Because the game was long.","Running around the bases.","The fans cheered loudly."],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-4-016","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"It is correct to say 'less sharks' because less is the word for things you can count.","why":"Use fewer for things you can count, like fewer sharks. Use less for amounts, like less water.","hint":"Can you count sharks one by one?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-4-017","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In 'The submarine glided beneath the waves,' which word is the preposition?","why":"A preposition shows where something is. Beneath tells where the submarine glided.","hint":"Which word tells where?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["beneath","glided","submarine","waves"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-4-018","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Seeing one fin was just the tip of the iceberg; many sharks were nearby. What does 'tip of the iceberg' mean?","why":"Most of an iceberg hides underwater. The saying means you see just a small part of something much bigger.","hint":"Think of how much of an iceberg you cannot see.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["a small part of something bigger","a very cold and icy place","a smart way to stay safe","a rare and very lucky chance"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-4-019","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A very faint star may not be ____ without a telescope. Which spelling is correct?","why":"Visible ends in -ible, not -able. Memory trick: if you can see it with your vision, it is visible.","hint":"The word starts like vision.","tags":["space"],"opts":["visable","vizible","visibul","visible"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-4-020","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'than' is used to compare things, as in 'The Moon is smaller than Earth.'","why":"Than compares two things. Then tells about time, as in first this, then that.","hint":"Think of words that compare, like bigger than.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-001","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'aud' means to hear. A whale's song can be ____ from far away. Which word fits?","why":"Audible means able to be heard. Visible is for seeing, edible for eating, and legible for reading.","hint":"Think of an audience, which listens.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["visible","edible","audible","legible"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-5-002","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"All the players ____ Sam got to practice on time. Which word fits?","why":"Except means leaving out. Accept means to take or agree to something.","hint":"Which word means everyone but Sam?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["except","accept","expect","excerpt"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-003","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses 'wind' with the long-i sound, as in 'find'?","why":"When wind means to twist or turn, it rhymes with find. The wind that blows rhymes with pinned.","hint":"Which wind is something you do to a toy?","tags":["space"],"opts":["A cold wind blew across the bay.","The wind moved the sailboat.","The wind rattled the window.","Please wind the toy rocket."],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-5-004","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'lead,' the metal, sounds exactly like 'led,' the past tense of 'lead.'","why":"The metal is said 'led.' So the two words sound alike, even though they are spelled differently.","hint":"Say the metal's name out loud.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-005","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'port' means to carry. Which word means 'able to be carried'?","why":"Port means carry and -able means can be. A porter is a person who carries, not a thing that can be carried.","hint":"Add the ending that means can be.","tags":[],"opts":["porter","portable","import","deport"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-5-006","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'The Moon was a silver coin hanging in the sky.' Which figure of speech is this?","why":"A metaphor says one thing is another, without like or as. A simile would say the Moon was like a coin.","hint":"Does it use the word like or as?","tags":["space"],"opts":["simile","metaphor","hyperbole","alliteration"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-5-007","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Crabs live in the intertidal zone. The prefix inter- means 'between.' Where is this zone?","why":"Intertidal means between the tides. It is the shore that is covered at high tide and bare at low tide.","hint":"Tidal has to do with tides.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["between high and low tide","far out in the deep sea","high up on the cliffs","under the ocean floor"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-008","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"number","q":"How many compound words are in this list: starfish, dolphin, seahorse, jellyfish, octopus, sailboat?","why":"Starfish, seahorse, jellyfish, and sailboat are each two words joined. Dolphin and octopus are not.","hint":"Can you split the word into two real words?","tags":["ocean"],"a":4},{"id":"wdsB-5-009","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is an exaggeration (hyperbole)?","why":"Hyperbole stretches the truth so far that no one believes it. Nobody could really eat a whale.","hint":"Which one could not possibly be true?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["I'm so hungry I could eat a whale.","I'm hungry after the long game.","The pitcher was hungry for a win.","We ate lunch after the game."],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-010","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'tsunami' comes from the Japanese words 'tsu' and 'nami.' What do they mean together?","why":"Tsu means harbor and nami means wave. A tsunami is a giant wave that can crash into a harbor.","hint":"One part names a place where boats stay.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["storm cloud","sea monster","rising tide","harbor wave"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-5-011","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A shark may ____ many teeth in its life. Which word fits?","why":"Lose is a verb that means to no longer have something. Loose means not tight.","hint":"You need the verb meaning to not keep.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["loose","loss","lost","lose"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-5-012","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in ABC order. Check each letter in turn.","why":"All start with pla. Plate has t fourth, so it goes last. For the others, the fifth letter decides: e, then k, then t.","hint":"Find the first letter where the words differ.","tags":["ocean","space"],"opts":["planet","plankton","plant","plate"]},{"id":"wdsB-5-013","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Tired is to exhausted as hungry is to ____ .","why":"Exhausted means extremely tired. Starving means extremely hungry. Both pairs go from mild to very strong.","hint":"Find the very strong word for hungry.","tags":["food"],"opts":["full","thirsty","starving","angry"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-5-014","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A writer describes a graceful dolphin. Which word has the most positive feeling?","why":"Words with similar meanings can feel different. Slender sounds graceful; scrawny and bony sound weak.","hint":"Which word would you like said about you?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["scrawny","bony","slender","skinny"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-5-015","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence joins its two thoughts correctly?","why":"Two full thoughts need more than a comma. A comma plus so joins them correctly.","hint":"Two full sentences need a joining word.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The ball flew, fans cheered.","The ball flew so, fans cheered.","The ball flew. so fans cheered.","The ball flew, so fans cheered."],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-5-016","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Telescope is to planet as microscope is to ____ .","why":"A telescope shows faraway things like planets. A microscope shows tiny things like cells.","hint":"Which tool shows tiny things?","tags":["space"],"opts":["faraway star","bright comet","huge whale","tiny cell"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-5-017","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'The shark is a torpedo with teeth' is a simile.","why":"A simile compares using like or as. This says the shark is a torpedo, so it is a metaphor.","hint":"Look for the words like or as.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-5-018","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A jellyfish injects toxin when it stings. Which word describes it most exactly?","why":"Venomous animals inject toxin with a bite or sting. Poisonous ones are harmful to eat or touch.","hint":"The clue word is injects.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["venomous","poisonous","dangerous","harmful"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-5-019","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'bass' can name a fish or a deep musical sound. How is the deep sound said?","why":"The fish rhymes with class. The deep sound rhymes with face. The spelling is the same but the sound differs.","hint":"Think of a bass guitar.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["like 'mass'","like 'base'","like 'bus'","like 'bask'"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-5-020","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these words came from a person's name?","why":"The sandwich is named for the Earl of Sandwich. Sunrise, seashell, and sailboat are made of two plain words.","hint":"Which word is not two simple words?","tags":["food"],"opts":["sunrise","seashell","sailboat","sandwich"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-001","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'rupt' means to break. Which word means to break in on someone's talking?","why":"Inter- means between, so interrupt means to break in between. Erupt means to burst out, and abrupt means sudden.","hint":"Which prefix means between?","tags":[],"opts":["erupt","corrupt","abrupt","interrupt"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-002","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Geologist is to rocks as astronomer is to ____ .","why":"A geologist studies rocks, and an astronomer studies stars and planets. Fossils belong to a paleontologist.","hint":"What does an astronomer study?","tags":["space"],"opts":["fossils","oceans","stars","storms"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-6-003","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'chron' means time. Which word describes events listed in the order they happened?","why":"Chronological means in time order. Chronic means lasting a long time. Chromatic comes from the word for color.","hint":"Think of a timeline.","tags":[],"opts":["chronic","chronological","chromatic","synthetic"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-6-004","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'ambiguous' means 'having only one clear meaning.'","why":"Ambiguous means unclear or open to more than one meaning. A word with one clear meaning is unambiguous.","hint":"Think about what un-ambiguous would mean.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-6-005","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A reporter keeps asking the pitcher many questions. Which word would sound like a complaint?","why":"Nosy has a negative feeling, as if the questions are unwanted. The other words sound positive or neutral.","hint":"Which word would the pitcher not enjoy hearing?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["curious","inquisitive","interested","nosy"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-006","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The root 'pisc' means fish and -vore means eater. Which animal is a piscivore?","why":"A piscivore is a fish eater. The cow, bee, and koala eat grass, nectar, and leaves.","hint":"Put the two word parts together: fish eater.","tags":["animals","ocean"],"opts":["a cow grazing in a field","a bee sipping nectar","a koala munching leaves","a pelican diving for fish"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-007","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The singular is 'alga,' a simple organism that includes seaweed. What is the plural?","why":"Alga comes from Latin, and its plural is algae, said AL-jee. Most English plurals add -s, but this one does not.","hint":"Latin words often change their endings.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["algae","algas","algi","algaes"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-6-008","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables are in the word 'paleontologist'?","why":"Pa-le-on-tol-o-gist has six beats. The middle part, on-tol-o, is easy to miss.","hint":"Clap slowly and watch the vowels in the middle.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":6},{"id":"wdsB-6-009","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The school ____ cheered for the baseball team. Which spelling is correct?","why":"A principal is the head of a school. Memory trick: the principal is your pal. A principle is a rule.","hint":"Which word means the head of a school?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["principle","princepal","principal","principall"],"a":2},{"id":"wdsB-6-010","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses 'fast' as an adverb?","why":"An adverb tells how an action happens. Fast tells how the mako swims. In the others, fast describes a noun.","hint":"Does fast describe a noun or an action?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["The mako is a fast swimmer.","The mako swims fast.","A fast mako zipped by.","That fast fish escaped."],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-6-011","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'umpire' once began with n: 'a noumpere.' People heard it as 'an oumpere.' What happened to the n?","why":"People heard 'a noumpere' as 'an oumpere,' so the n joined the article. Apron was once 'napron' the same way.","hint":"Say 'a noumpere' quickly out loud.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["It slid onto the word before it","It was replaced by a u","It moved to the end","It was lost in a spelling contest"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-6-012","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in ABC order. They all start with kn.","why":"Skip the shared kn and compare the third letters: a, e, i, o, u. The vowels go in that order.","hint":"The third letter is the first one that differs.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["knack","knee","knight","know","knuckleball"]},{"id":"wdsB-6-013","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Chilly is to frigid as damp is to ____ .","why":"Frigid is a much stronger word than chilly. Soaked is a much stronger word than damp.","hint":"Find the very strong word for damp.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["soaked","dry","mild","dusty"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-6-014","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The astronomer was meticulous. She checked every measurement of the star three times. What does 'meticulous' mean?","why":"The clue is that she checked every measurement three times. Meticulous means extremely careful about details.","hint":"Use the clue: checked three times.","tags":["space"],"opts":["very tired after long nights","excited about new finds","quick to finish her work","very careful about details"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-015","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses 'sound' to mean a stretch of sea water between pieces of land?","why":"A sound can be a stretch of sea between lands, like Puget Sound. The other sentences use other meanings.","hint":"Which sentence is about water and land?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A loud sound woke the crew.","The boat crossed the sound at dawn.","The old pier is safe and sound.","Sound the alarm if you see fins."],"a":1},{"id":"wdsB-6-016","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'disaster' comes from old words that mean 'bad star.'","why":"Dis- means bad and astro means star. Long ago, people blamed bad events on the stars.","hint":"Think of astro in astronaut.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wdsB-6-017","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Each of the players ____ a glove on the bench. Which verb fits?","why":"Each is singular, even with 'of the players' after it. So use has.","hint":"Each means one at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["have","having","haved","has"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-018","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Seaweed was abundant on the beach after the storm. Which word is the best opposite of abundant?","why":"Abundant means more than enough. Scarce means hard to find. Plentiful and common mean the same as abundant.","hint":"Which word means hard to find?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["plentiful","common","enormous","scarce"],"a":3},{"id":"wdsB-6-019","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The tenacious pitcher kept trying even after walking five batters. What does 'tenacious' mean?","why":"Tenacious means holding on and refusing to quit. The clue is that he kept trying after hard moments.","hint":"Use the clue: kept trying.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["determined, not giving up","easily upset by bad calls","much taller than the batters","careless with every pitch"],"a":0},{"id":"wdsB-6-020","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"'A rising tide lifts all boats.' What does this saying mean?","why":"The saying is figurative. When the tide rises, every boat rises. So when things get better, everyone gains.","hint":"Is it about real boats or a bigger idea?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Boats float better at night","Large boats always win races","When things improve, everyone benefits","Tides are highest in summer"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-1-001","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks swim in a huge body of salty water. What is it called?","why":"The ocean is enormous and salty. Puddles and ponds are small, and sharks could not live in them.","hint":"It covers most of our planet.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["The ocean","A puddle","A pond"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-002","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A map is a drawing that shows where places are.","why":"Maps show where things are, like towns, roads, mountains and oceans.","hint":"Think of what you use to find your way.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-003","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which vehicle can carry people across the ocean?","why":"Ships float on water and are big enough to carry many people far across the sea.","hint":"Think about what floats.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A bicycle","A ship","A skateboard"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-004","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A lifeguard sits by the beach. What is a lifeguard's job?","why":"Lifeguards watch the water and help swimmers who are in trouble.","hint":"The word says it: guarding lives.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Keep swimmers safe","Fix beach umbrellas","Sell ice cream"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-005","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A baseball ticket costs 4 dollars. How many dollars do 2 tickets cost?","why":"Two tickets means 4 plus 4, which makes 8 dollars.","hint":"Add the price two times.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":8},{"id":"worB-1-006","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Tacos are a famous food from which country?","why":"Tacos come from Mexico, where cooks fill warm tortillas with tasty things.","hint":"It is just south of the United States.","tags":["food"],"opts":["Canada","Mexico","Japan"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-007","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Great Wall is a very long stone wall built on hills and mountains. Which country is it in?","why":"The Great Wall of China winds across the northern part of the country for thousands of miles.","hint":"The wall's full name includes the answer.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["China","Egypt","Brazil"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-008","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order for riding a bus to school.","why":"You must wait for the bus before you can get on, ride it, and get off.","hint":"What must happen before you can ride?","tags":[],"opts":["Wait at the bus stop","Get on the bus","Ride to school","Get off at school"]},{"id":"worB-1-009","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which vehicle can travel underwater?","why":"Submarines are built to dive and stay underwater. Helicopters fly and buses drive on roads.","hint":"The word starts with a part meaning under.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A helicopter","A submarine","A school bus"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-010","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Dinosaur bones that slowly turned into stone are called what?","why":"Fossils form when buried bones turn to rock over millions of years. Some are 66 million years old.","hint":"They come from long, long ago.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Crystals","Seashells","Fossils"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-1-011","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"All the countries in the world share the same flag.","why":"A flag is a symbol for a country. Each country has its own flag with its own colors and design.","hint":"Think of flags at the Olympics.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-012","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What should drivers do at a red traffic light?","why":"Red means stop. Waiting at red lights keeps drivers and walkers safe.","hint":"Think of the colors of a traffic light.","tags":[],"opts":["Honk and go","Speed up","Stop"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-1-013","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which vehicle carries astronauts up into space?","why":"A rocket pushes very hard to climb above the air and carry astronauts into space.","hint":"It blasts off with fire and smoke.","tags":["space","machines"],"opts":["A sailboat","A rocket","A school bus"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-014","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these bodies of water in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Puddles are tiny, ponds are bigger, and oceans are so big that whales and sharks roam them.","hint":"Think about what could hold a whale.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A puddle","A pond","An ocean"]},{"id":"worB-1-015","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"number","q":"You save 2 dollars every week. How many dollars will you have after 3 weeks?","why":"Add 2 three times: 2 plus 2 plus 2 makes 6 dollars.","hint":"Count by twos.","tags":["money"],"a":6},{"id":"worB-1-016","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which invention lets you talk with a friend who is far away?","why":"A telephone carries voices over long distances. A compass shows direction and a ruler measures.","hint":"You hold it near your ear.","tags":[],"opts":["A telephone","A ruler","A compass"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-017","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"\"Hola\" is a way to say hello in which language?","why":"Hola is Spanish for hello. Spanish is spoken in Mexico, Spain and many other countries.","hint":"Many people in Mexico say it.","tags":[],"opts":["Spanish","German","Chinese"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-1-018","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A globe is a flat piece of paper.","why":"A globe is a ball-shaped model of Earth. A flat piece of paper is a map.","hint":"Think of a ball you could spin.","tags":["maps","space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-1-019","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"You drop coins into a jar and do not spend them. What are you doing?","why":"Saving means keeping money to use later, like for a big toy or a trip.","hint":"You are keeping it for later.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Borrowing","Selling","Saving"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-1-020","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Every day the Sun comes up in the...","why":"The Sun rises in the east and sets in the west. It looks like it moves, but Earth is spinning.","hint":"Sunset happens in the west.","tags":["space"],"opts":["South","West","East"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-2-001","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The Amazon is one of the mightiest rivers on Earth. It flows through which continent?","why":"The Amazon River winds through the rainforests of South America and empties into the Atlantic Ocean.","hint":"Think of the rainforest next to the Andes.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Asia","South America","Africa"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-2-002","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A ship sails from Japan to California. Which ocean does it cross?","why":"Japan is in Asia and California is in North America, and the Pacific Ocean lies between them. It is Earth's widest ocean.","hint":"Both places touch the same huge ocean.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["The Indian Ocean","The Atlantic Ocean","The Pacific Ocean"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-2-003","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Some sharks live in the freezing cold Arctic Ocean.","why":"Greenland sharks swim in icy Arctic water. Sharks are not only found in warm tropical seas.","hint":"Think about whether sharks can handle cold.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-2-004","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What do we call money a worker is paid for doing a job?","why":"Pay is money earned from working. A price tag shows what something costs, and a loan is borrowed money.","hint":"Workers get this for their work.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Pay","A price tag","A loan"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-2-005","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these ways to travel in order from slowest to fastest.","why":"Walking is the slowest. Bikes go faster than walkers, cars faster than bikes, and jets fastest of all.","hint":"Think about how far each goes in an hour.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Walking","Riding a bicycle","Driving a car","Flying in a jet"]},{"id":"worB-2-006","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Mount Fuji, a famous snowy volcano, is in which country?","why":"Mount Fuji rises near the coast of Japan, a country made of many islands in the Pacific Ocean.","hint":"The country is a chain of islands in Asia.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Norway","Japan","Peru"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-2-007","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"People in Brazil mostly speak which language?","why":"Brazil is the biggest country in South America, and its people speak Portuguese. Most neighbors speak Spanish.","hint":"It is not the language of most neighbors.","tags":[],"opts":["French","Spanish","Portuguese"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-2-008","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Baseball is played only in the United States.","why":"Baseball is loved in Japan, the Dominican Republic, Cuba and many other countries, and fans cheer for it all over the world.","hint":"Think of baseball stars from other countries.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-2-009","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A person who draws maps for a living is called a...","why":"Cartographers study the land and draw maps. Librarians care for books and plumbers fix pipes.","hint":"Think of someone who studies land and draws charts.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Cartographer","Plumber","Librarian"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-2-010","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A tall tower on the coast flashes a bright light at night to warn ships. What is it called?","why":"Lighthouses show sailors where rocks and shores are, so ships stay safe in the dark.","hint":"It stands on the shore and shines at night.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A lighthouse","A castle","A windmill"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-2-011","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A pack of 6 baseball cards costs 3 dollars. How many dollars do 4 packs cost?","why":"Four packs of 3 dollars each is 3 plus 3 plus 3 plus 3, which is 12 dollars.","hint":"Add 3 four times.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":12},{"id":"worB-2-012","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Pasta and pizza are famous foods from which country?","why":"Italy gave the world pizza and pasta, and cooks there still make them with tomatoes and cheese.","hint":"Its shape on a map looks like a boot.","tags":["food"],"opts":["Peru","Italy","Egypt"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-2-013","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Dinosaur fossils have been found in Antarctica.","why":"Antarctica was once warm and green. Scientists have dug up dinosaur bones there from long ago.","hint":"Think about Antarctica long, long ago.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-2-014","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What does a bridge help travelers do?","why":"Bridges hold up roads and train tracks over rivers and deep gaps so people can cross.","hint":"Think about what is below a bridge.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Cross over a river or valley","Dig tunnels under the ground","Float safely on big waves"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-2-015","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What carved the Grand Canyon over millions of years?","why":"The Colorado River slowly wore away rock, making the canyon deeper and deeper over millions of years.","hint":"Think about what water slowly does to rock.","tags":[],"opts":["Giant machines","A flowing river","A falling meteor"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-2-016","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A shark swims 3 kilometers every hour. How many kilometers does it swim in 4 hours?","why":"Four hours of 3 kilometers each is 3 plus 3 plus 3 plus 3, which equals 12 kilometers.","hint":"Add 3 four times.","tags":["sharks"],"a":12},{"id":"worB-2-017","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"In 1969, astronauts landed on a place beyond Earth and walked there. Where did they land?","why":"The Moon is Earth's nearest neighbor in space, so it was the first place astronauts landed. The Sun is far too hot.","hint":"It is the closest place to Earth in space.","tags":["space"],"opts":["The Sun","Jupiter","The Moon"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-2-018","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these helped people carry heavy loads more easily?","why":"Wheels roll instead of drag, so carts and wagons move heavy things with much less pushing.","hint":"It rolls.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["A kite","A paper clip","The wheel"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-2-019","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Australia is both a country and a continent.","why":"Australia is one country that covers a whole continent by itself. Most continents have many countries.","hint":"It is not shared with other countries.","tags":["maps","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-2-020","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"If you borrow a library book, what should you promise to do?","why":"Libraries share books with everyone. Returning yours lets the next reader enjoy it too.","hint":"Libraries lend books, not give them away.","tags":[],"opts":["Return it on time","Keep it forever","Draw in the pages"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-3-001","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"On a map, what does the legend (also called the key) tell you?","why":"The legend explains symbols, like a tent for a campground. The compass rose shows direction and the scale shows distance.","hint":"Look for a small box that explains the pictures.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Which way is north on the map","Who drew the map and when","How far apart two places are","What the map's symbols mean"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-3-002","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which ocean lies between Africa and Australia?","why":"The Indian Ocean touches Africa, Asia and Australia. The Atlantic is on Africa's other side and the Pacific is east of Australia.","hint":"Its name sounds like a country in Asia.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["The Atlantic Ocean","The Arctic Ocean","The Pacific Ocean","The Indian Ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-3-003","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Great white sharks are found off the coasts of California, South Africa and Australia.","why":"Great whites live in cool and warm coastal waters around the world, including all three of these places.","hint":"Great whites swim in many oceans.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-3-004","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a service, not a good?","why":"A service is work someone does for you. Goods are things you can hold, like bats, sandwiches and bicycles.","hint":"You cannot carry it home in a bag.","tags":["money"],"opts":["A baseball bat","A haircut","A sandwich","A bicycle"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-3-005","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these inventions in order from oldest to newest.","why":"Wheels are thousands of years old. The printing press came in the 1400s, the airplane in 1903 and personal computers later.","hint":"Which one is older than writing on paper?","tags":["machines"],"opts":["The wheel","The printing press","The airplane","The personal computer"]},{"id":"worB-3-006","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"People say \"arigato\" to mean thank you in which language?","why":"Arigato means thank you in Japanese. Korean, Hindi and Italian each have their own words for thanks.","hint":"It is spoken on islands in the Pacific.","tags":[],"opts":["Hindi","Italian","Japanese","Korean"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-3-007","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Mount Everest, Earth's highest mountain above sea level, belongs to which mountain range?","why":"Everest is part of the Himalayas, a giant chain of mountains in Asia. The Rocky Mountains and Alps are in other places.","hint":"It is in Asia.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The Rocky Mountains","The Himalayas","The Andes","The Alps"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-3-008","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A tag on a shark sends a signal every 2 hours. How many signals does it send in one whole day?","why":"A day has 24 hours. Splitting 24 hours into groups of 2 hours gives 12 signals.","hint":"How many 2s fit into 24?","tags":["sharks"],"a":12},{"id":"worB-3-009","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What is a port?","why":"Ports are busy places where cargo is lifted on and off big ships, and where passengers can board.","hint":"Think about where ships stop.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A small boat used for fishing trips","A flag that flies on a ship's mast","A rope tying a boat to a dock","A harbor where ships load cargo"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-3-010","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Every country in the world uses the same kind of money.","why":"Countries have their own money, like dollars, pesos or yen. A few groups of countries share one.","hint":"Think about money from different countries.","tags":["money"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-3-011","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Who looks after a national park and helps keep visitors and animals safe?","why":"Park rangers protect wild places, answer visitors' questions and help people who get lost.","hint":"Think of who protects wild places.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A park ranger","A chef","A mail carrier","An air traffic controller"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-3-012","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Why are dinosaur fossils often found in dry, rocky badlands?","why":"Dinosaurs lived in many places. In badlands, wind and rain wear away rock and show bones that were buried.","hint":"Think about what wears rocks away.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Wind and rain uncover buried bones","Dinosaurs only lived in dry places","Dry air turns bones into stone faster","Sand dunes carried the bones there"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-3-013","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A train travels 60 miles each hour. How many miles does it go in 3 hours?","why":"Add 60 three times: 60 plus 60 plus 60 equals 180 miles.","hint":"Add 60 three times.","tags":["machines"],"a":180},{"id":"worB-3-014","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"It is daytime in North America but nighttime in Asia. Why?","why":"Earth is a spinning ball, so the Sun lights only half of it. As Earth turns, day and night move around.","hint":"Think about what Earth does every 24 hours.","tags":["space"],"opts":["The Moon blocks sunlight over Asia each day","Earth spins, lighting one side at a time","The Sun travels all the way around Earth","Asia is much farther away from the Sun"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-3-015","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Dominican Republic, home to many baseball stars, sits in which sea?","why":"The Dominican Republic shares an island with Haiti in the warm Caribbean Sea, southeast of Florida.","hint":"Think of warm islands near Florida.","tags":["baseball","ocean","maps"],"opts":["The Caribbean Sea","The Mediterranean Sea","The Baltic Sea","The Red Sea"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-3-016","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these places in order from highest to lowest.","why":"Mountains tower above hills, beaches sit at sea level, and the ocean floor lies far below the waves.","hint":"Start with the place nearest the clouds.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["A mountain peak","A hilltop","A beach at sea level","The ocean floor"]},{"id":"worB-3-017","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Ancient Egyptians wrote using little pictures and symbols called...","why":"Hieroglyphs were carved on temples and painted on papyrus. Cuneiform used wedge marks on clay in Mesopotamia.","hint":"Think of picture writing carved on temple walls.","tags":[],"opts":["Morse code","Braille","Hieroglyphs","Cuneiform"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-3-018","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Around 1610, Galileo used a new invention to see moons circling Jupiter. What was it?","why":"Telescopes make faraway things look closer. A microscope magnifies tiny things.","hint":"It brings faraway things close.","tags":["space"],"opts":["A camera","A microscope","A compass","A telescope"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-3-019","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Some countries, like Switzerland, have more than one official language.","why":"Switzerland officially uses German, French, Italian and Romansh. Many countries have more than one language.","hint":"Think of a country with many neighbors.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-3-020","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A farmer grows wheat for bread. The family that buys the bread is called a...","why":"Producers make goods. Consumers buy and use them. The farmer is a producer and the family is a consumer.","hint":"The family eats it.","tags":["food","money"],"opts":["Inventor","Producer","Banker","Consumer"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-001","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Southwest is halfway between which two directions?","why":"Southwest blends south and west. North and west make northwest, and south and east make southeast.","hint":"Say the name in two parts.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["North and east","North and west","South and west","South and east"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-4-002","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A store lowers its coat prices in spring. Why?","why":"When fewer people want something, sellers often cut the price to sell what is left. Warm weather lowers demand for coats.","hint":"Think about who wants a coat on a warm day.","tags":["money","weather"],"opts":["Coats cost more to make in spring","More shoppers want coats in spring","Stores can only sell coats in winter","Fewer people want coats then"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-003","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Atlantic Ocean touches North America, South America, Europe and Africa.","why":"The Atlantic stretches between the Americas on one side and Europe and Africa on the other.","hint":"Picture the Atlantic in the middle of a world map.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-4-004","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which city is the capital of Australia?","why":"Canberra was planned as the capital. Sydney and Melbourne are bigger and better known, so many people guess them.","hint":"It is not the city with the famous opera house.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Canberra","Melbourne","Sydney","Perth"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-4-005","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A phone shows your spot on a map using GPS. GPS works by listening to signals from...","why":"GPS satellites orbit high above Earth and send signals. A phone uses them to work out exactly where it is.","hint":"They travel far above the sky.","tags":["space","maps"],"opts":["Cables buried deep under the ground","Satellites circling Earth","Giant towers built on the Moon","Lighthouses along every coast"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-4-006","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A ship sails 20 miles each hour. How many hours does it need to sail 100 miles?","why":"Divide 100 miles by 20 miles per hour. Five hours of sailing covers 100 miles.","hint":"How many 20s make 100?","tags":["ocean"],"a":5},{"id":"worB-4-007","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The English word \"safari\" comes from Swahili, a language of East Africa. What does \"safari\" mean there?","why":"Safari means journey in Swahili. The word came into English from people who made long trips across Africa.","hint":"Many people go on one to see wild animals.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["A wild animal","A sunset","A journey","A hunter"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-4-008","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which scientist studies weather and warns towns when a hurricane is coming?","why":"Meteorologists study air and weather. Oceanographers study the sea, geologists study rocks and cartographers make maps.","hint":"Think about the weather forecast.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["A geologist","A cartographer","A meteorologist","An oceanographer"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-4-009","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order from earliest to most recent.","why":"Dinosaurs came first by far. Cave art is tens of thousands of years old, pyramids are thousands, and the Moon landing was in 1969.","hint":"Which happened before any people existed?","tags":["dinosaurs","space"],"opts":["Dinosaurs roam Earth","People paint on cave walls","Egyptians build the pyramids","Astronauts land on the Moon"]},{"id":"worB-4-010","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Passing through the Strait of Gibraltar takes ships between the Atlantic Ocean and which sea?","why":"Gibraltar is a narrow gap between Spain and Morocco. It joins the Atlantic Ocean and the Mediterranean Sea.","hint":"The sea lies between Europe and Africa.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["The Caribbean Sea","The Red Sea","The Baltic Sea","The Mediterranean Sea"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-011","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Chocolate is made from the beans of the cacao tree. Where did people first grow cacao?","why":"Cacao trees need heat and rain. People in Central and South America used cacao long before Europe ever tasted chocolate.","hint":"Cacao trees need heat and rain.","tags":["food"],"opts":["Warm rainforests of the Americas","High, snowy mountain peaks of Asia","Dry, sandy deserts of North Africa","Cold, windy plains of northern Europe"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-4-012","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Big cities almost never grow up beside rivers or harbors.","why":"Boats carried food and goods cheaply, so people settled by rivers and harbors. Many large cities grew from those spots.","hint":"How did goods travel long ago?","tags":["ocean","money"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-4-013","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A scientist attaches a small tag to a shark. What can the tag help show?","why":"Tags record a shark's path and how deep it dives. They cannot count teeth or show what it ate.","hint":"Think about maps and travel.","tags":["sharks","maps"],"opts":["How many teeth it will grow","How loud its heartbeat is","Which fish it ate yesterday","Where the shark travels"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-014","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Gobi Desert is famous for dinosaur nests with eggs inside. On which continent is the Gobi?","why":"The Gobi stretches across Mongolia and northern China. Fossil hunters there have found dinosaur bones and nests.","hint":"Think of Mongolia and China.","tags":["dinosaurs","maps"],"opts":["Australia","Asia","Africa","South America"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-4-015","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A stand sells 40 hot dogs at a baseball game for 3 dollars each. How many dollars does it earn?","why":"40 times 3 equals 120. Money earned from selling is called income, and profit is what is left after costs.","hint":"Think 4 tens times 3.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":120},{"id":"worB-4-016","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Goods that a country sells to other countries are called...","why":"Exports leave a country to be sold elsewhere. Imports are goods that come in from other countries.","hint":"Think of 'ex' as in 'exit'.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Savings","Taxes","Imports","Exports"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-017","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Houses in very hot, sunny places are often painted white. Why?","why":"Light colors bounce sunlight away instead of soaking it up, so white walls stay cooler than dark ones.","hint":"Think about wearing a white shirt on a sunny day.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["White paint stops strong winds from blowing in","White is the cheapest paint color to buy","White paint keeps heavy rain from soaking in","White reflects sunlight and keeps rooms cool"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-4-018","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these ways of sharing messages in order from oldest to newest.","why":"Cave drawings are the oldest. Printed books came in the 1400s, telephones in the 1870s and email in the 1970s.","hint":"Which ones need electricity?","tags":[],"opts":["Cave drawings","Printed books","Telephone calls","Email"]},{"id":"worB-4-019","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"What did the printing press make possible?","why":"Before printing presses, people copied books by hand. Printing made books cheaper, so more people could read.","hint":"Think about how books were made before it.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Sending voices through wires","Copying books quickly","Crossing oceans faster","Seeing far-off stars clearly"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-4-020","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Panama Canal lets ships travel between the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans without sailing around which continent?","why":"The canal cuts across Panama, a narrow strip of land. Ships save thousands of miles by not rounding South America.","hint":"Think about the shape of the Americas.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["South America","Australia","Asia","Africa"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-5-001","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why do countries trade with each other?","why":"Climate, land and skills differ, so countries swap what they have plenty of for goods they cannot easily make.","hint":"Think about bananas in a cold country.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Trade helps only the country that sells","Every country makes everything it needs","Money cannot be used inside one country","Each has goods the other lacks"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-5-002","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Greenland looks as big as Africa on many flat world maps, but Africa is far larger. Why?","why":"Flattening a round Earth stretches places near the poles. Africa is really about 14 times bigger than Greenland.","hint":"Think about flattening an orange peel.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Greenland has grown since maps were drawn","Mapmakers draw bigger countries smaller","Greenland's ice makes the land taller","Flat maps stretch shapes near the poles"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-5-003","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If you cross the International Date Line going west, the calendar jumps back one day.","why":"Sailing west across the Date Line, in the Pacific, jumps the calendar forward a day. Going east takes a day away.","hint":"Which way does a new day start?","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-5-004","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"What do we call a narrow strip of land that joins two bigger lands, with water on both sides?","why":"Panama is an isthmus. A peninsula has water on three sides, and an archipelago is a chain of islands.","hint":"Panama is the famous example.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["An isthmus","An archipelago","A peninsula","A delta"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-5-005","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Besides goods, what else traveled along the Silk Road, a web of trade routes linking China to the Mediterranean?","why":"Papermaking and other ideas spread along the Silk Road, carried by travelers along with silk and spices.","hint":"Travelers also shared what they knew.","tags":[],"opts":["Giant steel ocean ships","Hot-air balloon passengers","Telephone lines and cables","Ideas and inventions"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-5-006","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these ocean zones in order from the surface down to the deepest.","why":"Sunlight fades with depth, from the bright top layer to the dim twilight zone, the dark midnight zone and the trenches.","hint":"Sunlight can only reach the top.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Sunlight zone","Twilight zone","Midnight zone","Deep ocean trench"]},{"id":"worB-5-007","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Rosetta Stone helped scholars finally read Egyptian hieroglyphs. Why?","why":"One script was Greek, which scholars could read. They matched it with the hieroglyphs to crack the code.","hint":"One of the scripts was already known.","tags":[],"opts":["A pharaoh had already translated it long ago","The same message was carved in three scripts","It was a full dictionary carved in stone","It showed a picture for every hieroglyph"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-5-008","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball game starts at 9:00 AM Thursday in Tokyo. Los Angeles is about 16 to 17 hours behind Tokyo. What day is it in Los Angeles?","why":"Going back 16 or 17 hours from 9:00 AM Thursday crosses midnight, landing in the afternoon of the day before.","hint":"Going back that many hours crosses midnight.","tags":["baseball","maps"],"opts":["Wednesday","Tuesday","Friday","Thursday"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-5-009","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Mediterranean Sea touches Europe, Africa and Asia.","why":"Southern Europe, northern Africa and western Asia all have shores on the Mediterranean.","hint":"Think of Spain, Egypt and Turkey.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-5-010","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these countries has no ocean coast at all?","why":"Switzerland is landlocked, surrounded by other countries. Portugal, Japan and Chile all have long ocean coasts.","hint":"Think of the Alps in the middle of Europe.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Portugal","Switzerland","Chile","Japan"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-5-011","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The same kinds of dinosaur fossils turn up in South America and Africa. What best explains this?","why":"Long ago the continents formed one big landmass, so dinosaurs could walk between lands that are now far apart.","hint":"Look at how the coastlines fit together.","tags":["dinosaurs","maps"],"opts":["Both lands have the same climate today","The continents were once joined","Fossil hunters carried bones between continents","Dinosaurs could swim across the Atlantic"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-5-012","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"number","q":"Earth is about 40,000 kilometers around the Equator. A jet flies 800 kilometers each hour. How many hours would it take to fly all the way around?","why":"Divide 40,000 by 800 to get 50 hours, which is more than two full days of flying.","hint":"Divide the distance by the speed.","tags":["maps","machines"],"a":50},{"id":"worB-5-013","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is usually paid for with taxes?","why":"Taxes pay for shared services like libraries, roads and fire stations. Shops and restaurants get their money from customers.","hint":"Think of services everyone in town shares.","tags":["money"],"opts":["A movie ticket","A soccer ball in a store","A public library","A restaurant meal"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-5-014","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Most goods that cross the ocean travel in standard metal containers. Why are standard sizes useful?","why":"Identical boxes stack neatly and move from ship to train to truck without unpacking, saving time and money.","hint":"Think about stacking identical blocks.","tags":["ocean","machines","money"],"opts":["They fit ships, trains and trucks alike","They keep goods cold on the voyage","They make the cargo weigh less","They help ships travel faster than planes"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-5-015","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order to show how a fish gets from the sea to a dinner table.","why":"Goods travel from producer to consumer: the fish is caught, landed at port, trucked to a store, then bought and cooked.","hint":"Follow the fish from sea to plate.","tags":["ocean","food"],"opts":["The crew catches the fish","The boat unloads at the port","A truck carries it to a store","A family cooks it at home"]},{"id":"worB-5-016","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why do pilots listen to air traffic controllers on the ground?","why":"Controllers watch the sky and tell pilots when to take off, land or change course, so planes never get too close.","hint":"Think of a busy sky with many planes.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["To learn how many passengers are aboard","To get the plane's gas at the airport","To hear which snacks are on board","To keep planes safely apart"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-5-017","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Many rocket launch sites are close to the ocean. What is one good reason?","why":"Rockets drop used-up parts as they climb. Launching over the sea keeps those pieces away from people.","hint":"Think about where leftover parts fall.","tags":["space","ocean"],"opts":["Ocean waves help push the rocket upward","Cool sea air makes every rocket lighter","Falling pieces land in water, not on towns","Rockets use salty ocean water as their fuel"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-5-018","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"An island can belong to only one country, never to two.","why":"Borneo holds three countries and Ireland holds two. An island's borders can cross it just like a continent's.","hint":"Think about land borders on islands.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-5-019","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A tagged shark swims through the waters of many countries. What does this show about protecting sharks?","why":"Sharks ignore borders. A shark safe in one country's waters can be in danger in the next, so countries must cooperate.","hint":"Sharks do not stop at borders.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Sharks are safe once they leave land","Rules matter only close to the shore","One country's rules can protect it everywhere","Countries must work together to protect them"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-5-020","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Farmers in the Andes and in Asia cut flat steps into mountainsides. Why?","why":"These steps are called terraces. They keep soil and water from washing downhill so crops can grow on steep slopes.","hint":"Think about rain running down a slope.","tags":["food"],"opts":["To block cold wind from villages","To keep animals from climbing up","Flat steps hold soil and water","To make the mountain look taller"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-6-001","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Britain is far to the north, yet its winters are fairly mild. What helps most?","why":"The North Atlantic Drift carries warm water from the tropics toward Europe, softening winters.","hint":"Think about the water touching its coast.","tags":["ocean","weather"],"opts":["Volcanoes heat the island all winter","It sits close to the Equator","A warm ocean current flows nearby","Hot deserts lie right next to it"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-6-002","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Over many years most prices slowly rise, so a dollar buys less than it used to. What is this called?","why":"Inflation is a general rise in prices. Interest is money paid for borrowing or saving.","hint":"Think of a balloon filling with air.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Scarcity","Interest","Exports","Inflation"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-6-003","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The deepest place in the ocean is deeper than Mount Everest is tall.","why":"The Mariana Trench is nearly 11 kilometers deep, and Everest is about 9 kilometers tall. Everest would sink out of sight.","hint":"Compare how far down with how far up.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-6-004","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A ship sits where the Equator meets the Prime Meridian, at 0 degrees latitude and 0 degrees longitude. Which ocean is it in?","why":"That spot is in the Gulf of Guinea, off West Africa. A weather buoy floats there.","hint":"Picture where the two lines cross on a world map.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["The Arctic Ocean","The Pacific Ocean","The Indian Ocean","The Atlantic Ocean"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-6-005","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Fossils of sea creatures have been found high in the Himalayas. What does this show?","why":"Colliding plates pushed old seafloor up into mountains. The ocean never rose that high.","hint":"What could lift rock for millions of years?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["The ocean once rose higher than Everest","Birds carried the shells there","Seabed was pushed up into mountains","Sea animals once climbed mountains"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-6-006","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A bank pays 10 percent interest each year on your whole balance. You deposit 100 dollars. After 2 years, with the interest added each year, how many dollars do you have?","why":"Year one adds 10 dollars, making 110. Year two adds 10 percent of 110, which is 11, making 121.","hint":"Find the total after year one first.","tags":["money"],"a":121},{"id":"worB-6-007","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Spanish, French, Italian and Portuguese all grew out of which ancient language?","why":"These languages grew from the Latin spoken across the Roman Empire, which is why many of their words look alike.","hint":"The ancient Romans spoke it.","tags":[],"opts":["Latin","Old English","Greek","Arabic"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-6-008","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these countries is crossed by the Equator, so it lies in both hemispheres?","why":"The Equator crosses northern Brazil. Peru lies south of it, and Mexico and Argentina are far from it.","hint":"Think of the country around the Amazon's mouth.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Argentina","Peru","Mexico","Brazil"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-6-009","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these loan steps in the correct order.","why":"A loan works in this order: ask, receive, spend, then repay with a little extra called interest.","hint":"You cannot spend money before you have it.","tags":["money"],"opts":["You ask a bank for a loan","The bank lends you the money","You use it to buy what you need","You pay the bank back with interest"]},{"id":"worB-6-010","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A Roman aqueduct slopes gently downward all the way to the city. Why?","why":"Romans had no pumps. A steady downhill slope let gravity move the water over many miles.","hint":"Which way does water like to flow?","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Gravity pulls the water along","Pumps pushed the water along","Wind turned wheels that moved it","Animals pulled the water in buckets"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-6-011","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"On a flat map, flights from Los Angeles to Tokyo curve northward. Why do pilots fly this way?","why":"The shortest path between far-apart places on a ball bends when shown on a flat map. It's called a great circle.","hint":"Think of a string stretched across a globe.","tags":["maps","space"],"opts":["On a round Earth, that path is shortest","Planes must always stay within sight of land","Mapmakers drew these routes the wrong way","Strong ocean currents steer the planes north"],"a":0},{"id":"worB-6-012","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The Arctic is a continent surrounded by ocean, while Antarctica is an ocean surrounded by land.","why":"It is the other way around. The Arctic Ocean has land around it, and Antarctica is a continent with ocean around it.","hint":"Compare the two ends of Earth.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-6-013","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which shark can swim far up rivers into fresh water?","why":"Bull sharks can live in fresh water and have been found far up rivers. Most other sharks stay in salty seas.","hint":"It can handle both fresh water and salty seas.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["The whale shark","The hammerhead shark","The great white shark","The bull shark"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"worB-6-014","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The Dead Sea is far saltier than the ocean. Why does salt build up there?","why":"Rivers carry in a little salt, but water can only leave by evaporating. Over time the salt piles up.","hint":"What is left when water dries up?","tags":[],"opts":["Salt-making fish live in its waters","Underwater volcanoes keep adding more salt","Strong winds blow salt in from the ocean","Water evaporates but the salt stays behind"],"a":3},{"id":"worB-6-015","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Pretend 1 dollar can be traded for 10 pesos. A baseball ticket costs 50 pesos. How many dollars is that?","why":"Each 10 pesos equals 1 dollar, and 50 pesos holds five groups of 10. So the ticket costs 5 dollars.","hint":"How many 10s fit in 50?","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":5},{"id":"worB-6-016","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Some of the earliest writing, pressed into clay, was used mostly for what?","why":"In Mesopotamia, scribes pressed wedge marks into clay to record grain, animals and trades. Stories and poems came later.","hint":"Think about what early traders needed to remember.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Writing down songs and poems","Making maps of the night stars","Counting and recording goods","Teaching young kids how to read"],"a":2},{"id":"worB-6-017","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Chile is very long and narrow from north to south. Why does it have deserts in the north and snowy lands in the south?","why":"Chile runs through many latitudes, from warm northern deserts to cold southern lands near Antarctica.","hint":"Think about how far apart its ends are.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The south is much closer to the Sun","It stretches across many latitudes","The whole country sits on the Equator","Sea winds blow in the snow and sand"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-6-018","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the steps of a shark-tagging study in order.","why":"The shark must be caught before tagging, then released. Later, the tag's signals let scientists map where it swims.","hint":"What must happen before attaching anything?","tags":["sharks","maps"],"opts":["Scientists catch the shark","They attach a tag","They release the shark","They map its path from signals"]},{"id":"worB-6-019","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Many rockets launch toward the east from sites near the Equator. How does that help?","why":"Earth spins toward the east, fastest at the Equator, so an eastward launch starts with a free boost.","hint":"Think about how fast the ground itself moves.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Warm air makes rockets weigh less","Earth's spin gives them a speed boost","Equator skies never have clouds or storms","The Moon always sits in the east"],"a":1},{"id":"worB-6-020","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"When two countries trade, one of them must always lose.","why":"Both sides trade because each expects to get something it values more than what it gives up.","hint":"Would anyone trade if they always lost?","tags":["money"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1}];
